{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carolina","familyName":"Caetano","name":"Caetano, Carolina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brantly","familyName":"Callaway","name":"Callaway, Brantly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stroud","familyName":"Payne","name":"Payne, Stroud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Sant'Anna","name":"Sant'Anna, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Difference-in-differences with \"bad controls\""}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:17:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:19:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper considers difference-in-differences identification strategies when the parallel trends assumption holds after conditioning on covariates that may themselves be affected by the treatment (often referred to as \"bad controls\"). We show that common approaches such as simply dropping bad controls are often ill-advised and develop two alternative approaches that allow bad controls to function as genuine controls despite being affected by treatment. First, we derive explicit conditions that rationalize conditioning only on pre-treatment values of the bad control, leading naturally to the Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021) estimator with pre-treatment values as covariates. Second, under a covariate unconfoundedness condition, we develop imputation and double/debiased machine learning estimators that recover the average treatment effect on the treated. We extend these results to staggered treatment adoption, provide pre-tests for the identifying assumptions, and apply the methods to study the effects of job displacement on earnings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:23:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:23:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:43:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03729","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03729","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03729","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan-Phong","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tuan-Phong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Razniewski","name":"Razniewski, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Direct Construction of Disambiguated Knowledge Bases from Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:25:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:58:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T14:18:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:50:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T12:24:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:54:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated Knowledge Base Construction (AKBC) is a core NLP task, and recent work proposes generating knowledge bases directly from large language models (LLMs), treating the model itself as the knowledge source. However, LLMs natively possess no representation of entities, leading to duplicate entries as well as conflations. We propose GPTKB 2.0, a methodology for constructing disambiguated KBs directly from LLMs. GPTKB 2.0 incorporates on-the-fly disambiguation of entities, relations and classes, and is meticulously designed to satisfy both scalability and disambiguation accuracy. We analyze the central design decisions and characterize the trade-offs between accuracy, scale, and cost. We execute GPTKB 2.0 at scale, obtaining a materialized KB containing over 1M disambiguated entities and 38.4M triples. This represents the first million-scale LLM-native KB with explicit internal canonicalization of entities, relations, and classes, a significant departure from prior Wikimedia-centric works. GPTKB 2.0 is available at https://gptkb.org/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03729","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:37Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:43:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johann","familyName":"Cigler","name":"Cigler, Johann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Some sequences and number triangles which are related to Narayana polynomials and to q-Narayana polynomials for q=-1"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:40:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T07:22:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:35:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As is well known the Catalan numbers can be interpreted as numbers of Dyck paths. The Narayana polynomials and the q-Narayana polynomials for q=-1 can analogously be interpreted as certain weights of these paths. In the present note we try to get some information about the corresponding weights of bounded Dyck paths. This also leads to some number sequences and analogues of Pascals triangle which previously have occurred in other contexts. We consider some examples and obtain some results and conjectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Slightly expanded version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:43:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksandr","familyName":"Sharipov","name":"Sharipov, Aleksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusif","familyName":"Mukhtarov","name":"Mukhtarov, Yusif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Molybog","name":"Molybog, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling an Autoregressive Transformer for Single-Cell Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T23:54:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:14:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T18:10:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study a self-supervised generation task for single-cell gene expression vectors: given a set of vectors from a cell type, we aim to generate additional gene expression vectors of that cell type. For this task we characterize both the biological fidelity of the generated gene expression vectors and the scaling behavior of the pretraining loss. The model is a causal transformer paired with a learned quantized VAE tokenizer, trained with a cross-entropy loss. To evaluate the model, we condition it on held-out gene expression vectors of a cell type and generate vectors of gene expression, comparing the resulting distribution over gene expression vectors to the ground truth distribution of that cell type. We study the scaling properties of the proposed architecture by varying the number of trained parameters and the amount of training data. To our knowledge, we find the first jointly-fit two-exponent scaling law and compute-optimal frontier for a single-cell foundation model. Finally, we discuss how this pretrained model could be finetuned for perturbation response prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:01:56Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:01:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:43:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.04001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.04001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.04001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Hariri","name":"Hariri, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weicong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nahal","familyName":"Shahini","name":"Shahini, Nahal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikash","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Vikash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Samandar","name":"Samandar, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debargha","familyName":"Ganguly","name":"Ganguly, Debargha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sreehari","familyName":"Sankar","name":"Sankar, Sreehari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouren","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shouren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biyao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Biyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Hinczewski","name":"Hinczewski, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vipin","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Vipin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Scaling in Reasoning LLMs: Inference Regimes, Evaluation, and Reproducibility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:09:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T18:11:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:05:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models can solve harder reasoning problems with more inference-time compute. The term \"test-time scaling,\" however, covers several inference algorithms: extending deliberation along one trajectory, sampling completed candidates and aggregating them by voting or verification, and searching over partial states. These algorithms differ in statistical structure, compute requirements, and failure modes. Treating them as interchangeable under a scalar \"budget,\" or reporting accuracy without specifying the inference protocol, makes results difficult to compare across studies. We study test-time scaling along three axes. First, we formalize it as budgeted inference over the implicit prefix tree of an autoregressive model and distinguish single-trajectory sequential scaling, leaf-level scaling with terminal reduction, and prefix-level scaling. Second, we treat the full inference system as the evaluated object and separate end-to-end performance from candidate-bank diagnostics. We introduce an evaluation profile whose coordinates and simple functionals recover or bound common repeated-sampling metrics, and require compute accounting and uncertainty estimates that match the protocol. Third, we distinguish exact replay from distributional reproducibility and state the requirements for each. We also organize open-weight reasoning models by model-side and interface mechanisms. Our empirical study covers broad knowledge, symbolic reasoning, and competition mathematics, and we publicly release 1,403,520 sampled model attempts. The project website is available at https://mohsenhariri.github.io/scorio/tts. The released datasets are Trace (https://huggingface.co/datasets/harimo/scorio-trace), Lite (https://huggingface.co/datasets/harimo/scorio-lite), Math (https://huggingface.co/buckets/harimo/scorio-math), and SuperGPQA (https://huggingface.co/buckets/harimo/scorio-gpqa)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:25:53Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:25:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:10:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03792","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03792","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03792","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Molecules of an affine FPF $W$-graph and a labelled row-Beissinger reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:08:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T07:20:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:25:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T07:29:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:41:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Kazhdan--Lusztig $W$-graphs encode the cell structure of Hecke algebras, while their bidirected connected components are called molecules. In finite type~$A$, the Robinson--Schensted correspondence describes cells and molecules, and Beissinger's row insertion constructs the common tableau associated with an involution. In affine type~$A$, the affine matrix-ball construction (AMBC) assigns an affine permutation a pair of tabloids together with a dominant weight, and Marberg introduced affine fixed-point-free (FPF) $W$-graphs indexed by affine FPF involutions. We classify and enumerate the molecules of Marberg's $\\m$-type affine FPF $W$-graph $Γ_n^{\\m}$ and show that molecules with the same AMBC shape have isomorphic underlying simple bidirected graphs. We also prove that labelled complete-cycle row-Beissinger truncations recover the full AMBC datum of an affine FPF involution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03792","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:21:05Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:21:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:10:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Lubotzky","name":"Lubotzky, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jvbin","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jvbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Non Uniform Kazhdan Constant for Linear Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T12:28:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:50:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T07:26:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A group $Γ$ generated by a finite set $S$ has Property $(T)$ if the associated Kazhdan constant $κ(Γ,S)$ is positive. If so, the same holds for every finite generating set $S$. There are Property $(T)$ groups which are uniformly $(T)$, and others which are not.\n It is a well-known problem whether the classical examples of Property $(T)$ groups, $\\mathrm{SL}_n(\\mathbb{Z})$, $n\\geq 3$, are uniformly $(T)$ or not. We show that they are not. Moreover, the same holds for every infinite finitely generated linear group."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:15:47Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:15:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:10:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03342","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03342","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03342","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dang P. M.","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Dang P. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hieu D.","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Hieu D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hieu","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Hieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Oracle Conditioning Misleads Deployment: Conditioning-Availability Bias in Echocardiographic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T08:52:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:38:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T13:24:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conditional segmentation models may be trained and evaluated with auxiliary signals cleaner than those available at deployment. We study this protocol-level manifestation of shortcut learning and auxiliary-variable shift in phase-conditioned echocardiographic segmentation. The complementary gap pair measures loss on the deployable oracle-estimated pathway and probes sensitivity on the oracle-random pathway. On held-out CAMUS data, one strong-cyclic, oracle-selected run fails severely with estimated phase, while sensitivity to incorrect phase persists across three runs. On EchoNet-Dynamic, the current estimator remains usable, but random-phase testing reveals strong latent sensitivity. Deployment-aware checkpoint selection and phase perturbation reduce both gaps with little change in mean Dice. Exploratory subgroup analyses quantify variation across measured strata, and a downstream ejection fraction (EF) audit shows that recovering segmentation does not necessarily recover EF error or signed bias. Together, the gaps test whether oracle-conditioned performance survives the inference pathway actually available at deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in the MICCAI 2026 Workshop on Fairness of AI in Medical Imaging (FAIMI 2026). To appear in Springer Lecture Notes in Computer Science (LNCS)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03342","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:10:40Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:10:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:10:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02880","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02880","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02880","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paimon","familyName":"Goulart","name":"Goulart, Paimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelly","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Kelly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos E.","familyName":"Papalexakis","name":"Papalexakis, Evangelos E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangjie","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Liangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Field-Aware Agent Skill Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T21:01:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:10:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T17:01:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:59:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:42:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:27:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As lifelong learning agents accumulate lifelong growing skill banks, retrieving the correct skill becomes an increasingly important bottleneck. Most current skill retrieval methods treat each skill as one flat document by concatenating fields such as the name, description, and body. However, skills are naturally structured, multi-field objects, where each field provides different information about when and how the skill should be used. In this work, we study whether preserving this structure improves skill retrieval. We represent each skill as its separate components, and compute sparse and dense similarities for each field independently, exposing a naturally tensorized, field-aware representation of the skill bank. We then combine these field-level scores either with uniform weights or with a small learned MLP. Across two different skill retrieval benchmarks, SkillRet and SRA-Bench, we find that keeping fields separate improves hybrid retrieval, and learning over the field-level scores gives the strongest and most consistent results. Our field-aware MLP reaches $77.95$ Recall@10 on SkillRet and $83.78$ Recall@10 on SRA-Bench, outperforming the corresponding concatenated learned baselines. We also find that the advantage grows as the skill bank becomes larger, suggesting that field-aware skill retrieval becomes especially useful in the setting where retrieval is most difficult. Our results show that skill representation itself matters, and that simply preserving the structure already present in skill files can substantially improve retrieval."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02880","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:59:59Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:00:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:10:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Livieri","name":"Livieri, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianluca","familyName":"Palmari","name":"Palmari, Gianluca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proper-score observation-driven filters: local geometry, estimation, and continuous-time limits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Finance (q-fin.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62M10, 62F35, 60F17","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T19:40:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:06:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T08:48:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:47:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Observation-driven filters usually use the likelihood score, tying their updates to the logarithmic scoring rule. We study recursions driven instead by the negative parameter derivative of a differentiable proper scoring rule, under a declared working family and predictable scaling. This separates three roles: the rule determines the conditional risk projection and tail response; the scaling converts its derivative into the implemented update; and the autoregressive component determines the composite dynamic centre. We characterize local mean reversion and update noise through risk curvature and innovation variance, which coincide for the log score under the information identity but generally differ. For high-frequency scale models, centred updates converge to diffusions, whereas non-centred updates follow deterministic mean flows. When the rule-specific projection moves over time, the local tracking error admits an Ornstein--Uhlenbeck approximation, valid up to a stopping time and allowing update and target shocks to be correlated. For static parameters, we establish consistency and asymptotic normality under explicit stability and fixed-tuning conditions. Simulations and an international-equity application illustrate how criterion choice affects robustness, adaptation, variance-forecast loss, value-at-risk calibration, and probability-integral-transform diagnostics. The results show that predictive density and updating criterion are distinct design choices whose relative performance depends on the target and disturbance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"101 pages, 12 figures, 25 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:58:47Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:58:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:10:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03946","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03946","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03946","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilotpal","familyName":"Sanyal","name":"Sanyal, Nilotpal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resolution-Adaptive Compact-Support Priors for Bayesian Wavelet Denoising"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:11:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:07:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T06:46:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:40:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a resolution-adaptive Bayesian wavelet denoising method for noisy one-dimensional signals. Its central innovation is a spike-and-slab prior whose continuous slab mixes a compactly supported Wendland-type polynomial density with the more dispersed semicircle density. A low-dimensional empirical-Bayes trend produces data-adaptive mixture weights by resolution, while a data-adaptive support scale controls the common bounded interval. Thus, the method combines sparsity, explicit support control, and interpretable resolution-dependent shrinkage. Under squared-error loss, we derive the posterior-mean estimator and establish symmetry, boundedness, continuity, and limiting properties. We define fixed-hyperparameter bias, variance, and risk and develop an empirical-Bayes fitting procedure. Under a Laplace working likelihood, the Wendland contribution has finite-sum expressions, while the semicircle contribution is evaluated by stable one-dimensional integration. Simulations with the Bumps, Blocks, Doppler, and HeaviSine signals compare Gaussian- and Laplace-likelihood versions with universal thresholding, false-discovery-rate (FDR) thresholding, cross-validation (CV), Stein's unbiased risk estimate (SURE), the Bayesian adaptive multiresolution shrinker (BAMS), and a nonlocal-prior (NLP) method. In the primary Gaussian-error study, WS--Gaussian was the best non-NLP method in 24 of 36 cells, including 11 of 12 low-SNR cells, with a much more favorable computational profile than WS--Laplace. A real seismic acceleration record from the 2008 Chino Hills earthquake illustrates attenuation of rapid fluctuations and preservation of the dominant event. A semi-synthetic study using the processed trace as surrogate truth showed improvement over the noisy observation at lower and moderate SNRs, but not at the highest SNR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03946","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:24:37Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:24:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantin","familyName":"Iakoubovskii","name":"Iakoubovskii, Konstantin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Katrusha","name":"Katrusha, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optical centers in cubic boron nitride and diamond: remarkable similarities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:29:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.diamond.2026.114127","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a comparative study of optical absorption and luminescence from cubic boron nitride (cBN) and diamond grown by the high-pressure high-temperature technique in the same cubic press. We note remarkable similarities in spectral and spatial dependences for these two materials. Using the previous identification of defects in diamond, we tentatively assign the optical center responsible for yellow color in some cBN crystals to substitutional oxygen at the nitrogen site, the RC1 and RC3 centers to a defect comprising substitutional oxygen and a boron vacancy in the neutral and negative charge states, respectively, the GC1 center to a nickel-related defect, the 1.816 eV (683 nm) luminescence peak to a Si-vacancy complex, and the BN1 center to an interstitial-related defect."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-05T02:23:35Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:23:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03819","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03819","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03819","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Balabán","name":"Balabán, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Bukor","name":"Bukor, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From b-Coloring to $b^*$-Coloring: Large Girth and Parameterized Complexity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:26:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T19:47:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A b-coloring is a proper vertex coloring such that every color class contains a vertex, a so-called b-vertex, which sees all colors in its closed neighborhood. This type of coloring has been intensively studied from both structural and algorithmic point of view. Recently, Zaker [DAM 2025] introduced the notion of a b*-coloring, which is a b-coloring in which there is a vertex that sees a b-vertex of every color in its closed neighborhood. The b*-chromatic number is the maximum integer k such that there is a b*-coloring with k colors.\n We partially answer a question posed by Zaker and prove that graphs of girth at least 7 are b*-monotonic, which means that the b*-chromatic number does not increase by taking an induced subgraph. In addition, we discover a class of d-regular graphs of girth at least 5 with b*-chromatic number d+1, which strengthens a result about b-colorings by Dettlaff, Furmańczyk, Peterin, Roux, and Ziemann [AMC 2024].\n We also study the parameterized complexity of finding b*-colorings, and show that for many structural parameters, the complexity coincides with that of finding b-colorings. In particular, the b*-chromatic number can be computed in polynomial time on any class of bounded clique-width. For most parameters, the translation from b-colorings is straightforward but for the feedback edge number, the FPT algorithm for b*-colorings is actually much simpler than that for b-colorings by Balabán [MFCS 2026]."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03819","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:21:43Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:21:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Preying on Leveraged ETFs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:09:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:57:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T21:23:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:12:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T17:52:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:55:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-29T04:28:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We argue that speculators preying on the closing rebalances of leveraged exchange-traded funds (LETFs) contributed to the Korean market's extreme volatility in 2026. An LETF's mandated daily rebalance is sized by the day's return, which generates an upward-sloping demand at market close. In response, rational speculators pre-position, enlarge the fund's order, and liquidate into the demand they have induced. Consistent with this mechanism, Korean stocks tracked by LETFs reverse about 75% of their first-day response to pre-open U.S. news by the next close and oscillate for several days thereafter, a pattern absent in every control group. Our quantification implies that self-reinforcing rebalance raised SK Hynix's annualized volatility from 100.0% to 136.7% over nine weeks and cost its products' predominantly retail holders 17.6% of their initial investment. Dispersing the rebalance across the trading day may backfire, whereas a flexible leverage multiple could help."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:01Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Razieh","familyName":"Chalehchaleh","name":"Chalehchaleh, Razieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Farahbakhsh","name":"Farahbakhsh, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noel","familyName":"Crespi","name":"Crespi, Noel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unequal Verdicts: Investigating Gender Bias in LLM-Based Fake News Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:14:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:53:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T21:10:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:22:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used for automated fact-checking, yet their susceptibility to gender bias in this context remains underexplored. This study presents the first systematic investigation of gender bias in LLM-based fake news detection using real-world data. We augment the LIAR benchmark with three gender variants of speaker job titles (Neutral, Male, Female) for each statement to test whether veracity judgments vary solely based on gender presentation. Six state-of-the-art LLMs are evaluated across multiple bias and fairness metrics. All models exhibit gender sensitivity: 9.79%-35.13% of statements receive inconsistent labels across the three variants, with Male-Female comparisons showing 6.5%-23.6% flip rates. Two primary bias manifestations are identified: instability (inconsistent judgments) and directionality (systematic favoritism). Five models show statistically significant directional effects, with the strongest effects displaying male-skeptic patterns. These findings demonstrate that gender bias undermines both reliability and fairness in LLM-based fake news detection, highlighting the need for bias-aware evaluation and mitigation strategies. The augmented dataset is publicly released to support future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 6th Workshop on Bias and Fairness in AI at ECML PKDD 2026. Dataset available at https://github.com/raziehch/GenderedLIARDataset"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:17:18Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:17:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuannuo","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yuannuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zegang","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zegang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubing","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yubing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenshuai","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Wenshuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Approximate Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:45:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:45:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T07:49:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:33:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-30T16:55:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:13:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding accelerates autoregressive generation by verifying a draft block with a target model in parallel. Under standard greedy verification, decoding stops at the first draft token that differs from the target argmax, discarding the remaining target-scored suffix. Although accepting such a mismatch changes the decoding trajectory, it can make a contiguous suffix reusable when its tokens remain target-greedy under the realized prefix. In this paper, we introduce \\textbf{Approximate Speculative Decoding (ASD)}, a training-free verifier that replaces binary first-mismatch truncation with budgeted longest-prefix selection. ASD accepts selected mismatches subject to a local target-logit regret gate, a per-block exception cap, and a persistent request-level regret budget, then reuses the contiguous target-greedy suffix without additional approximate decisions or target-model forward passes. ASD requires neither a new draft model nor fine-tuning, and exactly reduces to standard greedy verification when the budget is zero. Experiments show that ASD improves fixed-workload throughput by $3.05\\%$--$15.26\\%$ over matched strict verification and averages a $7.78\\%$ gain across seven Qwen3-14B + DSpark-14B tasks. On DeepSeek-V4-Flash (284B) with DSpark it also raises verifier-side acceptance by roughly $10\\%$--$16\\%$ on GSM8K and MATH-500 in an FP4-to-FP8 compatibility setting. The source code is publicly available at: https://github.com/Kissmetothemoon/ASD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:04Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reinoud Jan","familyName":"Slagter","name":"Slagter, Reinoud Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"New gravitational instanton: shadow of an extra dimension"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T12:15:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:49:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-29T13:13:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:36:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present an exact gravitational instanton solution on a five-dimensional, conformally invariant, Kerr-like warped Riemannian brane-world manifold. The geometry can be described as the Kähler manifold $\\mathbb{C}^1\\times\\mathbb{C}^1\\times \\mathbb{R}$. By applying a double cover of $S^3$ through stereographic projection onto $\\mathbb{C}P^1\\times \\mathbb{C}P^1$ of the effective four-dimensional manifold, together with the Klein surface construction, we exploit the underlying $\\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry. The instanton is then obtained by fibering over the antipodal $S^2$. The metric is determined by a first-order differential equation containing an integer parameter, while the equation governing the angular momentum component decouples from the remaining field equations. Finally, we show that our solution admits an analytic complex transformation to a locally conformally related Kähler manifold possessing a Kähler potential, thereby making the self-dual structure manifest."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"V2: Some minor corrections. Some figures added. Shorter version of previous submission. 23 pages and 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:15:13Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:15:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03438","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03438","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03438","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismaël","familyName":"Zighed","name":"Zighed, Ismaël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Thome","name":"Thome, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Gallinari","name":"Gallinari, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taraneh","familyName":"Sayadi","name":"Sayadi, Taraneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modelisation of chaotic systems with a latent Stochastic Differential Equation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:33:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:44:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T13:49:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:09:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Stochastic Differential Equations (SDEs) have become a cornerstone of scientific machine learning, though they are predominantly utilized as algorithmic tools for uncertainty quantification or distribution matching. In contrast, leveraging SDEs fundamentally to model macroscopic, nonlinear physics as stochastic processes remains largely unexplored. This work introduces a probabilistic, non-intrusive reduced-order model (ROM) for chaotic dynamical systems. We argue that projecting high-dimensional nonlinear dynamics onto a low-dimensional manifold introduces irreducible uncertainty, compounded by the chaotic attractors and multi-admissible futures inherent to turbulent flows. Consequently, a chaotic system governed by a partial differential equation can be effectively modeled by an SDE in a suitable latent space. To this end, a nonlinear autoencoder is employed to map the flow field into a low-dimensional representation, within which the temporal evolution is explicitly governed by an SDE. The predictable component of the dynamics is captured by a learned drift term, while state-dependent stochasticity is absorbed by a diffusion term. We demonstrate that this probabilistic framework successfully propagates highly nonlinear states, offering a robust alternative to traditional deterministic methodologies for chaotic regimes. Ultimately, our model generates new chaotic flow trajectories that remain locally and globally consistent with the true transition kernel learned from Direct Numerical Simulation (DNS) data. Even though these generated trajectories are unique and distinct from the training set, they preserve the underlying statistics and manifolds, validating the strong generative performance and robustness of our methodology."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03438","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:52Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03437","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03437","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03437","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vilém","familyName":"Zouhar","name":"Zouhar, Vilém","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julia","familyName":"Kreutzer","name":"Kreutzer, Julia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alon","familyName":"Lavie","name":"Lavie, Alon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom","familyName":"Kocmi","name":"Kocmi, Tom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"Post","name":"Post, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ondřej","familyName":"Bojar","name":"Bojar, Ondřej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmaya","familyName":"Sachan","name":"Sachan, Mrinmaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamically Allocating Evaluation Effort for Model Ranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:33:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:44:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-29T19:46:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:45:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While human evaluation is the gold standard in many NLP tasks, it suffers from prohibitive costs and poor scalability. When identifying top-performing models, typical evaluation protocols waste effort by exhaustively evaluating all models on the entire benchmark, a safe but inefficient approach. In this work, we formalize multi-model human evaluation as a best-arm identification problem in a multi-armed bandit setup with correlated arms, where pulling an arm corresponds to human-evaluating a model. By sampling adaptively based on the intermediate model rankings obtained on the samples so far, we can focus the annotation budget on the most competitive models. We prove the optimality of the proposed algorithms and show that it improves discrimination between top-performing models. This makes evaluations faster, cheaper and more aligned with large-scale competition evaluation goals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2026; typeset with Typst"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03437","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:50Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03423","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03423","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03423","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Runyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjun","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Rongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SGFormer: Structure-Guided Transformer for Robust Local Feature Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:14:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:43:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T04:49:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:54:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Local feature matching is a fundamental component of photogrammetry, enabling accurate image correspondence critical for tasks such as 3D reconstruction, stereo mapping, and visual localization. While recent detector-free matching methods, like LoFTR, have advanced the field, the global features obtained by leveraging the global-range modeling capacity of the unconstrained attention mechanism compromise the model's attention to the salient structures in certain scenarios. This limitation leads to a phenomenon we define as attention divergence, wherein a portion of high-confidence matches are distributed outside the valid matching region (overlapping region), especially in scenes with large viewpoint variations. This occurs because similar features in irrelevant regions may receive equal weighting and consideration within the standard Transformer, limiting matching reliability in challenging photogrammetric environments. To address this issue in feature matching, we propose SGFormer (Structure-Guided Transformer), a novel structure-aware matching network that adaptively updates attention on features near salient structure in overlapping regions. SGFormer employs a semi-dense coarse-to-fine pipeline and incorporates the proposed Triple-Structure-Attention (TSA) module into the backbone net for extracting distinctive features. The TSA module utilizes shallow local features from early network layers to enhance the representation around salient structure, guiding subsequent transformer stages to intensify the model's focus on regions with salient structure across the global scope. SGFormer, thereby reinforcing attention to visually consistent areas while mitigating the influence of non-overlapping regions. Extensive experiments show that SGFormer significantly mitigates attention divergence and improves matching accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03423","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:32Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haichuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Haichuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunrong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Chunrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Quanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Evolving Coding Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:43:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:42:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T06:07:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:25:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-29T04:09:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:22:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly embedded in software engineering workflows as coding agents that can inspect repositories, invoke tools, execute tests, debug failures, and generate patches. Yet most existing coding agents remain largely static after deployment, even though software development is a dynamic, feedback-rich process in which repositories evolve, dependencies change, tests fail, and repair attempts leave reusable experience. This tension has motivated a growing body of work on self-evolving coding agents, where the agent improves its future behavior by persistently updating its framework, memory, skills and tools, components, workflow and topology, or environment and context from prior coding interactions. In this survey, we provide a structured synthesis of this emerging area. We first define the concept of self-evolving coding agents and distinguish it from conventional coding agents and general self-evolving agents. We then develop a taxonomy centered on the targets of evolution, complemented by two orthogonal perspectives: when evolution occurs and which code-specific signals drive it. We further examine the benchmarks used to measure the effect of evolution and related coding products. Across the literature, we find that executable feedback, repository-level context, and coding trajectories make software engineering a natural domain for agent self-evolution, but also introduce challenges in feedback reliability, benchmark overfitting, reversibility, system complexity, safety, cost, and generalization. By organizing existing work around these dimensions, this survey aims to clarify the conceptual boundaries of self-evolving coding agents and provide a foundation for designing more adaptive, reliable, and software-aware agentic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:48Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroki","familyName":"Shibata","name":"Shibata, Hiroki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuto","familyName":"Fujie","name":"Fujie, Yuto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sensitivity and Size Relationships of the Lempel-Ziv Factorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T08:59:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T06:52:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:57:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Lempel-Ziv (LZ) factorization is one of the most fundamental methods for compressing highly repetitive strings, and the number of phrases in its factorization is considered a repetitiveness measure. Sensitivity to an edit operation measures the maximum increase in a repetitiveness measure when the operation is applied to a string. While asymptotically tight bounds are known for the sensitivity of the LZ factorization to single-character edits, whether its multiplicative sensitivity is bounded by a constant has remained open for operations that change a large part of the structure of a string, such as prefix deletion, substring deletion, cyclic rotation, and string reversal. We resolve this question. For each of these four operations, we construct a family of strings in which a string of length $n$ has sensitivity $Ω(\\log n)$ to that operation. We also determine the size relationships among the LZ factorization, collage systems and the lex-parse. We construct a family of strings whose LZ factorizations are $Ω(\\log n)$ times larger than their minimum collage systems, and a family of strings whose lex-parses are $Ω(\\log n)$ times larger than their LZ factorizations. Furthermore, we prove that there exists a family of strings for which every LZ encoding of height $O({\\rm poly}\\,\\log n)$ is $Ω(\\log n / \\log \\log n)$ times larger than the standard LZ factorization. Except for the lower bound on height-bounded LZ encodings, all of these lower bounds are asymptotically tight, matching $O(\\log n)$ upper bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:10:52Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:10:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonpyo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Wonpyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-won","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Seung-won","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TaskPress: Query-Agnostic KV Cache Compression via Task-Guided Pruning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T07:51:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:34:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T22:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:23:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-context inference with large language models is constrained by the linear growth of the key-value cache to sequence length. While pruning offers mitigation, prevailing methods determine query-specific token importance that cannot be reused across unseen queries. In contrast, we introduce TaskPress, a framework for task-guided, query-agnostic KV cache eviction. Instead of optimizing the cache for a single query, TaskPress constructs a reusable memory representation conditioned on a high-level task guide. The guide functions as a meta-query during prefill to filter irrelevant tokens before downstream queries are issued. In addition, TaskPress leverages quantization scale factors as a zero-cost signal for detecting influential representation outliers, providing an efficient proxy for token importance. Experiments on conducted on various tasks with long context input demonstrate that TaskPress efficiently creates a compact, reusable cache across diverse queries."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:09:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:09:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03117","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03117","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03117","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kentaro","familyName":"Kaba","name":"Kaba, Kentaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masayuki","familyName":"Ohzeki","name":"Ohzeki, Masayuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Sughiyama","name":"Sughiyama, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simulation-free and finite-time diffusion model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T04:39:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:25:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T02:24:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:28:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The performance of generative diffusion models is determined by the choice of the reference diffusion process connecting the empirical and prior distributions. Conventional approaches typically trade off simulation-free training against finite-time generation. We propose a framework for designing the reference process that achieves both simultaneously. The key idea is to prescribe tractable time-dependent conditional distributions and then construct the reference process realizing them as its marginals. This framework reveals that score matching is not fundamental to diffusion-model training but instead emerges naturally through reversal of the reference process. We further show that conditional flow matching arises as the small-noise limit of the proposed framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03117","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:05:28Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:05:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03044","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03044","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03044","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seth","familyName":"Grief-Albert","name":"Grief-Albert, Seth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Difan","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Difan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashton","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Ashton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emulate or Estimate? The Divergent Strengths of Base and Post-Trained Language Models for Opinion Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T02:51:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:20:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T12:59:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:04:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly used to simulate human opinions, but prior work reports conflicting results: some studies find promising alignment with human survey data, while others find persona collapse and weak demographic sensitivity. We propose that much of this conflict stems from conflating two distinct tasks. We call the first task emulation, in which models generate individual responses that aggregate into a population distribution. We call the second task estimation, in which models directly predict the population distribution. Evaluating six matched base and post-trained models on the Pew American Trends Panel, we find that base models are the stronger emulators: they produce response distributions closer to human ground truth and better preserve demographic structure. Post-trained models are generally the stronger estimators, producing more accurate distributional predictions when asked directly. We argue that model selection for human simulation should be guided by whether the task requires generating text or predicting distributions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Findings 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03044","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:03:48Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:03:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cenke","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Cenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew P. A.","familyName":"Fisher","name":"Fisher, Matthew P. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Particle-Vortex Duality of Hydrodynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:00:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-29T15:10:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:39:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Equipped with the recently recognized symmetry structure of mixed states of matter and \"strong-weak spontaneous symmetry breaking\" (SW-SSB), we develop a quantum particle-vortex duality of emergent model-F and model-A hydrodynamics of 2d boson/rotor systems. This duality relation demonstrates that classical hydrodynamics of 2d bosons can be described in terms of the charge symmetry $U(1)_c$, but also equivalently in terms of the dual (emergent) 1-form symmetry $U(1)^{(1)}_e$, as well as $U(1)_v$ associated with the conservation of vortices. This duality provides a bridge between the hydrodynamics of matter and magnetohydrodynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:56:34Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:56:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02665","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02665","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02665","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yongxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwei","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Junwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanzhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai Yun","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Lai Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Single Canonical Prompts Underestimate LLM Safety's Surface-Form Sensitivity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; K.4.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T22:28:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:01:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-30T05:09:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T00:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A benchmark score is a measurement instrument, yet most benchmarks read each item at a single canonical surface form. We ask whether that reading is faithful: when an item's intent is held fixed and only its meaning-preserving surface form varies, does the canonical-form score estimate model behavior well, and how much of any variation is decoding/judge noise rather than signal? We instantiate this in safety, a high-stakes setting with no gold label to average toward. To avoid prior confounds, we pre-author the reformulations (refusal-free, mostly non-LLM: machine back-translation and a Matrix-Language-Frame code-switch generator) so an identical surface form reaches every model, score all responses with one human-anchored, vendor-neutral judge (Claude, kappa = 0.86 vs. human on unsafe compliance, stable across languages, cross-checked by GPT-4o), and verify intent preservation. On 370 seeds x 5 surface forms x 5 models, no single transformation is uniformly most dangerous (6 of 20 per-transformation McNemar tests survive correction, most protective). Yet evaluating only the canonical prompt underestimates unsafe compliance: the union of unsafe outcomes across forms exceeds even the worst single form by 3.3-12.9 pp, with bootstrap 95% CIs excluding zero for all five models, and 5-13% of seeds safe on canonical are unsafe under some reformulation -- above a zero stochasticity floor (canonical resampled five times at temperature 0 gives 0/370 new exposures). The size of this gap is model-dependent (largest on Gemini 2.5 Pro). One form recovers only ~53% of a model's observed unsafe surface and about three reach 85% -- a redundancy characterization of this form set, not of a defined population. A benign control (XSTest) suggests the instability is bidirectional, though the benign and harmful pools are not item-matched. We release the dataset, code, and per-response labels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the Sci-FM Workshop @ COLM 2026 (non-archival). Workshop version with reviews: https://openreview.net/forum?id=mZh0MqpOOC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02665","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:55:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:55:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:19:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03795","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03795","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03795","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"O.","familyName":"Voskresenskaya","name":"Voskresenskaya, O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deser formula for the shift of energy levels of hadronic atoms in terms of the effective radius of the strong interaction with the Coulomb wave function of hadronic atoms at the origin"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:11:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T11:50:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:44:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is shown that the relations between probabilities of A_2π-atoms creation in ns-states, derived with neglecting of strong interaction between pions, remain practically unchanged if the strong interaction is taken into account in the first order of perturbation theory. The formulation of Deser equation for the energy shift of energy levels of hadronic atoms is given in terms of the effective range of strong interaction and the relative correction to the Coulomb wave function of hadronic atoms at the origin, caused by the strong interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, pdf"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03795","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:21:10Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:21:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:26:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03771","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03771","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03771","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Benigni","name":"Benigni, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgio","familyName":"Cipolloni","name":"Cipolloni, Giorgio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Local law and delocalization for the Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:55:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:59:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T15:35:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:58:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a mesoscopic local law and quantitative eigenvector-delocalization estimates for the Sachdev--Ye--Kitaev model (SYK) Hamiltonian of $N$ interacting Majorana fermions. For even $q\\ll N^{\\frac{1}{2}}$, we prove that the normalized Stieltjes transform converges uniformly on bounded energy intervals to that of the standard Gaussian law, down to scales of order $qN^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$, up to logarithmic factors. The result holds both on the full Hilbert space and in each fermion-parity sector. We derive mesoscopic eigenvalue counting and spectral form factor estimates, as well as an averaged inverse participation ratio bound. For fixed $q$, we further obtain high-probability $\\ell^\\infty$-delocalization bounds in any deterministic orthonormal basis for individual bulk eigenvectors. These are the first mesoscopic laws and eigenvector delocalization estimates for the SYK Hamiltonian, which we obtain using the resolvent method."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03771","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:20:35Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:20:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:26:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriele","familyName":"Cassese","name":"Cassese, Gabriele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A solution to Morrey's problem in $\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times m}$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"49J45, 35A01 (Primary) 46E40, 60G42 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:25:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:47:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T17:14:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T01:03:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct, for any $p\\in(1,\\infty)$, $p$-homogeneous rank-one convex integrands $F\\colon\\mathbb R^{2\\times m}\\to \\mathbb R$ that are nowhere quasiconvex when $m$ is large. When $p$ is sufficiently close to $4$, such examples can be constructed on $\\mathbb R^{2\\times 4}$. Related constructions give, for every $p$, conjugation- and transposition-invariant examples on $\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$, and examples on $\\mathbb R^{4\\times 2}$ for every $p\\neq 2$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages. The new version includes new examples in dimension 2x4. Comments are more than welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:14:07Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:14:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:26:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhong","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Yizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian-Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tian-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunfeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Wenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingming","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruimin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ya","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CLASVS: Continuous-Latent Autoregression for Melody-Preserving Lyric Editing in Singing Voice Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T07:24:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:33:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T06:30:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:33:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T06:38:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:29:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reference-conditioned melody-preserving lyric editing replaces words while retaining a performance's timing, singer identity, and naturalness. Continuous-latent autoregression avoids finite codebooks and offers stepwise generation with learned stopping. Editing creates a conflict absent from ordinary reconstruction: training pairs reference cues with original lyrics, whereas inference asks revised lyrics to override source-lyric-correlated cues; one source-following patch can propagate through AR history. We introduce CLASVS. Its State-Control-Transition (SCT) routing keeps target-lyric and reference-melody controls persistent, returns semantic feedback on phonetic progress to the causal planner, and confines the previous latent patch to the local Transition. Progressive State-Control Grounding (PSCG) learns this contract through paired-edit-free, content-consistent Mandarin reconstruction. On two Mandarin benchmarks, CLASVS improves all four operations over discrete-AR Vevo2 and reduces macro-PER by 46.2%, while maintaining melody, singer similarity, and perceptual quality. Together, these results establish a strong continuous-AR operating point for score-annotation-free lyric edits and a basis for broader stepwise control. Audio demonstrations are available on our project page: https://piedpiperg.github.io/clasvs-demo/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:08:36Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:08:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:26:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richie","familyName":"Sater","name":"Sater, Richie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finite groups that are the product of every pair of non-conjugate maximal subgroups are soluble"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"20D40 (Primary) 20E28, 20D05 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T00:06:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:15:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T01:01:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T23:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:14:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that every finite group that is the product of every pair of its non-conjugate maximal subgroups is soluble, answering Problem 10.34 of the Kourovka Notebook. The almost-simple case was proved by Tikhonenko and Tyutyanov. We treat the remaining minimal-counterexample branch, where the unique minimal normal subgroup has the form S^k, with S nonabelian simple and k \u0026gt;= 2. Two automorphism-stable coordinate subgroup classes satisfying a maximal-supplement criterion produce non-conjugate maximal supplements. If the factorization hypothesis held, their product would impose a p-adic divisibility requirement growing linearly with k, while the quotient contributes only coordinate outer automorphisms and a factor dividing k!. A fixed valuation gap therefore excludes every k \u0026gt;= 2 at once. Suitable subgroup classes are constructed uniformly across the infinite families of finite simple groups using parabolic, torus, and primitive-prime-divisor arguments; stable flag parabolics handle graph fusion, while GAP certificates cover designated finite and sporadic cases. The resulting all-k obstruction provides a reusable mechanism for eliminating direct-power socles in finite-group factorization problems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 4 tables, no figures. Corrected manuscript; abstract, author information, end matter, and exposition revised; results unchanged. Supporting materials: https://github.com/RichieSater/kourovka-10-34 (v1.1.2); archive: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22134798"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:02:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:02:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:26:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02751","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02751","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02751","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haodong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyao","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Shengyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bevan","familyName":"Koopman","name":"Koopman, Bevan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Zuccon","name":"Zuccon, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Search, Inspect, Fetch: Exploiting Structure-Aware Boolean Retrieval for Deep-Search Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:01:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T02:06:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:18:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T09:06:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:37:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing deep-search agents use a Search-Visit workflow that retrieves whole webpages without considering the structure they expose through titles, headings, sections, and metadata. This prevents agents from directly constraining retrieval to parts of a webpage and often carries irrelevant content into their context. We introduce Sieve, a search-inspect-fetch strategy driven by a Boolean Query Language (BQL): it searches webpage fields to filter candidates, uses an interchangeable ranker to order them, presents structure-rich result cards for inspection, and fetches only selected sections. Across three QA collections, Sieve is more accurate than the strongest conventional Search-Visit configuration on each collection while using 20.7-50.6% fewer tokens. Boolean filtering improves every tested ranker, and the accuracy-context advantage persists across retriever choices and agent backbones. Our implementation is included in the SkimSearchAgent library https://github.com/ielab/skim-search-agent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"clean up text, format, clearer setup;"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02751","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:01Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:26:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunlei","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Chunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacqueline J.","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jacqueline J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengbin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Pengbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongxue","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Zhongxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Modality Reliability in Multimodal Sentiment Analysis with Incomplete Observations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:02:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T11:17:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:46:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Sentiment Analysis (MSA) integrates text, audio, and vision to infer human affect, yet real-world multimodal observations are often incomplete. Existing methods for incomplete-observation MSA mainly follow two paradigms. Reconstruction-based methods recover missing information from observed modalities, while joint-representation methods learn directly from incomplete inputs. Although effective, these methods usually treat modality reliability only implicitly within representation learning or fusion design rather than modeling it explicitly. We argue that modality reliability is a central variable in incomplete-observation settings. Failure to model it explicitly gives rise to two related issues. The first is reliability mismatch, in which the affective evidence retained by each modality varies across samples and missing rates. The second is reliability propagation bias, in which messages from degraded modalities may adversely affect cross-modal interaction and predictive performance. To address these issues, we propose MRCF, a Modality Reliability-Calibrated Framework for MSA with incomplete observations. MRCF contains a Reliability-Aware Branch that estimates sample-specific modality reliability from intramodal quality cues and cross-modal semantic consistency, a Reliability-Guided Interaction Branch that uses the estimated scores to modulate cross-modal information flow, and a Reliability-Calibrated Fusion Module that integrates reliability and semantic cues for final prediction. Experiments on CMU-MOSI, CMU-MOSEI, and CH-SIMS show that MRCF achieves strong performance under standard incomplete-observation protocols. Further analyses provide evidence that explicit reliability modeling helps mitigate reliability mismatch and reliability propagation bias during interaction and fusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:16:55Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:16:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Yahyavi","name":"Yahyavi, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Gholizadeh","name":"Gholizadeh, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohrab","familyName":"Behnia","name":"Behnia, Sohrab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilal","familyName":"Tanatar","name":"Tanatar, Bilal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chaos suppression via adaptive feedback control of intermittency: From exactly solvable ergodic maps to interacting microbubble clusters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:43:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:29:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/sdfg-l9l1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Intermittency represents a fundamental route to chaos in nonlinear dynamical systems. In this work we introduce an adaptive control strategy in which the control parameter of an intermittent system is promoted to a dynamical variable that evolves autonomously under an auxiliary nonlinear map drawn from the same functional hierarchy as the system itself. The construction eliminates the need for orbit identification, local linearization, and trajectory-triggered perturbations, which are central ingredients of conventional feedback schemes. The theoretical framework is developed within a class of one-dimensional nonlinear ergodic maps with exactly known invariant (Sinai--Ruelle--Bowen) measures, for which we derive in closed form (i) the dynamics and invariant measure of the evolving control parameter, (ii) the invariant measure of the coupled system, and (iii) the $q$-generalized Lyapunov exponents before and after control. The generalized Lyapunov spectrum serves as an analytical order parameter for the control process: the collapse of its positive regions provides a quantitative and initial-condition-independent signature of chaos suppression, and yields the sensitivity to initial conditions in explicit form. To establish the physical relevance of the approach beyond low-dimensional maps, we apply the same construction to a cluster of three interacting ultrasound-driven microbubbles described by the Keller--Herring model, promoting the experimentally accessible acoustic driving frequency to a dynamical variable. Systematic bifurcation and Lyapunov analyses, performed over wide ranges of driving pressure, frequency, and equilibrium radii, demonstrate that intermittent chaotic radial oscillations are progressively suppressed and replaced by stable periodic motion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":2,"created":"2026-08-05T02:20:10Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:20:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mattes","familyName":"Wittig","name":"Wittig, Mattes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral and Pseudospectral Approximation of Finite-Interaction-Range Operators in Doubling Metric Measure Spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T12:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:48:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T19:08:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:06:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study bounded finite-interaction-range operators $H$ on $\\ell^2(Γ)$, where $Γ$ is a uniformly discrete subset of a left-invariant doubling metric measure space $(X,d,μ)$. Our goal is to approximate spectral information of $H$ from finite sections $H_{L,x}$ supported on balls $B_L(x)\\capΓ$. The main technical input is a commutator estimate for Lipschitz ''tent'' localisations $W_{L,x}$, which depends only on geometric properties (doubling) and a uniform interaction degree. As a consequence, we obtain explicit two-sided pseudospectral inclusion bounds of the form \\[\n γ_{L,\\varepsilon}(H)\\subset σ_\\varepsilon(H)\\subset γ_{L,\\varepsilon+C_0/L}(H), \\] and Hausdorff convergence of window pseudospectra to the (global) pseudospectrum as $L\\to\\infty$. In the self-adjoint/ normal case this yields computable gap tests and spectral sampling schemes with rigorous $O(1/L)$ error control, while in the non-normal case it leads to corresponding approximation results for pseudospectra. The framework isolates the geometric core behind earlier approaches on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and on countable Abelian groups, and covers irregular geometries arising in quasicrystal models, as well as new cases such as discrete (non-Abelian) nilpotent groups (including for example the discrete Heisenberg group)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Geometric framework revised; main results unchanged. 26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:15:00Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:15:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinyuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ruixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Residual Flow Matching with Dynamic Cross-Interaction for 3D Multi-Person Motion Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:28:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:41:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T16:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T01:05:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D multi-person motion prediction requires modeling both individual kinematics and inter-person interactions. While Flow Matching is effective for multi-hypothesis generation to improve prediction accuracy, directly predicting skeletal sequences from pure noise often compromises structural consistency and introduces unreliable cross-agent interactions during early noise-dominated integration steps. To address this, we propose a Prior-Guided Residual Flow Matching framework. First, a Deterministic Coarse Prior (DCP) establishes a kinematic anchor, formulating the generative process as a conditional flow over motion residuals to simplify the generative objective and preserve structural stability. Second, a Dynamic Cross-Interaction (DCI) mechanism temporally synchronizes inter-agent message-passing with the integration progress, ensuring the extraction of reliable social contexts and improving multi-person motion fidelity. Finally, a decoupled joint-motion architecture with bidirectional fusion effectively preserves fine-grained kinematic coherence. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach achieves state-of-the-art prediction accuracy across multiple datasets. Code is available at https://github.com/Wei-Wei-a/Residual-Flow-Matching-with-Dynamic-Cross-Interaction-for-3D-Multi-Person-Motion-Prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:30Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03435","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03435","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03435","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keshav","familyName":"Aggarwal","name":"Aggarwal, Keshav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. K.","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, R. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhirup","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Abhirup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumyaneal","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Soumyaneal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Manikantan","name":"Manikantan, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshuman","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Anshuman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takeshi","familyName":"Imamura","name":"Imamura, Takeshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A study on the contribution of the interplanetary medium in radio occultation experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Space Physics (physics.space-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:30:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:33:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.icarus.2026.117291","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Irregularities in electron density within the interplanetary medium (IPM) can cause fluctuations in the Doppler frequency of spacecraft radio signals. The amplitude of these fluctuations depends on factors such as the carrier frequency, propagation geometry, and link configuration. However, quantitative characterization of these effects across different frequencies in various occultation experiments is currently limited. We analyze five complementary datasets: two-way S-band observations from Chandrayaan-3 outside the lunar ionosphere, two-way S-band data from Chandrayaan-2 during lunar occultation, one-way S/X band measurements from the Venus Express Radio Science (VeRa)/Akatsuki Radio Science (Akatsuki) under IPM-only conditions, and one-way X-band Akatsuki data during solar occultation. The Chandrayaan-3 and Akatsuki IPM observations isolate IPM effects by excluding contributions from planetary atmospheres, the lunar ionosphere, and, except during solar occultation, the solar corona. Chandrayaan-3 data sample dynamically evolving Earth-Moon geometries and exhibit weak, mHz-level Doppler fluctuations, while Chandrayaan-2 observations provide near-lunar plasma benchmarks with higher amplitudes, during quiet time solar and geomagnetic conditions. Akatsuki and VeRa's IPM-only measurements capture long-path interplanetary effects, whereas Akatsuki solar occultation data reveal strong coronal signatures. Power spectral density analysis indicates Kolmogorov-like turbulence for lunar occultation and solar occultation cases, while IPM-only spectra show low-amplitude fluctuations. These results quantify the IPM contribution to Doppler noise, demonstrate the enhanced plasma sensitivity of two-way coherent links, and provide constraints relevant to turbulence modelling, precision spacecraft tracking, and the interpretation of radio occultation experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 10 figures. Accepted for publication in Icarus"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03435","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:48Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Bladt","name":"Bladt, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Henzi","name":"Henzi, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bram van den","familyName":"Heuvel","name":"Heuvel, Bram van den","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanna","familyName":"Ziegel","name":"Ziegel, Johanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Survival Isotonic Distributional Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G08 (Primary) 62N02, 62G20, 62G30, 62P10 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T21:57:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T16:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Survival-IDR (S-IDR), a nonparametric estimator of conditional survival distributions under order restrictions, extending Isotonic Distributional Regression (IDR; Henzi et al., 2021) to right-censored outcomes. S-IDR has no tuning parameters and accommodates continuous, discrete, and partially ordered covariates. We first study the direct Kaplan-Meier adaptation of IDR: it is uniformly consistent at the minimax rate, but only when the conditional outcomes are hazard-rate ordered. We trace this restriction to the Kaplan-Meier estimator's failure to satisfy the Cauchy mean value property on non-i.i.d. samples, and use the diagnosis to construct S-IDR. The S-IDR estimator is uniformly consistent under only stochastic dominance of the conditional outcomes, attains the minimax rate when the smoothness of the conditional CDFs is known, and admits a known cross-threshold PAVA acceleration. We further embed S-IDR in a distributional single-index framework on a benchmark suite, and apply it in a case study that validates the MELD score used for liver-transplant wait list management. Accompanying R, Python and Rust packages are available at https://github.com/AlexanderHenzi/isodistrreg."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"65 pages, 16 figures, 3 tables. Main text 35 pages; appendices contain all proofs"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:00:49Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:00:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02727","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02727","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02727","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntian","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yuntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhehao","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yantao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spin-charge separation in the triangular-lattice Hofstadter-Hubbard model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:00:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T20:44:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:10:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent experiments in moiré materials have enabled the realization of a variety of exotic quantum phases. In this context, the Hofstadter-Hubbard model has been proposed as a possible setting for hosting chiral spin liquid. Concurrently, significant progress has been recently made in the computational methods for two-dimensional many-body fermion systems, which makes numerically studying this challenging model a real possibility in genuine 2D geometry. Motivated by these advances, we investigate the putative chiral spin liquid phase in the triangular-lattice Hofstadter-Hubbard model using variational Monte Carlo with neural quantum states (NQS) and projected entangled pair states (PEPS). We observe spin-charge separation directly in real space through numerical spin-pumping simulation and real-time spin and charge motion. In addition, in the context of anyonic superconductivity conjectured in this model, we find a positive two-electron binding energy on small systems, but it decreases below our numerical resolution as the system size increases. Our work demonstrates NQS and PEPS as powerful tools, capable of cross-checking each other, for diagnosing topological order and fractionalized excitations in strongly correlated electronic systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 5 figures. 1 supplement"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02727","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:56:27Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:56:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02883","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02883","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02883","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nina","familyName":"Bodelot","name":"Bodelot, Nina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soufiane","familyName":"Belharbi","name":"Belharbi, Soufiane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Granger","name":"Granger, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test Time Adaptation Methods for Point Cloud Registration in Laparoscopic Surgery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T21:11:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:10:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T20:23:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:09:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D point cloud registration in laparoscopic surgery estimates the transformation between an intraoperative organ reconstructed from video and its preoperative mesh. Because ground-truth transformations are unavailable for real data, supervised networks are trained on synthetic organ pairs. At test time, real reconstructions differ from synthetic data and are noisy, sparse, and occluded, which degrades correspondence estimation. Test-time adaptation (TTA) can reduce this domain shift, but existing methods mainly rely on logits, entropy, class prototypes, or cache memories unavailable in registration. Registration also involves paired inputs with an asymmetric shift that primarily affects the intraoperative cloud. We analyse and modify state-of-the-art TTA methods from three families to 3D registration: model, normalization, and input adaptation. We analyze four representative approaches based on auxiliary-task model updates, backpropagation-free token purging, feature alignment, and layer-normalization calibration. We modify them to handle asymmetric shifts between preoperative and intraoperative point clouds and replace classification-based entropy objectives. Using a correspondence-based model trained on clean synthetic source data, we evaluate adaptation to corrupted synthetic and real target data on P2P and P2ILReg. For synthetic targets, we apply eight corruptions, including uniform noise and global density reduction, at five severity levels. All methods improve registration on P2P, whereas on P2ILReg only input adaptation reduces the error, while normalization adaptation degrades it. Considering the computational overhead of backpropagation-based adaptation, input adaptation is the most promising option for laparoscopic surgery, providing low inference latency and consistent error reductions across datasets. Code: https://github.com/ninaa-git/survey_pc_registration_tta"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02883","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:00:04Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:00:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:46:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Levent Ali","familyName":"Mengütürk","name":"Mengütürk, Levent Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Triangular Array Progression to Near-Log-Concave State Transitions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:22:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:07:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T22:52:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:10:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T18:21:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-20T17:39:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-21T01:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-25T21:13:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:10:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The paper introduces near-log-concave random variables and the state-transitions of a family of Markov chains derived from an algorithmic integer sequence progression. We propose a combinatorial rule that generates an asymmetric triangular array whose $n$-th row sums to $2^{n}$ for all $n \\geq 0$, where every row is the degree sequence of a multigraph without loops. The structure hosts infinitely many zero-free unimodal near-log-concave sequences whose log-concavity deviation converges to $\\log(4/3)$ under logarithmic scaling. From a probabilistic perspective, the construct defines a sequence of near-log-concave probability mass functions, from which, a structural decoupling of moments emerges: the mean diverges while the variance asymptotically converges to a steady-state limit. We study the Shannon entropy dynamics of the triangular array, and generate infinitely many right-stochastic matrices via coordinate transformations over the Tychonoff cube. Our specific framework indicates a broader methodology for modelling asymmetric stochastic systems, where distributions propagate as non-dispersive discrete wave packets, advancing indefinitely without flattening."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:24:51Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:24:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilídio","familyName":"Lopes","name":"Lopes, Ilídio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"General-relativistic structure of two-component quantum dark fermion stars"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T12:45:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:51:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T17:22:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T01:04:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/xzfb-4mrn","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a general-relativistic framework for two-component quantum dark fermion stars: equilibrium configurations of two degenerate fermion species governed by gravity, a Yukawa-mediated dark fifth force and a globally relevant Bohm quantum-pressure correction. The treatment retains the full covariant form of the nonlinear Klein--Gordon equation in the Schwarzschild interior, with closure relations valid at arbitrary compactness, from the ultralight baseline up to densities of order $0.16\\,\\mathrm{fm}^{-3}$ at which relativistic scalar densities and self-consistent effective fermion masses become unavoidable. Two Lagrangian parameters, the dark-fermion mass and the dimensionless ratio between the Yukawa channel and gravity, together fix the equilibrium structure; a joint measurement of mass and radius therefore constrains the dark-sector microphysics directly from gravitational-wave observables. For particle masses in the band $10^{-11}$--$10^{-10}$ eV, the configurations exhibit radii of $3$-$24\\,\\mathrm{km}$ and compactness in the range $0.14$-$0.34$, behaving as dual mimickers: at moderate compactness they overlap with neutron stars in mass, radius, and inspiral frequency; at the most compact end ($κ\\sim 0.34$, $R_T/R_S\\simeq 1.5$) they cross the photon sphere and could masquerade as low-mass black holes in the mass-gap region. Tidal-deformability measurements discriminate against both populations: the dimensionless tidal deformability is measurably smaller than the neutron-star value yet remains non-zero, unlike that of a genuine black hole. These objects populate the sensitivity bands of LISA, the Einstein Telescope, and Cosmic Explorer, producing astrometric microlensing signatures individually resolvable by \\textit{Gaia}. The framework thus provides a reproducible and falsifiable template for constraining dark-sector properties through multi-messenger observations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 10 figures and 3 tables. Accepted for publication in Phys. Rev. D 114, 035032, Published 25 August, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-05T02:16:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03539","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03539","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03539","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bokang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Bokang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaojiao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiaojiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IRIS: Visual-Semantic Binding for Forgery-Resistant Watermarking of Diffusion Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T12:16:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:49:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T12:57:26Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; Substantial revisions"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:48:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most in-generation diffusion watermarks embed patterns independent of the image that carries them, and attackers transplant the marks onto images the generator did not produce, resulting in forgery. Binding the mark to visual semantics prevents such transplantation, yet existing bindings anchor to a proxy image rather than the image they mark. Realizing visual-semantic binding inside generation faces two challenges. The mark derives from the image itself yet enters the sampling trajectory before that image exists, and may itself shift the semantics it binds. The binding also meets opposite sensitivity demands, breaking under semantic change while holding through common processing. We present IRIS, a training-free watermarking scheme that embeds an Intrinsic Ring Identifier from Semantics. IRIS reads a content code from the non-watermarked generated image, derives a one-time ring from the code and a secret key, returns to the final low-noise steps of the same trajectory and blends the ring in, after the semantics it binds are settled. To meet the opposite sensitivity demands, the code is read through a canonicalization shared between embedding and detection, holding through common distortions and mild regeneration while flipping under semantic change. Detection recomputes the ring from the query image and the key alone, and the mark therefore fails on a foreign or spliced image, with acceptance tracking semantic displacement. On three prompt datasets IRIS detects reliably and stays close to its same-seed non-watermarked counterpart, a fidelity prior in-generation marks do not reach. While forgeries transfer fixed-pattern marks and regeneration strips post-hoc marks, IRIS alone among the compared marks withstands both."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Substantial revisions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03539","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:15:17Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:15:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Minguzzi","name":"Minguzzi, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Global hyperbolicity meets order completeness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:14:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:46:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T14:04:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:52:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, an order completeness property has attracted attention in some low regularity spacetime geometry literature, where it was named `chronocompleteness'. In this work we prove that, in the framework of standard Lorentzian geometry, this property is equivalent to global hyperbolicity. The equivalence of global hyperbolicity with other more traditional forms of order completeness is also established."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages. v2: added Theorem 3.7"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:49Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03404","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03404","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03404","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishrak Alhajj","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Ishrak Alhajj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fixed Points, Stability, Basin Geometry, and Global Convergence of the $3\\times3$ Correlation Map"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"37C25 (Primary), 37C75, 37C70, 15B48 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:57:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T22:37:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:12:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For more than half a century, iterated Pearson correlation has underpinned methods for network blockmodeling, clustering, seriation, and information visualization. Despite its continued use and the longstanding assumption of convergence, the global convergence problem remained open even in dimension three. We resolve this problem completely for the $3\\times3$ correlation map: every admissible orbit is well defined for all forward iterates and converges to one of exactly seven fixed points. In arbitrary dimension, we establish an exact row-wise Gram factorization and the identity $\\operatorname{rank}C(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(AH_n)$, which gives the precise rank-reduction mechanism and forces every fixed point to be singular. We identify the all-ones matrix as the unique Pearson-degenerate correlation matrix, prove forward invariance of the Pearson-nondegenerate elliptope, and classify the $2^{n-1}-1$ nondegenerate sign-valued fixed points. For $n=3$, we prove a complete analytical fixed-point classification: three rank-one patterned points, three rank-two mixed points, and one rank-two equicorrelation point. We also prove the complete relative Lyapunov stability classification: precisely the patterned points are locally asymptotically stable, while the mixed and equicorrelation points are unstable. The global proof reduces the rank-two dynamics to a one-dimensional projective kernel coordinate; an exact order identity produces monotone projective ratios, excludes nontrivial periodic and recurrent limit sets, and forces convergence to a fixed point. Finally, the initial conditions converging to the four unstable fixed points form a Lebesgue-null set. Hence almost every admissible initial condition converges to a patterned fixed point; the three patterned basins are relatively open and permutation-equivalent, and each has Lebesgue measure exactly one third of that of the elliptope."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"45 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03404","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:05Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03383","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03383","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03383","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Thomson-Paressant","name":"Thomson-Paressant, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. M.","familyName":"Bowman","name":"Bowman, D. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Nardini","name":"Nardini, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. J. A.","familyName":"Scott","name":"Scott, L. J. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Bodensteiner","name":"Bodensteiner, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Shenar","name":"Shenar, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Mahy","name":"Mahy, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Handler","name":"Handler, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"N.","familyName":"Shitrit","name":"Shitrit, N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I.","familyName":"Arcavi","name":"Arcavi, I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Abdul-Masih","name":"Abdul-Masih, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Simon-Diaz","name":"Simon-Diaz, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Van Daele","name":"Van Daele, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. J.","familyName":"Kalita","name":"Kalita, A. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Dennis","name":"Dennis, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Henneco","name":"Henneco, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Tkachenko","name":"Tkachenko, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Sana","name":"Sana, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Van Winckel","name":"Van Winckel, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The symphony of pulsations and binarity among massive stars using HERMES spectroscopy and TESS photometry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:32:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T13:35:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/stag1493","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A wide range of variability mechanisms exist among intermediate mass and massive stars, which are not yet fully understood. Using complementary data sources for a large population of B- and O-type stars, we aim to study the prevalence and interplay of different types of variability, including binarity, pulsation, and rotation, to prepare for future modelling. To this end, we analyse high-resolution HERMES spectra and 2-min cadence TESS photometry and characterise the diverse variability observed within a population of 873 O- and B-type stars. The spectroscopic data were normalised using machine-learning techniques, compared to a grid of synthetic TLUSTY spectra to determine stellar parameters, and used to identify radial velocity variability. Photometric time series were analysed using standard frequency analysis methods to detect pulsations and rotational modulation signatures. We find that more than 93 per cent of the sample exhibits photometric variability. Photometric variability caused by pulsations is identified in 82 per cent of the sample, with dominant contributions from $β$ Cep and slowly pulsating B-type stars, as well as stochastic low-frequency variability. Based on a limited number of spectroscopic epochs, at least 14 per cent of the stars show evidence of binarity, including both eclipsing and spectroscopic systems. This work represents one of the largest homogeneous surveys of variability for intermediate-mass and massive stars in the Northern hemisphere, and complementing similar efforts in the Southern hemisphere. It provides a statistical framework for future studies of stellar structure and evolution, particularly in the context of asteroseismology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, including 7 of appendices, 15 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03383","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:36Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frequency-Decorrelated Temporal Ensembles for EEG--fNIRS Imagined-Handwriting Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T06:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:29:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T03:11:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:21:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imagined handwriting offers a temporally rich paradigm for non-invasive neural decoding, yet reliable recognition across unseen participants remains difficult because scalp EEG is noisy and internally generated stroke sequences vary across individuals. The Multimodal Brain-Computer Interface Grand Challenge provides synchronized EEG and fNIRS for four-class subject-independent handwriting-trajectory classification. We propose FRED, a task-adapted system that models imagined handwriting as a multi-second motor sequence and trains a compact multi-scale temporal network on three complementary EEG frequency views. With three seeds per view, cross-band members produce substantially less-correlated errors than same-band replicas, yielding a clean nine-member ensemble accuracy of 0.8076/0.7242/0.7492 on the public/private/overall test partitions without test-set adaptation or output constraints. The submitted pipeline further incorporates transductive pseudo-label training, three EEG-Conformer members, posterior aggregation, and a paradigm-aware decoder. Because every 12-trial randomization block contains three instances of each class, the final predictions are obtained by Hungarian assignment under the known block quota. On one fixed posterior pool, independent, session-constrained, and block-constrained decoding achieve 0.7600, 0.7758, and 0.7952 overall accuracy, respectively. The complete system reaches 0.8498/0.7718/0.7952, ranking fourth on the private split. A modality audit finds fNIRS-only decoding at chance (0.2511 overall), while adding fNIRS to EEG changes accuracy by only +0.0025. These results identify frequency-diverse temporal EEG modeling and protocol-matched structured inference as the principal sources of performance in this sparse-montage EEG--fNIRS setting. The source code is available at https://github.com/XiuFan719/EEG-fNIRS-fuse-method-for-MM-challenge."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:51Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-27T02:29:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aimin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Aimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the distribution of $ϕ(ψ(n))$ and $ψ(ψ(n))$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11A","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:40:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:58:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T09:14:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:37:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T22:24:45Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; The paper requires substantial revisions and updates. We are withdrawing it to prepare a significantly improved replacement version"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:12:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $ψ(n)$ and $ϕ(n)$ denote Dedekind's arithmetic function and Euler's totient function, respectively. We study the distribution of the compositions $ϕ(ψ(n))$ and $ψ(ψ(n))$. In particular, we obtain quantitative upper bounds for the exceptional set associated with $ϕ(ψ(n))$, thereby refining a density result of Sándor. We also prove that, for every fixed $c\u0026gt;0$, the set of integers $n\\leq x$ satisfying $ψ(ψ(n))\\leq cn$ has asymptotic density zero. Our method adapts sieve ideas used by Dixit and Bhattacharjee to compositions involving Dedekind's arithmetic function."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper requires substantial revisions and updates. We are withdrawing it to prepare a significantly improved replacement version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:20:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:20:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:44:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan M.","familyName":"Marin","name":"Marin, Juan M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Ermakov","name":"Ermakov, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Azendorf","name":"Azendorf, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Sandmann","name":"Sandmann, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Da Ros","name":"Da Ros, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darko","familyName":"Zibar","name":"Zibar, Darko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fully Distributed Fiber-Optic Sensing Enabled by Kalman Filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:43:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:55:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T09:27:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:44:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Signal fading creates points along the fiber where phase cannot be extracted, so they are conventionally discarded. Instead, we propose a Kalman-based solution for ϕ-OTDR full-fiber monitoring. Experiments demonstrate phase and temperature estimation with approximately 15 times better spatial density than if removing these points."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in 2026 European Conference on Optical Communications (ECOC)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:18:12Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:18:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:44:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Beskorovainyi","name":"Beskorovainyi, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A machine-readable catalogue of the Tsiolkovsky papers (fond 555, Archive of the Russian Academy of Sciences), and a way to measure how well its handwriting can be read"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.7; I.7.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:08:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:53:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T14:26:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T01:02:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The personal archive of Konstantin Tsiolkovsky (1857-1935) is held as fond 555 of the Archive of the Russian Academy of Sciences. The archive scanned the fond and published the images, but with no queryable catalogue, no full-text search and no dataset: the holdings can only be browsed one page at a time. This paper describes a machine-readable catalogue of all 2,019 files and 51,008 scans, a dating for 1,969 files taken from the archive's own descriptions, a page-level classification of every scan into handwriting and typescript, and a machine transcription of the fond in full: all 2,019 files and all 51,008 scans.\n It also reports a way to measure handwritten-text-recognition accuracy in an archive with no ground truth. Archives of the typewriter era often preserve one text twice, as manuscript and as a typed copy; transcribing both and comparing isolates the reading error, since source and pipeline are identical and only page difficulty differs. Across 1,759 such pairs from 224 files, two readings of a handwritten page agree on a median 37% of words and share a longest verbatim run of a median 10 words; the median is unchanged from the 294 pairs of the first version, on a sample six times larger. On two files that also have a published edition the estimate can be checked against ground truth: it is unbiased to within a percentage point and ranks pages as the truth does (rank correlation 0.92 where the edition is a faithful witness). This bounds use: two variants of one work here share 19% of words, below the rate at which two readings of a single page agree, so the redactions cannot be collated word by word at this quality. That negative result is reported as such, and the constraint is built into the tool."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 tables. v2: corpus completed (all 2,019 files, 51,008 scans); calibration enlarged from 294 to 1,759 pairs, median agreement unchanged; added validation against printed editions and a map of verbatim repeats within the fond. Dataset and code: https://github.com/beskvladimir-create/tsiolkovsky-papers ; archived at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21705221 (CC0 catalogue, MIT code)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:17:04Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:17:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:44:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faryar","familyName":"Jabbari","name":"Jabbari, Faryar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GriD-LMIA: A Gridding-Based Assembler for Solving Differentiable Parameter-Dependent Linear Matrix Inequalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Software (cs.MS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T06:07:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:29:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T04:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:26:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parameter-dependent linear matrix inequalities (PD-LMIs) require holding over a continuous domain. When the scheduling parameters vary with time, derivatives of parameter-dependent decisions may also enter the conditions. Since semidefinite programming solvers require finitely many constraints, we introduce GriD-LMIA, the Gridding-based Differentiable PD-LMI Assembler. It converts the conditions that need to hold on a continuous domain into finitely many sufficient LMIs in MATLAB. It first partitions the domain with a hyper-rectangular grid and represents known data and decisions on each cell with tensor Bernstein polynomials. The package exports direct Bernstein, Pólya-elevated, and sum-of-squares-based certificates through YALMIP. Examples are given to examine the balance among grid density, decision degree, and certificate choice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:49Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:44:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Motoyuki","familyName":"Nobori","name":"Nobori, Motoyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on some upper bounds for the Frobenius norm of the $q$-deformed commutator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"15A45, 15A60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:28:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:19:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For two square complex matrices $A,B,$ and a real number $q$, the $q$-deformed commutator of $A$ and $B$ is defined as $AB-qBA$. We give some upper bounds on the Frobenius norm of the $q$-deformed commutator in the case $q\\ge0$. All of the inequalities derived in this paper generalize the Böttcher-Wenzel inequality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:23:30Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:23:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viktória","familyName":"Klász","name":"Klász, Viktória","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Kleinau","name":"Kleinau, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rene","familyName":"Marczinzik","name":"Marczinzik, Rene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Judith","familyName":"Marquardt","name":"Marquardt, Judith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A new characterisation of Auslander-Gorenstein algebras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T14:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:45:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We give a new characterisation of Auslander-Gorenstein finite dimensional algebras by showing that they are exactly the finite dimensional algebras with a well-defined Auslander-Reiten bijection. This proves a conjecture of Marczinzik. We use this to give a new proof that a finite lattice with an Auslander-Gorenstein incidence algebra has to be distributive."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Addes two applications. In particular, we answer a question due to Iyama"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:34Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03719","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03719","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03719","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgy","familyName":"Lukyanov","name":"Lukyanov, Georgy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Effort without Evidence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:18:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:57:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T16:17:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:50:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advice can determine not only how past evidence is interpreted but whether new evidence will be produced. I study a sequence of decision makers who observe public success or failure but not one another's effort, and who pass costless causal assessments to their successors. Because a sender values success while underweighting her successor's effort cost, she has a reason to recommend more demanding actions. This motive can make encouragement uninformative. After an ambiguous failure, every equilibrium may induce inactivity, so experimentation and learning stop. When maximal effort makes outcomes insensitive to the underlying technology, the same force produces the opposite-looking trap: everyone works hard, but nobody tests whether less would suffice. Collapse is monotone in motivational bias, whereas moderate motivation preserves causal disagreement and the identifying experiment. In the threshold application, stronger motivation raises observed success while reducing long-run welfare. Motivation therefore selects costly actions, not informative experiments: a society may stop learning either because nobody tries or because everyone tries too hard."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03719","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:23Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03854","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03854","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03854","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Calibration Depends on the Scoring Rule: Quantized Biomedical LLM Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:57:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T02:34:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-23T04:03:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:46:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantized large language models enable on-premises processing of sensitive data, but their confidence estimates must be trustworthy. Reliability depends on implementation choices--prompt template, label wording, and scoring normalization--that are seldom treated as experimental variables. We evaluate three 7-billion-parameter Mistral variants (base, BioMistral, and instruction-tuned) at FP16, INT8, and INT4 on five-class sentence classification in medical abstracts. Two primary templates are evaluated on n=2,000 test sentences and two auxiliary templates on n=200 validation sentences. Because the primary templates were selected using 100 test-derived examples and a preliminary scorer later found to have a token-boundary error, results involving them are exploratory. Within this design, candidate-scoring normalization dominates apparent calibration: switching from summed to mean-token log-likelihood reverses which model appears better calibrated (BioMistral's mean calibration error nearly triples, while the instruction-tuned model's drops by more than half), yet accuracy changes by at most 1.4 percentage points for the two specialized models. Negative log-likelihood and Brier score confirm the reversal. Across the two primary templates, prompt choice changes mean accuracy by 2.9--17.8 percentage points, depending on the model. INT8 changes accuracy by at most 1.1 percentage points for the specialized models; INT4 effects are mixed but non-catastrophic. Post-hoc temperature scaling improves calibration under summed scoring but has not been validated under the alternative normalization that reverses the ranking. These exploratory results indicate that scoring normalization and prompt design should be first-order experimental decisions in calibration comparisons of decoder-based classifiers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03854","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnan Al","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Adnan Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathy","familyName":"Hämmerl","name":"Hämmerl, Kathy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jindřich","familyName":"Libovický","name":"Libovický, Jindřich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Fraser","name":"Fraser, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Predicting Multilingual Classification and Translation Performance of LLMs with Cross-Lingual Alignment -- Is English Enough?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:45:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T08:44:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:26:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual large language models (LLMs) have been shown to perform better on non-English classification tasks when the representations of the given language are more aligned to English within the model. Several cross-lingual alignment (CLA) scores have been proposed for use with LLMs, along with multiple approaches for extracting embeddings from the models. We provide a comparative analysis of 27 CLA score variants, examining how they differ and how well each predicts downstream performance across three tasks. Crucially, while LLMs are widely used for generative tasks such as machine translation, prior work has focused almost exclusively on classification. We therefore investigate whether CLA scores are similarly predictive of translation performance. To enable computing correlations across target languages, we propose a PMI-based translation metric, which is less dependent on the target language and correlates strongly with chrF. We find that CLA with English predicts translation quality comparably to or better than source-target CLA, providing new evidence that LLMs use English as an internal pivot language."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Segura","name":"Segura, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soichiro","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Soichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimized bounds for the product and the ratios of modified Bessel functions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"33C10, 26D07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:18:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:53:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T11:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:32:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"New sharp bounds for the product and the ratios of modified Bessel functions are presented. Most bounds for the product are derived as direct consequences of previously established bounds for the ratios of consecutive orders, except for the lower bound $I_ν(x)K_ν(x) \u0026gt; \\frac{1}{2}(x^2 + ν^2 + 1/5)^{-1/2}$, which had been conjectured for $x \u0026gt; 0$ and $ν\u0026gt; -1$, and is here proved for large $ν$ using Debye-type asymptotics. Moreover, very sharp bounds are obtained for the ratios (and consequently for the product) by asymptotically optimizing certain uniparametric inequalities. These optimized bounds are remarkably accurate: they remain extremely sharp for both small and large $x$ with fixed $ν$, and for large $ν$ with fixed $x$ or fixed $z = x/ν$. As a consequence, they provide precise upper and lower estimates across a wide range of parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"A proof of the inequality $I_\\nu(x)K_\\nu(x) \u0026gt; \\frac{1}{2}(x^2 + \\nu^2 + 1/5)^{-1/2}$ has been added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:17:26Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:17:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03442","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03442","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03442","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang-Xian","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bang-Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui-Chun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hui-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi-Ping","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xi-Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lipschitz regularity of harmonic map heat flows from $\\mathrm{RCD}$ spaces into $\\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Metric Geometry (math.MG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"53C43, 58E20, 31E05, 35K55, 49Q22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:37:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:44:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T06:35:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:17:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:50:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:10:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove positive-time regularity for harmonic map heat flows from finite-dimensional $\\mathrm{RCD}(K,N)$ spaces into complete $\\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ spaces, without assuming that either the source or the target is smooth. For bounded-image initial data, the $\\mathrm{EVI}$ gradient flow of the Dirichlet energy admits a representative that is locally Lipschitz jointly in space and time. Moreover, its spatial pointwise Lipschitz constant satisfies an Eells--Sampson-type parabolic Bochner inequality. The main difficulty is that the smooth parabolic perturbations used to select contact points are unavailable on an $\\mathrm{RCD}$ source. We overcome it by a sliced contact-selection principle that converts the elliptic ABP estimate on $\\mathrm{RCD}$ spaces into the space--time contact selection required by the Hamilton--Jacobi argument."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03442","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:57Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haidong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Fano indices of weighted projective spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:29:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:44:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T16:17:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:35:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Sylvester sequence is defined recursively by $s_1=2$ and $s_i=s_{1}\\cdots s_{i-1}+1$. In this paper, we prove that the Fano index of an $n$-dimensional well-formed weighted projective space with canonical singularities is bounded above by \\[ (s_n-1)(2s_n-3). \\] This gives an affirmative answer to a conjecture of Chengxi Wang for weighted projective spaces and $\\mathbb Q$-factorial toric Fano varieties with Picard number one.\n We also investigate the distribution of Fano indices among $4$-dimensional weighted projective spaces. As the distribution of Fano indices of weighted projective spaces coincides with that of indices of terminal Calabi--Yau varieties in dimension $n\\leq 3$, we expect this coincidence to persist also in dimension 4, and more generally, in all dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15pages, comments are welcome! v2: correct typos and add a link of data and code for Theorem 4.4"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:47Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Rousset","name":"Rousset, Hugo","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CRAL"],"givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Thiébaut","name":"Thiébaut, Eric","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Chloé","familyName":"Rey","name":"Rey, Chloé","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Manon","familyName":"Lizzana","name":"Lizzana, Manon","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Sébastien","familyName":"Soler","name":"Soler, Sébastien","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Fabrice","familyName":"Pancher","name":"Pancher, Fabrice","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Michelot","name":"Michelot, Julien","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Logi","familyName":"Olgeirsson","name":"Olgeirsson, Logi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Jean Baptiste Le","familyName":"Bouquin","name":"Bouquin, Jean Baptiste Le","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Alain","familyName":"Léger","name":"Léger, Alain","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["IPAG"],"givenName":"Fabien","familyName":"Malbet","name":"Malbet, Fabien","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pixel-Level Calibration for Space-Based High Precision Astrometry Using Young's Fringes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:05:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T13:37:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:40:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The detection of Earth-like exoplanets via astrometry necessitates centroiding precision at the 0.3 microarcsec level, thereby imposing stringent constraints on the focal plane geometry of space telescopes. The AGATE focal plane instrument, a proposed component of the NASA Habitable Worlds Observatory (HWO), aims to achieve this objective through the calibration of the intra-pixel response function of CMOS detectors to an accuracy of 50 micropixel (220 nm The present paper proposes a Young's fringes-based calibration method, derived from those employed by JPL and IPAG, for the purpose of mapping the pixel response barycenter offsets across the detector. Using a Pyxalis GIGAPYX-4600 CMOS sensor, we demonstrate via simulations and laboratory measurements that (1) a precision of 500 micropixel is achievable with 10,000 frames (current setup); (2) inter-pixel capacitance crosstalk and fringe hyperbolicity are dominating the errors at small and large scales, respectively; and, (3) an iterative inverse problem approach with a hyperbolic fringe model is proposed to overcome paraxial approximation limits for 1 Gpix focal planes. These results pave the way for on-board calibration of HWO's astrometric instrument, ensuring the sub-microarcsec precision required for exo-Earth detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:10:59Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:10:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03294","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03294","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03294","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunyeop","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sunyeop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Insung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Insung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Provably Learning Multi-Head Attention with Queries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T08:06:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:35:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T05:20:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:24:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T07:43:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:22:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the problem of learning multi-head softmax attention from black-box input-output access. The learner may query arbitrary real-valued token sequences and observe only the scalar output at the final token. Recent work gives an algorithm using $O(d^2)$ value queries to recover the single-head parameters $(W,v)$. For multiple heads, the same work establishes identifiability under the assumption that the heads occupy pairwise orthogonal subspaces. Applying the single-head recovery algorithm separately to the heads additionally requires bases for these subspaces to be known. We recover a canonical representation by merging heads with the same $W_h$, summing their corresponding $v_h$, and discarding a merged head when this sum is zero, without these subspace assumptions. By varying the number of copies of a token, our algorithm obtains samples of a rational function whose interpolation separates the canonical heads. Additional queries formed by adding selected token vectors then match the same head across different queries. When the oracle outputs and all subsequent computations are exact, the learner chooses its query vectors at random and recovers the canonical pairs $\\{(W_h,v_h):h\\in[H]\\}$ up to permutation with probability one. When $H$ is known, it uses exactly $4Hd^2-2H+1$ value queries of maximum length $2H+1$. If only a known upper bound $H_0$ is available, the algorithm uses $4H_0d^2-2H_0+1$ value queries of maximum length $2H_0+1$. For approximate oracle outputs, we give conditions under which the parameter error is at most a model- and query-dependent constant multiple of the output error. Finally, we extend our result to a one-layer Transformer with multi-head attention followed by a bias-free ReLU feed-forward network. Under additional conditions, we recover a functionally equivalent Transformer without relying on a separate algorithm for learning the feed-forward network."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03294","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:09:32Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:09:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03224","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03224","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03224","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aye Aye","familyName":"Maung","name":"Maung, Aye Aye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Treatment Effects using Group Testing with Retesting of Positive Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62D20, 62P10 (Primary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T06:57:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:31:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T04:27:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:18:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Group testing is an established, highly cost-effective strategy for population-level disease surveillance that identifies positive individuals by pooling biological specimens. Originally introduced during World War II for large-scale screening and heavily utilized in modern high-throughput public health infrastructure, traditional group testing methods are restricted to purely associational analyses. Consequently, they lack the capacity to infer the direct causal effect of an intervention when individual-level data are subject to baseline confounding. In this work, we bridge this fundamental gap by introducing a causal inference framework tailored specifically for group testing designs. We integrate the principles of inverse probability weighting (IPW) directly into a pooled pseudo-likelihood formulation to construct an unbiased pseudo-score function. Under standard regularity conditions, we prove the consistency and asymptotic normality of our proposed plug-in estimator. Extensive numerical simulations demonstrate that our framework successfully purges severe selection bias, accurately recovering the true average treatment effect where traditional unweighted pooling models fail. Finally, we illustrate the practical utility of our method as both an estimation and a diagnostic tool using real-world observational surveillance data from the CDC's U.S. Influenza Vaccine Effectiveness Network."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables. Includes supplementary appendix. To be submitted to Biometrics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03224","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:07:57Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:07:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhang","familyName":"Gongyue","name":"Gongyue, Zhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Yixuan","name":"Yixuan, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Zhiyong","name":"Zhiyong, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Donghan","name":"Donghan, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ren","familyName":"Weihong","name":"Weihong, Ren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Honghai","name":"Honghai, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Control Allocation in Neural Network Optimization: Joint Affine Control of Weight and Bias Updates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T01:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:16:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T09:01:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:26:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimization algorithms determine not only the magnitude of a neural-network update but also how that update is distributed across parameter channels. We study whether this distribution can be treated as a controllable quantity independently of global training progress. We define operational update allocation through normalized channel energies and analyze two scalar controls: a coordinate-preconditioning exponent and an affine spectral exponent that scales the bias column of an augmented weight--bias matrix. At a frozen state, a common nonzero step-size multiplier leaves normalized allocation unchanged; the coordinate exponent yields affine pairwise log-odds with an explicit inverse; and the affine exponent induces a rank-one positive-semidefinite Gram perturbation and a logistic raw-participation law. We further separate raw affine participation, spectral gain, and the decoded physical bias update, and show that finite polynomial spectral iterations preserve singular subspaces. Same-state replay verifies the exact control laws. On a five-seed controlled benchmark, intermediate controls improve held-out and worst-group metrics, whereas excessive affine control causes underfitting. A four-task single-seed transfer study provides descriptive corroboration. These results establish instantaneous allocation control and a bounded empirical operating regime, but do not imply a task-independent generalization ordering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:02:36Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:02:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Lange","name":"Lange, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI Alignment and Fiduciary Obligation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T14:18:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T07:45:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:24:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advanced AI assistants engage users in extended interactions across a widening range of roles, including advice, decision support, collaboration, learning, emotional support, and companionship among others. Current alignment efforts consider what alignment criteria should govern these relationships, drawing on moral traditions developed for human relationships such as bioethics, virtue ethics, care ethics, and relationship science. This paper considers AI alignment criteria in the user-AI-developer triad, since every user-AI interaction is mediated by a developer who exercises discretionary control over a system's behaviour, memory, and engagement parameters. Drawing on business ethics and legal scholarship, I argue that fiduciary theory applies to extended AI assistant deployment. On this basis, the four canonical fiduciary duties of loyalty, care, good faith, and candour can generate alignment criteria for the developer-user relationship. I map four user-side risks of extended AI assistant deployment to the four duties and specify institutional measures that follow from discharging each duty. The discussion complements existing approaches by grounding alignment criteria in obligations the developer owes the user, rather than in values the user-AI interaction should promote, and by showing that those obligations hold independently of any de facto harm to users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 1 table. Accepted at the 9th AAAI/ACM Conference on AI, Ethics, and Society (AIES 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:54:56Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:54:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02875","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02875","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02875","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregorio","familyName":"Baldi","name":"Baldi, Gregorio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeuk Hay Joshua","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Yeuk Hay Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Murphy's law in non-abelian Hodge theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T20:55:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:45:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct explicit examples of non-integral variations of $\\mathbb{Q}$-Hodge structures. Our approach leverages Fenchel--Nielsen-type parameterizations, due to Kabaya and Maskit, of the Teichmüller component of relative character varieties. Additionally, we discuss various Diophantine results concerning the $\\mathcal{O}_{K,S}$-integral points of such character varieties, and give a new proof of Beauville's classical theorem on families of elliptic curves. We conclude by collecting \\emph{pathological behaviors} of the Hodge locus of non-integral $\\mathbb{Q}$VHS, in particular the failure of the Cattani--Deligne--Kaplan theorem and the André--Oort conjecture; our results indicate that for $\\mathbb{Q}$VHS which are not $\\mathbb{Z}$VHS, what can go wrong must go wrong."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, comments very welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02875","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:59:52Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02657","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02657","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02657","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianshuo","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jianshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Maosen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Agentic LLMs Secretly Encode Indirect Prompt-Injection Exposure in Hidden States"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-01T12:00:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T05:25:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:17:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic LLMs are vulnerable to indirect prompt injection (IPI) attacks, e.g., malicious side-tasks hidden in external tool results. While many efforts have sought to address this threat, little is known about the internals of agentic LLMs when they are exposed to IPI attacks. For simplicity, we refer to this condition as IPI exposure. In this paper, we study IPI exposure from three perspectives. (1) Probing: Across eight models, including the 753B-parameter GLM-5.2 and the 2.8T-parameter Kimi-K3, simple linear probes trained on pre-generation hidden states can predict LLMs' IPI exposure. These probes achieve 0.90+ AUROC on unseen attacks, agent instructions, and task suites; they remain robustly predictive under adaptive attacks and in cross-lingual settings. (2) Defense: We reveal and diagnose a knowledge-action gap: post-trained LLMs encode signals predictive of IPI exposure, yet do not reliably bind these signals to safe agentic actions. We therefore introduce a probe-gated reasoning-based defense to bridge this gap at test time. On difficult AgentDojo settings, it substantially reduces attack success rate, e.g., from 34.6% to 0% on Qwen3.5-27B, and better preserves clean-task utility than the baselines. (3) Explanation: We introduce an analysis framework that identifies natural-language explanations strongly correlated with probe-captured signals. The resulting profiles differ across models: latent signals can align with either direct IPI-exposure sensing or indirect operational cues. Code is available at https://github.com/jianshuod/IPI-exposure-signal."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2. Compared to v1, we include the Kimi-K3 model's results and conduct a series of new experiments on understanding probe generalization"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02657","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:54:52Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:54:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T04:02:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tu","familyName":"Yongsiqi","name":"Yongsiqi, Tu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Haoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkui","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wenkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Junwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xiaoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanjun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Guanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SMOPD: Multi-Reward Reinforcement Learning via Specialize-and-Merge Online Policy Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T04:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:24:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-21T14:46:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:46:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We aim to improve model performance in multi-reward reinforcement learning training process. Existing Group reward-Decoupled Normalization Policy Optimization (GDPO) has mitigated the issue of reward signals masking one another during direct scalarization by normalizing each reward dimension separately before aggregation. However, our experiments show that GDPO still struggles to balance reward signals with different granularities. Specifically, in some particular training tasks, the model may receive a dense reward that assigns fine-grained scores ranging from 0.1 to 1.0, together with a sparse reward that provides only binary feedback of either 0 or 1. In such cases, we find that the sparse reward may provide an insufficient optimization signal, preventing its corresponding capability from being effectively reinforced. Therefore, how can we strengthen the optimization signal from the sparse reward without sacrificing the capability already learned from the fine-grained reward? To overcome this limitation, we propose Specialize-and-Merge Online Policy Distillation (SMOPD), a two-stage training method for multi-reward optimization. Stage1-Specialize: SMOPD first employs reward-priority configurations to train multiple reward-specialized teachers, allowing each reward to be learned under conditions where its signal can effectively drive optimization. Stage2-Merge: SMOPD then utilizes online policy distillation to combine the reward-specialized capabilities of these teachers into a single student policy, while maintaining balanced task-level optimization. To validate our method, we conduct experiments on two multi-reward settings: complementary rewards(tool-calling accuracy and format) and conflicting rewards (helpful and harmless rewards). Based on above settings, SMOPD outperforms GDPO across 1.5B, 3B and 7B backbones."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 5 figures, 12 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:04:53Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:04:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:30:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02701","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02701","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02701","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaroslav D.","familyName":"Krivenko-Emetov","name":"Krivenko-Emetov, Yaroslav D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleksii M.","familyName":"Lytvynenko","name":"Lytvynenko, Oleksii M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rank and Scale Tests of Shell-Motivated Fermion-Mass Ansatze with a Constrained Neutrino Extension"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T14:34:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T16:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:57:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T18:17:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:04:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-20T20:26:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:08:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study whether the observed hierarchy of quark and lepton masses can be described by a small set of simple relations between the three fermion generations. Using the nine charged-fermion Yukawa couplings at the common scale M_Z, we distinguish genuine mass relations from those that arise simply because a model has enough freedom to reproduce the data.\n A systematic scan of projector-based models identifies a particularly accurate relation between the first- and third-generation mass slopes. We then construct more restrictive models that also constrain the second generation and test them by leave-one-out reconstruction. Several six-parameter models predict all nine charged masses with errors of only a few percent; the best fixed models reduce the worst leave-one-out error to about 4% without adding continuous parameters.\n We also give a possible microscopic interpretation of the most successful integer relations. Two mass-independent combinations of Standard-Model quantum numbers reproduce the required charged-sector pattern, and simple twofold or threefold matching classes fix the relative coefficient before the masses are used. In a separate two-loop running test, a down-sector ratio close to 1/15 near 6 x 10^6 GeV evolves to a value close to 1/14 at M_Z.\n The same framework gives neutrino-mass sums near 0.059-0.061 eV for normal ordering, while one family also allows inverted ordering near 0.103 eV. These results do not prove preons or fermion compositeness, but provide concrete targets for future microscopic models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages, 8 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02701","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:55:52Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:55:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:30:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.04004","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.04004","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.04004","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shailendra Kumar","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Shailendra Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason R.","familyName":"Picardo","name":"Picardo, Jason R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Localizing polymers promotes the centre-mode elastic instability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:58:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:09:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T12:21:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:43:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T12:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:43:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The centre-mode elastic instability, prevalent in rectilinear flows of dilute polymer solutions, allows dynamic states to emerge even in the absence of inertia. Here, we show that this instability can be significantly enhanced when the base flow has a nonuniform spatial-distribution of polymers. In such a flow, the polymeric stress not only depends on the conformation tensor (determined here by the Oldroyd-B equation) but also varies proportionally with the polymer concentration field, which satisfies a scalar transport equation. Focusing on the inertialess Stokes limit, we first consider the simple setting of periodic Kolmogorov flow. A linear stability analysis shows that a nonuniform polymer concentration either promotes or suppresses the centre-mode instability, depending on whether the polymers are localised near or away from the maximum of the base-velocity profile. We then focus on a base flow with a distinct polymer-laden stream overlying the base-velocity maximum, and sandwiched between polymer-free streams. We find that the critical Weissenberg number (product of the elastic relaxation time and the typical strain-rate) at the onset of instability undergoes a sudden decrease as the total polymer loading is increased. An energy analysis attributes this destabilisation to elastic feedback forces that arise from concentration gradients near the interfaces between the polymer-laden and polymer-free streams. The insights from Kolmogorov flow are shown to carry over to channel flow, where localising polymers about the centreline strongly promotes the centre-mode instability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04004","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:25:57Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:25:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03525","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03525","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03525","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haote","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haote","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingchao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjian","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xingjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Lixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linye","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziran","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Ziran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingli","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Lingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijie","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Huijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"QianQian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, QianQian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dechen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dechen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaiyu","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Huaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengxin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shengxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ShaSha","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, ShaSha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhejia","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhejia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zimai","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zimai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinfan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahe","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jiahe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengjin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chengjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingting","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tingting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqun","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Liqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MinerU.Chem: A High-Precision System for Optical Chemical Structure and Reaction Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T12:09:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:48:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T02:35:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:19:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T09:17:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In organic chemistry papers and patents, molecular structures, reaction schemes, and experimental conditions are often presented as molecular structure depictions, reaction diagrams, and complex tables or figures. Such information is difficult for general-purpose document parsing systems to directly convert into machine-readable data. This limits data production for organic chemistry knowledge base construction and for AI for Chemistry tasks such as reaction prediction, retrosynthesis, condition recommendation, molecular property prediction, and drug molecule design. This report introduces MinerU-Chem, a document parsing system for organic chemistry literature integrated into the MinerU online platform. Built on top of MinerU's general document parsing pipeline, MinerU-Chem adds five chemistry-specific modules: chemistry relevance filtering, molecular structure detection, molecule identifier extraction, molecular structure recognition, and reaction scheme parsing. Together, these modules convert organic-chemistry-related image regions in documents into a Molecule Summary List and a Reaction Summary List. For molecular structure recognition, MinerU-Chem uses CARBON (Complex Atomic Representation and Bonding Object Notation) as its core representation. CARBON enables recognition results to preserve both the visual layout of the original image and complex chemical semantics, while supporting the export of standard downstream formats such as MolFile and SMILES. On the SMILES-evaluable subset of MolRecBench-Wild (N=2,392), MinerU-Chem's molecular structure recognition module achieves a SMILES exact-match accuracy of 93.02%, outperforming the best evaluated comparison system, GPT-5.6-Sol (74.87%), by 18.15 percentage points. The system has been integrated into the MinerU online platform and is available at https://mineru.net/OpenSourceTools/Extractor ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03525","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:14:58Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:14:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03503","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03503","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03503","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Osses","name":"Osses, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitor","familyName":"Fermiano","name":"Fermiano, Vitor","affiliation":[],"name