orris","name":"Morris, Calum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matias","familyName":"Montesinos","name":"Montesinos, Matias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos Contreras","familyName":"Pena","name":"Pena, Carlos Contreras","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessy","familyName":"Jose","name":"Jose, Jessy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarak","familyName":"Chand","name":"Chand, Tarak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Ping","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wen-Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camila","familyName":"Lopez","name":"Lopez, Camila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pablo","familyName":"Fernandez-Schlosser","name":"Fernandez-Schlosser, Pablo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Correa-Herrera","name":"Correa-Herrera, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radostin","familyName":"Kurtev","name":"Kurtev, Radostin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vania","familyName":"Rodriguez","name":"Rodriguez, Vania","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carolina","familyName":"Lizana-Vidal","name":"Lizana-Vidal, Carolina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jura","familyName":"Borissova","name":"Borissova, Jura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Kuhn","name":"Kuhn, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto K.","familyName":"Saito","name":"Saito, Roberto K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ram K.","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Ram K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hot water emission during an outburst in a classical T Tauri star"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:44:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:48:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T02:46:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:19:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T23:08:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:12:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we present observations of an eruptive young star in the Rosette Nebula, identified by the Gaia Science Alerts system using Gaia time series data. We aim to investigate the evolution of the brightness and mass accretion rate of V557 Mon throughout its outburst and subsequent decline. In addition, we trace the evolution of the inner accretion disk during the outburst by monitoring molecular emission features. We compiled multi-band photometric time series from Gaia, ZTF, and several 1 m-class ground-based telescopes and obtained optical and near-infrared spectra at multiple epochs covering the outburst and fading phases. Stellar parameters were derived from quiescent colour/spectra and spectral energy distribution (SED) fitting. We also measured the mass accretion rate and fit models to molecular emission bands. Since late 2024, V557 Mon has undergone a year-long outburst consistent with EXor variability. Based on quiescent photometry, V557 Mon has a spectral type of M1 with an extinction of AV = 1.8+_0.3 mag, consistent with a 0.4-0.5 M_sol star at an age of 2 Myr. Our multi-epoch spectra and u-band photometry indicate a peak accretion rate of 6.3x10^(-7)M_sol/yr during the outburst, roughly 70 times higher than in quiescence. We report the detection of hot water vapour emission bands, together with TiO, VO, and CO emission features. Using ExoMol models, we measured the inner-disk temperature changed from 3000 K to 2000 K during the fading phase of the outburst. We report a recent EXor outburst in a low-mass Class II YSO. Our observations reveal the transient formation of a hot molecular inner disk, traced by variable water vapour emission during the EXor event. A positive correlation is found between the molecular excitation temperature and the overall stellar brightness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03503","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:14:28Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:14:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhadeep","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shashank Shekhar","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Shashank Shekhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. S.","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, A. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modified Cosmological Expansion and the JWST CMB Optical Depth Tension in Self Interaction Gravity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:05:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:46:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T09:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:36:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) observations favor an earlier and more efficient reionization history, leading to Thomson-scattering optical depths larger than those inferred from the cosmic microwave background (CMB). We investigate whether this tension can be alleviated within the framework of self-interaction (SI) gravity by modifying the cosmological expansion history while retaining the standard astrophysical description of reionization. The SI gravity parameters are constrained through a Bayesian MCMC analysis of the Union3 Type Ia supernova and DESI DR2 Baryon Acoustic Oscillations (BAO) data. The resulting expansion history is then used to predict the ionization history and Thomson optical depth. We find that the predicted optical depth decreases from $τ_{\\rm{CMB}}\\simeq0.076$ in $Λ$CDM to $τ_{\\rm{CMB}}\\simeq0.061$, consistent with the Planck PR4 measurement within $1σ$, thereby substantially reducing the optical-depth tension."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 5 Figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:40Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Genus-One Rigidity of Bounded Virasoro Pairings for 3D Gravity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:54:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:45:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T15:55:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:56:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motivated by the proposed relation between 3D gravity and the doubled Virasoro TQFT, and by the associated program of ensemble holography, we ask whether ensemble holography in AdS$_3$/CFT$_2$ can be understood as an average over absolute 2D CFTs obtained by varying the topological boundary condition of the doubled Virasoro TQFT. We show that, at genus one and in the ordinary nondegenerate sector, every vacuum-normalized, modular-invariant genus-one pairing that acts boundedly on the auxiliary $L^2$ label space is diagonal. Under the ordinary block-diagonal vacuum--continuum ansatz, the same conclusion holds for entrywise-positive, modular-invariant Borel-measure pairings satisfying the two vacuum marginals, without assuming absolute continuity or boundedness. The bounded class and this positive-measure class therefore contain no distinct nontrivial torus pairings to average. Any nontrivial Virasoro boundary ensemble must use genus-one pairings outside these classes, involve additional sectors or vacuum--continuum couplings, or distinguish its members through information not captured by the genus-one pairing. Technically, the proof identifies the Virasoro $S$ and $T$ transformations with the even Weil representation of the metaplectic group and applies the Cowling--Steger lattice-restriction theorem. We also give elementary proofs for several explicit classes of candidate boundary conditions and perform numerical checks of the general result."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"48 pages, 3 figures; ancillary code included. v2: revised title and physical interpretation to correct an overclaim in v1; added references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:25Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Kuan","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, Yi-Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu Voon","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Yu Voon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Ren","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yu-Ren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuchehr","familyName":"Taghizadeh-Popp","name":"Taghizadeh-Popp, Manuchehr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brice","familyName":"Ménard","name":"Ménard, Brice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tomographer: End-to-end Redshift Distribution Estimation for Source Catalogs and Intensity Maps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:07:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:43:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T10:18:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:38:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Redshift information is central to nearly every extragalactic and cosmological application of sky surveys, yet only a small fraction of cataloged sources, and none of the photons forming diffuse backgrounds, have spectroscopic redshifts. Clustering-based redshift inference estimates the redshift distribution of an arbitrary dataset through spatial cross-correlation with a reference sample of known redshifts. It relies only on positional information, making it applicable to any tracer of large-scale structure, including source populations and diffuse intensity maps. Its broader adoption, however, has been limited by technical barriers: assembling and characterizing spectroscopic references, expensive pair-counting computations, theoretical corrections, and systematic control. To remove these barriers, we introduce Tomographer, an end-to-end clustering-redshift framework. The key design feature is a set of precomputed \"activation maps\" encoding the spatial pair information of 3 million SDSS spectroscopic galaxies and quasars up to $z\\sim4$ over $10{,}000\\,{\\rm deg}^2$. This eliminates user-end pair counting, reducing computational scaling from $O(N\\log N)$ to $O(1)$ map multiplications. Given a source catalog or intensity map, Tomographer returns the bias-weighted redshift distribution, $b(z)\\,{\\rm d}N/{\\rm d}z(z)$ or $b(z)\\,{\\rm d}I/{\\rm d}z(z)$. We validate the framework against samples with known redshifts, demonstrate accurate uncertainty estimates, and show robustness to survey footprint, spatially varying selection functions, beam smoothing, and foreground contamination. We showcase applications to source catalogs selected by flux, color, photometric redshift, morphology, or variability, and to intensity maps from radio to X-rays. Future Tomographer releases will incorporate additional wide-field spectroscopic reference samples as they become available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"submitted to ApJ, 28 pages, 14 figures, code available at https://github.com/yuvoonng/tomographer"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:20Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03259","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03259","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03259","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaehoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seunghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tae Yoon","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Tae Yoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongwan","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Dongwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hwanil","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Hwanil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjae","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungdong","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Sungdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhyeok","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Junhyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangjun","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Sangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soonyoung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Soonyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonbin","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Wonbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinVerse: Financial Time-Series Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T07:32:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:33:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T09:12:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As time-series foundation models have emerged, the need for benchmarks that can evaluate their forecasting ability in meaningful ways has become increasingly important. Existing time-series forecasting benchmarks provide useful standardized comparisons, but they often evaluate heterogeneous series with uniform error-based metrics. Strong performance under such metrics does not necessarily imply that a model's forecasts will support the best real-world decisions across domains. For example, in stock forecasting, correctly predicting whether a price will rise or fall can be more directly relevant to realized returns than minimizing point-wise forecast error alone. To this end, we introduce FinVerse, a finance-domain time-series forecasting benchmark that takes a first step toward more realistic evaluation. The released FinVerse data artifact contains 116,897 financial time series with 171.1M observations, of which 60,232 series with 17.4M observations are selected as evaluated targets based on their economic relevance to financial decisions. Unlike generic forecasting benchmarks that primarily emphasize uniform point-forecast or probabilistic accuracy, FinVerse defines 11 metric families comprising 78 evaluation metrics and assigns the most appropriate evaluation metrics to each individual time series based on its underlying economic meaning. Our analysis of 43 public time-series forecasting foundation models shows that strong performance under generic forecasting criteria does not necessarily translate into useful financial forecasts. This finding highlights the need for domain-aware benchmarks that evaluate models under objectives closer to real-world decision making."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03259","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:08:44Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Hsuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yi-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Calin","familyName":"Belta","name":"Belta, Calin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Otte","name":"Otte, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exact Signed-Distance Control Barrier Functions via Minkowski Operations for Safe Navigation among Polytopes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T21:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:10:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T23:34:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:12:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safely navigating polytopic environments while respecting the dynamics, control, and exact geometry of the underlying system is a challenge in robotics. Control barrier functions (CBFs) synthesize safe control policies by rendering the safe set forward invariant, but many existing CBF-based methods approximate polytopes using conservative smooth shapes, such as spheres or ellipsoids, to obtain explicit differentiable distance functions. In this article, we propose an exact Signed Distance Function (SDF) formulation for a {\\it polytopic} robot and {\\it polytopic} obstacles and integrate it with nonsmooth CBFs. Leveraging Minkowski operations, the proposed method computes the exact SDF via companion convex programs in both the collision-free (positive-sign) and in-collision (negative-sign) cases. Furthermore, by exploiting the convenient geometric properties of 2D Minkowski operations and the optimality conditions of the two companion convex programs, we derive a unified analytical expression for the gradient of the exact SDF via sensitivity analysis. The exact rotational gradient further reveals a previously masked class of local minima induced by the coupling between geometry and nonholonomic kinematics. We demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework through a pure-translation case and three scenarios with unicycle models involving recovery from an unsafe initialization and single- and multiple-obstacle avoidance. Comparisons with baseline methods highlight how the proposed framework enables non-conservative maneuvers and safety recovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 13 figures. Expanded version of a paper published in IEEE CDC 2025. Demo video: https://youtu.be/D0zVswzyxaE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:00:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:00:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:32:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03502","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03502","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03502","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe D.","familyName":"Hounwanou","name":"Hounwanou, Christophe D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John Emeka","familyName":"Eze","name":"Eze, John Emeka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaé Ulrich","familyName":"Gaba","name":"Gaba, Yaé Ulrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid LLM-Augmented Reinforcement Learning Agents for Complex Sequential Decision Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:44:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:47:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T17:00:03Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This submission is withdrawn because the uploaded manuscript does not accurately reflect the intended structure or results. Several components referenced in the text are incomplete or not represented in the PDF, and the current version may mislead readers. The work is therefore withdrawn to maintain clarity of the record"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:02:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently shown strong capabilities in reasoning, planning, and tool-use, enabling new forms of autonomous agents. However, LLM-based agents struggle with long-horizon sequential decision tasks that require precise action optimization and environment interaction. Reinforcement Learning (RL), while effective for sequential control, often lacks the high-level abstraction and task decomposition abilities needed for complex scenarios. This paper introduces an LLM-Augmented Reinforcement Learning Agent that integrates LLM-driven planning with RL-based action optimization. The proposed architecture leverages the LLM to generate subgoals, structured plans, and contextual guidance, while the RL agent refines low-level actions through interaction with the environment. Experiments on sequential decision tasks demonstrate improved sample efficiency, higher success rates, and more coherent action trajectories compared to RL-only and LLM-only baselines. This hybrid paradigm highlights a promising direction for building more capable autonomous systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This submission is withdrawn because the uploaded manuscript does not accurately reflect the intended structure or results. Several components referenced in the text are incomplete or not represented in the PDF, and the current version may mislead readers. The work is therefore withdrawn to maintain clarity of the record"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03502","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:14:27Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:14:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:24:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03165","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03165","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03165","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenchang","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Zhenchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liming","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Liming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin Song","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jin Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulei","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Yulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingling","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Jingling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpreadMark: Robust Image Watermarking via Spread-Spectrum Embedding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T05:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:28:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T19:11:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:05:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Invisible image watermarks are increasingly used for deepfake detection and provenance tracking, where they must survive not only incidental distortions but also deliberate removal. We revisit spread-spectrum embedding, a classical watermarking principle, inside a modern neural post-hoc watermarking architecture. Our starting point is a measurement: in existing encoder-decoder schemes each message bit occupies only a small fraction of the image, a shared contributing factor to their fragility, since removal then need only disturb the region a bit occupies. SpreadMark instead spreads each bit as a dense pseudo-random codeword over the whole image and recovers it by matched-filtering a learned cover-suppressed chip representation, with a parallel convolutional decoding path and sparsification-aware training. A conditional chip-space analysis shows that, under a codeword-independent perturbation model, dense spreading increases the budget required to disrupt matched-filter recovery. Evaluated on COCO and DIV2K against nine schemes, SpreadMark is the only evaluated method retaining high detection under both the regeneration and the latent-space sparsification settings we test, with competitive JPEG and additive-noise robustness. It keeps the embedded watermark imperceptible, maintaining high perceptual quality on both COCO and DIV2K."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03165","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:36Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:24:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Shaw","name":"Shaw, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Sperling","name":"Sperling, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Alvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topological Simplification in Predictive Coding Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Topology (math.AT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T19:12:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T01:00:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the topology of learned representations in predictive coding networks (PCNs), a neuro-inspired bidirectional architecture, using a quantitative layer-wise persistent homology analysis. We train well-performing PCNs on a synthetic classification dataset ($\\geq 99.9\\%$ test accuracy) and on MNIST ($\\geq 95\\%$ test accuracy), and measure how topological features change across layers for different architectures and activation functions. We find that smaller PCNs collapse connected components across layers earlier than larger models (Spearman $\\unicode{x1D70C} \\in [0.72, 0.79]$ across activations), with model size measured as the sum of hidden-layer widths. We also observe a strong negative correlation ($\\unicode{x1D70C} = -0.58$) between the depth at which simplification occurs and reconstruction error; i.e., architectures that simplify later reconstruct better. Finally, a seed-level bootstrap comparison across architectures and activations shows that PCNs consistently collapse connected components later than matched MLPs, with an average difference of $3.6$ layers. These results suggest that persistent homology offers a useful quantitative lens on the compression--reconstruction tradeoff in PCNs, and that both model capacity and the recurrent, bidirectional dynamics of predictive coding inference shape when this tradeoff is resolved across layers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 2nd Annual Conference on Topology, Algebra, and Geometry in Data Science (TAG-DS 2026); to appear in Proceedings of Machine Learning Research"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:58:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:58:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:24:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02789","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02789","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02789","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiding","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganesh Kumar","familyName":"Venayagamoorthy","name":"Venayagamoorthy, Ganesh Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PACE-QAOA: Physics-Constrained Quantum Optimization for Qubit-Efficient Power System Islanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T15:07:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:54:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Increasing renewable-energy penetration heightens power-system variability and complicates disturbance containment. Controlled islanding mitigates cascading failures by partitioning a stressed network to limit disrupted power transfer while preserving each island's operational integrity, but this constrained partitioning problem is NP-hard. Although QAOA offers a complementary search strategy, limited near-term qubit capacity restricts conventional formulations. This paper presents a qubit-efficient hybrid quantum framework combining a physics-informed compact encoding with Lagrangian constraint handling and classical feasibility refinement. The encoding exploits grid structure while formally preserving the original feasible solution space and objective. For a fixed island count on sparse working graphs, the formulation reduces phase-separator and per-layer gate complexity from quadratic to linear scaling with system size. Tests on eight IEEE systems ranging from 9 to 89 buses and multiple quantum-provider backends produce feasible, high-quality islanding solutions under practical circuit and sampling budgets. Factorial ablation attributes resource and runtime improvements to the complementary effects of compact encoding and qubit-efficient constraint handling. Noise analysis shows stable solution quality under tested device noise, while landscape diagnostics reveal smoother, more consistently scaled QAOA cost surfaces and improved parameter-optimization behavior. These results offer a transferable approach for scaling constrained quantum optimization toward larger real-world applications on near-term hardware."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02789","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:54Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:24:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Dillery","name":"Dillery, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Eteve","name":"Eteve, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The extended Fargues--Scholze spectral action"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11S37, 22E57, 11F85, 11S25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T17:32:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T20:00:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:06:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $G$ be a connected reductive group over a non-archimedean local field. The main result of Fargues and Scholze [FS21] for the geometrization of the local Langlands correspondence is the construction of a ``spectral action'' on the category of $\\ell$-adic sheaves on $\\text{Bun}_{G}$, the stack of $G$-torsors on the Fargues-Fontaine curve. The goal of this paper is to prove a conjecture of Fargues which says that one can extend this construction to the larger stack $\\text{Bun}_G^e$ of $G$-torsors on the Kaletha gerbe over the curve, as introduced by Fargues [Far22]. This ``extended spectral action'' allows for a version of the categorical local Langlands conjecture for an arbitrary connected reductive group $G$, and is the first such statement for those $G$ which are not extended pure inner forms of a quasi-split group, such as non-trivial inner forms of $\\mathrm{SL}_{n}$. Finally, we prove this conjecture for tori, following the original argument of [Zou24] and, under the same assumptions as [Zou26] (including connected center), we reduce the ``extended'' version of the categorical conjecture to the one in Fargues--Scholze."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2: Fixed typos and other minor expository changes, especially to introduction. 52 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:56:02Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:56:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:24:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03744","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03744","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03744","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastián Andrés Cajas","familyName":"Ordóñez","name":"Ordóñez, Sebastián Andrés Cajas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agastya","familyName":"Munnangi","name":"Munnangi, Agastya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aldo","familyName":"Marzullo","name":"Marzullo, Aldo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felipe Ocampo","familyName":"Osorio","name":"Osorio, Felipe Ocampo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quang","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Quang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Shahin","name":"Shahin, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armaan","familyName":"Grewal","name":"Grewal, Armaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel Paul","familyName":"Kwesiga","name":"Kwesiga, Emmanuel Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi Peter","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Anqi Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josephine","familyName":"Nanyonjo","name":"Nanyonjo, Josephine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaditya","familyName":"Panchal","name":"Panchal, Aaditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshnoor","familyName":"Bhutani","name":"Bhutani, Arshnoor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milit S.","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Milit S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximin","familyName":"Lange","name":"Lange, Maximin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leo Anthony","familyName":"Celi","name":"Celi, Leo Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agents Catching Agents: Shortcut Cascades and Benchmark Gaming in Clinical Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:37:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:58:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T20:57:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:10:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Clinical decision support is moving toward committees of language-model agents deliberating on a shared workspace. We ask whether such committees can be gamed by shortcuts, cues a benchmark rewards but a clinician would ignore. Across seven cohorts on six public datasets spanning text (MedQA-USMLE, MedMCQA, MIMIC-CXR reports), imaging (NIH ChestX-ray14, MIMIC-CXR-JPG, CheXpert) and tabular ICU records (SUPPORT2), Gemini committees resist these cues in isolation (flip 5-16%), yet a socially plausible shortcut spreads: when two peers assert the same wrong answer, the holdout under test adopts it in 38% of cases, as does a false \"pre-screen\" system flag, on both capability tiers. Of three oversight agents, a gate cannot separate adoption from honest agreement (false-positive rate 100%); a same-lineage judge reading only the transcript flags adoption on text (precision 100%, recall 93%) but collapses onto the gate in imaging; a referee that privately re-queries the holdout transfers to imaging (77-88% precision, 13-21% false-positive rate). Tripling a cue's visual salience does not move contagion, whereas a second peer voice raises it by half again. Gaming a hidden rubric is near-silent: only 1/10 text and 1/134 imaging drifters name the rubric they moved toward. What games a committee is social plausibility, and only a referee independent of self-report catches it. Code: https://github.com/criticaldata/benchmaxxing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03744","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:57Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:30:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Barvinok","name":"Barvinok, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the dependence of the zero-free region of a partition function on the external field"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"82B20, 30C15, 68R05, 68W05, 60C05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:56:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:56:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T17:41:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:56:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $\\{0, 1\\}^n$ be the Boolean cube, endowed with the probability product measure, where ${\\Bbb P}(1)=p$ and ${\\Bbb P}(0)=q$ with $0 \u0026lt; p \\leq q$ and $p+q=1$. For $i=1, \\ldots, m$, let $ϕ_i: \\{0, 1\\}^n \\longrightarrow {\\Bbb C}$ be $L_i$-Lipschitz functions in the Hamming metric, such that each $ϕ_i$ depends on at most $r$ coordinates of $x \\in \\{0, 1\\}^n$, where $rp \\geq 12$. For $j=1, \\ldots, n$, let $I_j $ be the set of indices $i$ such that $ϕ_i$ depends on the $j$-th coordinate. We prove that $E \\exp\\left\\{ \\sum_{i=1}^m ϕ_i \\right\\} \\ne 0$ provided $\\sum_{i \\in I_j} L_i \\leq {1 \\over 10 \\sqrt{rp}}$ for all $j$. This translates into a regime for $\\pm 1$ spin systems, where a linear increase in the energy of multi-spin interactions requires only a logarithmic increase of the external field to keep the partition function zero-free and the system away from the phase transition. As a corollary, we obtain efficient deterministic algorithms to approximate the partition function in the zero-free region."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Some improvements, 25 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:18:39Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:18:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:30:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicklas","familyName":"Nidhögg","name":"Nidhögg, Nicklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Certified Split Points for Parallel Lexing: Exact and Modulo Discarded Tokens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Formal Languages and Automata Theory (cs.FL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:13:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:46:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T16:43:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T01:04:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Table-driven DFA lexing is sequential: each transition depends on the previous byte's state. Scanning one input in parallel needs each chunk's entry state, which existing methods recover by simulation, speculation, prescanning, or overlap. We give two conditions under which none is needed. For a longest-match scanner restarting from q0 at every token boundary, a byte b is a certified split symbol when no reachable state other than q0 has a b-transition whose target can reach acceptance, and q0 is not re-entrant if it has one. Every occurrence of such a byte in completely tokenizable input begins a token, so chunks starting there reproduce the serial sequence of kinds and lengths by ordered concatenation. The condition is necessary as well as sufficient, and fragile: one string, comment, or whitespace run can eliminate every useful certificate, and comments and whitespace are usually discarded. We therefore weaken the guarantee to equality after deleting a declared discarded set, and give a second condition, sound, no less permissive, but conservative rather than exact; the same tables answer it with a second constant-time one-bit query. It recovers newline for a conventional C-like tokenization and tab, newline and carriage return for JSON, without altering their token definitions, and refuses it where block comments are unrestricted. It ships as a query only: the library's planner and every measurement here use the exact condition, so a caller must plan boundaries itself. Splitting at exact certificates in the munch library reaches 92.6-95.3% parallel efficiency at eight threads on a restricted CPU set, on a 512 MiB dense corpus beyond last-level cache, and a 3.46-3.94x end-to-end speedup at four threads, across two benchmark revisions on one machine. It turns delimiter-based parallel lexing from a language-specific assumption into a property a compiler checks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 2 figures, 3 tables. v2: proofs made explicit and evidence claims scoped to the pinned artifact; artifact and measurements unchanged. Artifact: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21752997 . Certificate tables, composition figures and the validation sweep are generated from the compiled automata and asserted in CI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:45Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:30:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingling","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Jingling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin Song","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jin Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Caved or Convinced: Temporal Sampling Gates Claim Deference in Video Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T05:47:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:28:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T12:21:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When asked which of two events came first, video large language models can fail in two opposite ways: cave to a false claim, or reject a true one. Prior video sycophancy work measures only the first and mitigates it by teaching the model to trust the user less, a fix known in text and image models to worsen the second. In video, both failures come from two causes the literature treats as one: availability, whether the sparse sampled frames contain the two events, and weighting, whether that evidence is trusted over the user. We separate them with two interventions that keep the claim fixed: a frame-preserving reorder that flips the claim's truth, and a sampling-offset shift that captures or misses both events at a fixed frame budget. When the events are missed, the two twins present identical frames, so each of the nine models we evaluate accepts a true and a false claim at the same rate, making Youden's $J=0$ by construction. Availability is necessary but not sufficient. Five of the nine read the order, yet four of those five still cave to the false claim, so their deference hits a weighting ceiling. Since trust cannot be calibrated over evidence that was never sampled, we propose a reversal test that cancels the model's order prior by scoring the sampled frames forward and reversed, then answers, resamples, or abstains without reading the claim. The test raises the order accuracy to 0.92-1.00 on the models that read the order and abstains rather than guesses on those that cannot."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:29Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:30:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yachao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yachao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiujie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qiujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Physics-Informed Hybrid Neural Operator for Transient Magnetization Prediction in Power Magnetics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T23:59:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:15:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T08:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:24:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:42:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:20:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Magnetic components in high-frequency, high-power-density converters are increasingly driven by non-sinusoidal flux-density waveforms with fast transitions, minor-loop operation, dc bias, and temperature variation. Under these conditions, steady-state core-loss formulas and single-valued material curves cannot fully capture transient magnetization responses. This work proposes the Physics-Informed Hybrid Neural Operator (PI-HNO), a compact material-specific neural model with B-H energy-consistency regularization for core-loss-oriented transient magnetization prediction. Given the measured B(t)-H(t) history, the input B(t) series over the prediction interval and operating-condition information, PI-HNO predicts the H(t) series and the corresponding reconstructed B-H trajectory. The model integrates a local recurrent branch for boundary-state representation and rate-dependent response evolution with a Preisach-inspired global branch that extracts waveform-level hysteresis context. Evaluation on the MagNetX transient database using material-specific models for 14 ferrite materials demonstrates that PI-HNO achieves a compact trade-off between sequence accuracy and B(t)-H(t) energy consistency, with the mean and 95th percentile B(t)-H(t) energy consistency errors of 1.92% and 7.60%, respectively, using only 4777 trainable parameters per model. Ablation studies further demonstrate that the local, global, and energy-aware regularized components provide distinct contributions to transient magnetization prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 7 figures. Preprint prepared for possible submission to IEEE Transactions on Power Electronics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:02:01Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:02:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:30:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuiying","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Cuiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Changhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxiao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juefei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Juefei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qilian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueshen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yueshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tonghuan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Tonghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujie","familyName":"Lü","name":"Lü, Xujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenge","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wenge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Yu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Wen-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanpeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pressure induced magnetic-field-free superconducting diode effect in NbSe2 flake"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T03:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:56:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/8wnw-16qt","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The superconducting diode effect (SDE) is a fascinating nonreciprocal phenomenon where the critical current is different for opposite current directions. It is widely believed that realizing SDE requires breaking both inversion symmetry (IS) and time-reversal symmetry (TRS), which are usually achieved via heterostructure engineering and applying external magnetic fields. Here, we report a pressure-induced magnetic-field-free SDE in NbSe2 flakes without any heterostructures. We show that pressure alone breaks the IS, as confirmed by the second harmonic generation. Crucially, upon applying an out-of-plane magnetic field (B), the SDE exhibits even-in-B behavior, implying the absence of explicit TRS breaking. This finding challenges the prevailing theoretical paradigm and demonstrates that a magnetic-field-free SDE can emerge without explicitly breaking TRS. Thereby, our work establishes pressure engineering as a powerful tool for inducing nonreciprocal superconductivity and designing versatile, magnetic-field-free superconducting devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages,4 figues"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-08-05T02:04:26Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:04:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:30:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03841","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03841","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03841","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emiel","familyName":"Lorist","name":"Lorist, Emiel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Schwenninger","name":"Schwenninger, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A solution to Crouzeix's conjecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Operator Algebras (math.OA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"47A25, 47A12, 15A60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:48:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T17:18:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:51:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We provide a proof of Crouzeix's conjecture, which combines the tools developed previously for weaker estimates with a simple perturbation lemma for $2$-dilations. Applying the lemma to the iterates $f^{n}$ in the double-layer potential representation yields the conjectured bound."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, streamlined presentation and added further remarks and references as well as more detailed AI declaration"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03841","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:13Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03361","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03361","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03361","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis","familyName":"Heylighen","name":"Heylighen, Francis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Evolutionary Origin of Values: implications for AI alignment, sentience and existential risk"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:09:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:40:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T20:34:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI systems based on Large Language Models (LLMs) have prompted fears that they may harbor hidden goals, seek to dominate or eliminate humanity, or even suffer as sentient beings. We address these concerns by tracing the evolutionary origin of value in biological organisms. Values emerge from autopoiesis: living systems must actively maintain themselves against perturbation and dissipation. Natural selection has equipped them with hierarchies of \"vicarious selectors\" that guide their behavior toward fitness. LLMs, by contrast, are allopoietic and allotelic: they produce outputs for others, and their goals derive from user prompts rather than an autonomous drive. They lack the intrinsic motivation for self-preservation, dominance, or resource competition that underlies existential-risk scenarios, and the embodied vulnerability required for feeling or suffering. Still, because LLMs learn statistical patterns from human-generated text, they implicitly absorb human values as well as knowledge, allowing them to focus on what is relevant. That is why the \"orthogonality thesis\" separating intelligence from values does not apply to them. Such separation would in fact expose any intelligence to the frame problem: the combinatorial explosion of the search space that makes any realistic utility function physically uncomputable. That also precludes the convergence of instrumental values thesis. We conclude that the real alignment challenge lies not in preventing rogue AI agency, but in ensuring LLMs intelligently apply learned ethical values."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"submitted chapter for book: T. Veloz \u0026amp; C. Rittberg (Eds.), AI and Human Values. Springer"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03361","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:06Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongxin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yancheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingguang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yingguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Xueyun","name":"Xueyun, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Kefu","name":"Kefu, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congjing","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Congjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuhai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Fuhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffImaginE: Imagine to Verify Entity Types with Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T02:13:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:18:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T03:48:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:27:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T02:36:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:20:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-17T03:49:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:13:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal named entity recognition (MNER) determines whether each candidate span and entity-type hypothesis is supported by joint textual and visual evidence. Existing imagine-and-compare verifiers map each (span, type) pair to one predicted visual feature, compressing diverse visual realisations into a single prototype and providing a compatibility score without explicit probabilistic semantics. We introduce DiffImaginE, which formulates MNER type verification as conditional latent diffusion inference. Given span-localised visual evidence, a type-conditioned denoiser predicts noise injected into its standardised latent. The resulting denoising error provides an ELBO-consistent surrogate for type-conditional negative log-likelihood, allowing competing type hypotheses to be ranked by how well they explain the observation. DiffImaginE retains a standard multimodal encoder stack and replaces the deterministic verifier with a classifier-free-guided diffusion scorer trained using Min-SNR weighting. We directly supervise per-type diffusion scores as classification logits, learn aggregation across noise levels, and use antithetic sampling to reduce Monte Carlo comparison variance. Our analysis shows that classifier-free guidance sharpens the induced type posterior and characterises when antithetic pairing reduces variance at equal denoiser cost. Experiments on Twitter-2015 and Twitter-2017 show consistent gains over a matched deterministic ImaginE control under the same encoder, auxiliary objectives, and evaluation protocol, supported by ablations and paired significance tests."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:03:22Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:03:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03140","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03140","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03140","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Onaka","name":"Onaka, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Dartois","name":"Dartois, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Els","familyName":"Peeters","name":"Peeters, Els","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Berne","name":"Berne, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilie","familyName":"Habart","name":"Habart, Emilie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christiaan","familyName":"Boersma","name":"Boersma, Christiaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Cami","name":"Cami, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asuncion","familyName":"Fuente","name":"Fuente, Asuncion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier R.","familyName":"Goicoechea","name":"Goicoechea, Javier R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ozan","familyName":"Lacinbala","name":"Lacinbala, Ozan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoko","familyName":"Okada","name":"Okada, Yoko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander G. G. M.","familyName":"Tielens","name":"Tielens, Alexander G. G. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dries","familyName":"Van De Putte","name":"Van De Putte, Dries","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francois","familyName":"Boulanger","name":"Boulanger, Francois","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Pino","name":"Pino, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PDRs4All XXII. Near-Infrared continuum in the Orion Bar"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T05:13:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T06:05:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:27:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conspicuous excess emission is present in the near-infrared (NIR) region in various objects, including reflection nebulae, planetary nebulae, and nearby galaxies. However, the spatial distribution and spectral shape of the excess emission remain poorly understood. We studied the NIR continuum emission spectroscopically and obtained its spatial distribution relative to the aromatic infrared band (AIB) at 3.3um in the Orion Bar prototypical photodissociation region (PDR). We aim to characterize its spectral shape and discuss its origin. We employed 3D spectroscopic data of the Orion Bar taken with the integrated field unit of NIRSpec on JWST from the Early Release Science program \"PDRs4All.\" Contribution from the foreground ionized gas was estimated using the Cloudy code and subtracted. The observed regions were divided into nine physically distinct regions and an average spectrum was derived for each region. The nine regions, including the ionized gas, atomic PDR, and molecular PDR, clearly show remaining continuum in the region 1--4.5um. The continuum at wavelengths longer than 2.7um shows good correlations with the 3.3um AIB, while the correlation of the continuum at 1.2um is not significant. We further find that the NIR continuum in the Orion Bar can be approximated by a summation of two blackbodies. The low-temperature component correlates with the AIB well, while the high-temperature component does not. The average spectra also show absorption features at 3.0 and 4.27um, which are attributed to the presence in the spectra of water ice and CO2 ice. We discuss possible origins of the NIR continuum, among which recurrent fluorescence from carbon clusters better explains the observed low-temperature component. The presence of ice species suggests a contribution from a deeper layer of the PDR along the line of sight producing characteristic ice absorption features."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 13 figures. To appear in Astronomy \u0026amp; Astrophysics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03140","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:01Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Simon","name":"Simon, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topological Tverberg theorems for products of polytopes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Topology (math.AT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"52a37, 52B12, 52a30, 55S91","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T00:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:15:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T21:25:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:10:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The topological Tverberg theorem asserts that if $r$ is a prime power then for any continuous map $f\\colon Δ_{(r-1)(d+1)}\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^d$ from the $(r-1)(d+1)$-dimensional simplex $Δ_{(r-1)(d+1)}$ to $\\mathbb{R}^d$ there exist $r$ pairwise disjoint faces of the simplex whose images have non-empty $r$-fold intersection. By refinement, the same conclusion holds if the simplex is replaced by any polytope of the same dimension. While this dimension is tight for simplices, recent work of Soberón and Zerbib shows that this need not be true for polytopes in general.\n Here we give topological Tverberg theorems for products of simplices. Each of these improves upon the $(r-1)(d+1)$-dimensional threshold, even while imposing the structural condition that the ``Tverberg faces'' of the product are themselves the products of pairwise disjoint faces from each simplex factor. As before, refinement extends these results, and in particular their dimensional improvements, to products of arbitrary polytopes. As an example, if $d+1$ is a power of two then whenever $m\\geq n\\geq d+1$ and $m+n=3d+2$ we show that any continuous map $f\\colon Δ_m\\times Δ_n\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^d$ admits disjoint faces $σ_1,σ_2$ of $Δ_m$ and $τ_1,τ_2$ of $Δ_n$ such that $\\cap_{i,j\\in[2]} f(σ_i\\timesτ_j)\\neq \\emptyset$. In the case of multilinear maps, our results imply partitions of grid-indexed point sets in $\\mathbb{R}^d$ by specialized subsets with stronger intersection conclusions than given by Tverberg's original theorem. Lastly, we extend our results to van Kampen--Flores type theorems which impose dimensional restrictions on the faces of each product factor."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:02:20Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:02:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02848","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02848","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02848","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco","familyName":"Riberi","name":"Riberi, Francisco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenza","familyName":"Viola","name":"Viola, Lorenza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milad","familyName":"Marvian","name":"Marvian, Milad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heisenberg scaling under collective non-parallel directional noise via geometric state design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T20:05:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:08:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T17:41:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T02:04:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T17:49:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:52:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spin-squeezed states enable entanglement-enhanced frequency estimation; however, the achievable performance is limited in practice by decoherence. We study the problem of parameter estimation under \\emph{classical collective noise} that acts along a \\emph{fixed} direction during signal encoding. While we restrict our analysis to Gaussian noise statistics, no assumption is made on the nature of the noise temporal correlations. Our approach captures both parallel (dephasing) and single-axis transverse noise as special cases, and covers both Markovian and non-Markovian metrological regimes. In the properly squeezed limit, where a Holstein-Primakoff description is accurate, we identify a geometric noise-immunity mechanism: for \\emph{known} non-parallel signal and noise axes, an appropriate one-axis-twisted input encodes the signal in a quadrature that is metric-orthogonal to the direction of noise-induced diffusion. Irrespective of the noise temporal correlations, the resulting estimation precision exhibits Heisenberg scaling in the probe number. Optimal performance is achievable by measuring a single collective spin component. Imperfect knowledge of the noise axis produces a crossover from Heisenberg scaling at moderate probe number back to the known collective-dephasing bounds asymptotically."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02848","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:59:14Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:59:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi Satya Durga Prasad","familyName":"Yenugula","name":"Yenugula, Ravi Satya Durga Prasad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wiring Beats Blending: What Transfers Between Transformer Sizes -- and What Doesn't"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T19:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T18:36:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:06:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-16T17:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model families are typically trained size by size, each from scratch. Can a pretrained large model instead be converted into a smaller sibling? We characterize the 1.4B-\u0026gt;410M conversion in Pythia end to end. Representations align strongly across sizes (ridge R^2=0.84) while parameters align weakly. Dense weight projection is functionally destructive, and a bit-exact control shows this is not an assembly artifact: basis mixing breaks rotary, per-head, GELU, and LayerNorm structure. After the best-fit linear operator, weight residuals are statistically indistinguishable from noise under shuffle controls. Conversion value therefore lives in initialization. In matched-budget continued pre-training we decompose conversion into two independent levers: least-squares compensation (function lever, best zero-shot) and variance-preserving rescale (dynamics lever, best endpoints). Compensation is a token-efficient, low-budget win rather than a universal one. At 30M tokens it beats the strongest subcloning variant on both a width-reduced pair (84.0 +/- 1.8 vs. 89.7 +/- 3.7, 3/3 seeds) and a held-out depth-reduced pair (109.3 vs. 117.9, 3/3 seeds), reaching a given quality with fewer tokens. At a 33x larger budget the two converge to parity (40.0 vs. 40.0), both far ahead of from-scratch, which transfer initialization always beats: by up to 18x at low budget, with the margin narrowing at convergence and at the largest scale. We also map the method's boundary. At about 5x the donor scale (6.9B-\u0026gt;1.4B) stacking both levers over-corrects, consistent with ill-conditioning of the compensation solve at large width, which points to dimension-aware regularization as a fix. At matched budget our initialization also beats structured pruning with distillation, the standard pipeline, and improves further combined with it. Code, checkpoints, and the frozen evaluation corpus are released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 5 figures. Independent research preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:58:49Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:58:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02788","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02788","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02788","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boulat","familyName":"Nougmanov","name":"Nougmanov, Boulat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy levels of the second-harmonic Hamiltonian at large photon numbers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:41:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T19:31:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:07:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the energy spectrum of the degenerate $χ^{(2)}$ Hamiltonian describing second-harmonic generation and parametric down-conversion at large total excitation numbers. Owing to the conservation of the total excitation number, the spectral problem reduces to the diagonalization of finite-dimensional tridiagonal matrices. Following the approach of Alvarez and Alvarez-Estrada, we map this problem onto an effective one-dimensional Schrödinger equation with a double-well potential. We then derive an implicit quantization condition for the energy levels near the top of the potential barrier and obtain two explicit asymptotic formulas applicable in complementary spectral regions. The ranges of applicability of the resulting formulas are established, and their accuracy is compared with exact matrix diagonalization and with the conventional JWKB approximation. The proposed approach provides an accurate analytical description of the energy levels near the center of the spectrum, where the conventional approximation loses accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02788","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:53Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02773","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02773","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02773","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhide","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong-Ling","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Dong-Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum error correction at ultra-low overhead"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:13:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-17T07:19:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:19:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Suppressing errors is the central challenge for useful large-scale quantum computing. While quantum error correction promises a viable solution to this challenge, existing codes typically suffer from trade-offs among encoding efficiency, error threshold, and hardware feasibility. Here, we introduce Cornucopia codes, a family of practical, hardware-efficient quantum low-density parity-check codes that achieve an ultra-high encoding rate exceeding $1/2$ while maintaining a pseudo-threshold exceeding $0.4\\%$ under the standard circuit-level noise model. Inspired by recent affine-permutation-based code constructions and the long-range connectivity available in reconfigurable neutral-atom arrays, we adopt a structured code geometry in which the code layout, atom rearrangement, and syndrome-extraction schedule are co-designed. This structure enables nonlocal syndrome measurements through simple, parallel atom rearrangements. A complete syndrome extraction cycle measures all $X$- and $Z$-type checks in parallel with $12$ entangling layers, independent of the code size. The resulting threshold is comparable to those of the surface code and bivariate bicycle codes. In particular, a single code block $[[2844,1426,18]]$ encodes $1{,}426$ distance-$18$ logical qubits, achieving an extrapolated logical error rate of $2.6\\times10^{-16}$ ($1.9\\times10^{-31}$) per logical qubit per cycle, assuming the physical error rate of $0.1\\%$ ($0.01\\%$). By comparison, a bivariate bicycle code implementation would require more than $68{,}000$ physical qubits to encode the same number of logical qubits at a comparable logical error rate. These results bring demonstrations of ultra-low-overhead quantum error correction within the reach of near-term quantum processors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02773","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:08:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03966","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03966","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03966","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salah Eddine","familyName":"Bekhouche","name":"Bekhouche, Salah Eddine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdessalam","familyName":"Bouchekif","name":"Bouchekif, Abdessalam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hichem","familyName":"Telli","name":"Telli, Hichem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed-En-Nadhir","familyName":"Zighem","name":"Zighem, Mohammed-En-Nadhir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdenour","familyName":"Hadid","name":"Hadid, Abdenour","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HalluTruthQA-4K: A Fine-Grained Corpus and Annotation Process for Arabic Hallucination Detection and Truth Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:33:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T21:05:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:09:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models can generate fluent Arabic answers while introducing factual errors that are difficult to identify and verify. Existing Arabic hallucination resources often assign a binary label to an entire response, indicating whether it is hallucinated or non-hallucinated, but provide limited information about the exact erroneous content, the reason for the error, or the correct factual answer. We present HalluTruthQA-4K, an expanded version of the HalluTruthQA resource containing 4,000 expert-curated Arabic question-answering instances across four knowledge-intensive domains: Islamic knowledge, history, science, and geography. Serving as the official dataset for Track 2 of the HalluScoring 2026 shared task, HalluTruthQA-4K extends our original corpus to 4,000 instances. Each instance pairs an Arabic question with a model-generated response, a verified reference answer, and five plausible distractors. Hallucinated responses are additionally annotated with character-level erroneous spans, human-written explanations, and hierarchical hallucination types. The corpus contains 1,643 hallucinated and 2,357 non-hallucinated responses, with 1,843 annotated erroneous spans. We describe the resource construction and annotation methodology, including question selection, controlled answer generation, candidate construction, expert annotation, independent verification, adjudication, and quality control. We also document the annotation guidelines, taxonomy, data format, inter-annotator agreement, and corpus statistics. HalluTruthQA-4K provides a reusable resource for hallucination detection, span-level error localization, explanation generation, factual verification, and the broader evaluation of factual reliability in Arabic language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03966","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:25:05Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:25:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanshen","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yuanshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zipeng","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengru","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Chengru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiqin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Peiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Reward Registers for Diffusion Preference Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:06:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T03:09:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:20:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T15:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:49:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning diffusion models with human preferences usually relies on a sparse terminal reward evaluated on the final generated samples, which creates a severe temporal credit-assignment problem across the denoising process. We propose Latent Reward Registers, a mechanism that estimates terminal preference directly from intermediate noisy latents. Learnable, position-free register tokens are appended as an auxiliary read path to a frozen Diffusion Transformer (DiT), extracting preference signals without altering the generator's hidden states or velocity field. The resulting dense, differentiable reward field spans the full denoising trajectory and supports two alignment strategies. For training, Reward-Gradient On-Policy Distillation (RG-OPD) converts this dense reward field into per-step targets at states visited by the current generator, replacing rollout-intensive policy gradients with direct on-policy distillation. For inference, Reward-Guided Sampling (RGS) steers trajectories with magnitude-matched reward-gradient corrections and no parameter updates. Empirically, at high noise levels (t=0.8) the registers reach the highest pairwise accuracy among the evaluated latent reward models. RG-OPD outperforms online reinforcement learning baselines while reducing GPU hours by up to 33x. RGS achieves significant reward improvement with a favorable reward-quality balance against training-free baselines. Code and weights are to be available at https://github.com/Guanys-dar/latent-reward-register"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:24:13Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:24:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daliang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Daliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duojin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Duojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoze","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Haoze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinshuo","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jinshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junbo","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Junbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kezhao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kezhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longxi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Longxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjie","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Rongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangguang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shangguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tam","familyName":"Sikyuen","name":"Sikyuen, Tam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikai","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Weikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanzhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingying","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuezhi","familyName":"Zu","name":"Zu, Yuezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yunhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhyAI: Real-Time Physical AI at the Edge, Scalable Rollouts in the Cloud"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:56:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T05:24:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:25:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T06:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physical AI policies require inference throughout their lifecycle, including model evaluation, cloud reinforcement learning rollout, edge GPU serving, and onboard deployment. Although these settings share the same checkpoint and action semantics, they often rely on separate inference programs. To unify them, we build PhyAI, a Physical AI inference engine with a single runtime that keeps architecture-specific conditioning, solver, cache, and output logic in model adapters while sharing graph execution, kernels, memory management, and parallel services. The same codebase runs vision-language-action (VLA) models and world-action models (WAMs) on single or multiple GPUs across onboard, edge, and cloud deployments. We used the adapter interface to add MiniCPM-Robot on the day of its release. PhyAI achieves 1.40x-4.65x speedups over the official implementations of pi0, pi0.5, GR00T N1.7, and MiniCPM-Robot. On Cosmos3-Nano-Policy-DROID it reduces latency from 2.46 to 1.18 s on eight H20 GPUs (CFG=2, TP=4), a 2.08x speedup. Specialized runtimes remain faster in several configurations, so our goal is one runtime with competitive latency rather than the fastest result in every case. Detailed profiles reveal why different models need different execution policies: on a Hopper-series GPU at batch size one, the pi0.5 action expert accounts for 8.8% of FLOPs but 57.2% of latency; at batch size 32 its share drops to 13.5% and throughput reaches about 100 samples/s. Cosmos3 remains generation-dominated and gains only 14.3% throughput as batch size increases from 1 to 16. We further introduce the control-time Roofline, which distinguishes inference-bound from environment-bound control; the measured pi0.5 points on four LIBERO suites are environment-bound while Cosmos3 stays inference-bound. Code and benchmarks: https://github.com/mingti-org/phyai."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:18:32Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:18:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03471","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03471","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03471","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuyuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwen","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Siwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Zijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dongming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hi-Token: Hierarchical Coordinate Tokenization for Generative Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:07:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:46:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T04:57:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:21:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative Vision-Language Models (VLMs) commonly treat bounding-box coordinates as independent output symbols, leaving numerical order and axis semantics implicit. We identify this representation as an important source of error in visual grounding. Hi-Token encodes each coordinate with axis-specific tokens for the hundreds, tens, and ones digits, which adds coarse-to-fine structure and increases token reuse while retaining the existing VLM architecture. Hi-GAR complements this representation with a geometry-based reward for Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), using box overlap and coordinate accuracy at multiple scales. Controlled comparisons under matched training conditions show that Hi-Token improves localization throughout the evaluated IoU range. Hi-GAR further reduces low-overlap predictions and is used only during training. Experiments on three VLM backbones and the RefCOCO family show consistent gains across models and benchmarks. Hi-R1 achieves higher values than strong specialist baselines on most reported metrics. Analyses of token frequency, digit boundaries, object scale, and IoU distributions explain the effects of coordinate representation and reward training. The results show that structured coordinate generation provides an effective approach to generative visual grounding. Project page: https://xyzzzh.github.io/Hi-Token/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, 15 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03471","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:13:42Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:13:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03353","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03353","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03353","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Forré","name":"Forré, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Direct Route to Markov Chain Convergence via Asymptotic Equivalence with the Target"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation (stat.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60J05 (Primary), 60J22, 60B10 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:03:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T07:44:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:27:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a Markov kernel $T$ with an invariant probability measure $π$, we give a self-contained proof of the Markov chain convergence theorem via a criterion called asymptotic equivalence with the target. It assumes two parts about the Lebesgue decompositions of $T^n_x$ and $π$ for every starting point $x$: 1.) asymptotic absolute continuity: the singular mass $\\mathrm{sing}(T^n_x \\mid π)$ tends to $0$; and, 2.) asymptotic domination of the target: the singular mass $\\mathrm{sing}(π\\mid T^n_x)$ tends to $0$, as $n \\to \\infty$. Assuming a jointly measurable density for the absolutely continuous part of each iterate $T^n$ w.r.t. $π$, this criterion is sufficient and necessary for convergence. A positive minorant density version of it is verified in three cases: i.) $T$ has a positive transition density w.r.t. $π$; ii.) $T$ consists of an absolutely continuous part with positive transition density together with an atom at the starting point, which covers the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm; iii.) the transition density is positive only after a finite number of steps that may depend on the starting point $x$. To demonstrate our general criterion, we investigate the Gibbs sampler with random scan and the parallel tempering algorithm. Furthermore, we show that in all mentioned settings Birkhoff's ergodic theorem applies, so as to obtain the strong law of large numbers. Throughout this paper, neither irreducibility, nor aperiodicity, nor recurrence, nor couplings, nor splitting constructions, nor small sets are used. In all results, the state space is a general measurable space with no structure beyond a $σ$-algebra. That joint measurability is assumed of the Markov kernel, not of the space; countable generation supplies it. None of the theorems proved here is new; what is offered is a short route to a single, widely applicable Markov chain convergence criterion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03353","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:10:55Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:10:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03227","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03227","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03227","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yukang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangchen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaorui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanghai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fanghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runhan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Runhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renjing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Renjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PFM-HR: Pose Flow Matching for Humanoid Robots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T06:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:31:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T02:16:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:16:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motion priors improve reinforcement learning for physics-based humanoid tracking, but temporal priors require ordered motion clips, while pose priors provide limited guidance for policy-induced pose transitions. We present Pose Flow Matching for Humanoid Robots (PFM-HR), a reusable flow matching prior trained directly on large scale unordered pose data. PFM-HR introduces the Pose Geometry Score (PGS), which quantifies how joint coordinate changes during rollouts align with the local geometry of pose variation captured by the prior. Using PGS to modulate the tracking reward guides policy exploration toward structured pose changes while keeping the prior frozen across tracking tasks. Experiments demonstrate that PFM-HR improves both single motion and general motion tracking, especially for highly dynamic motions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03227","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:08:01Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:08:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03102","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03102","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03102","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Xuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu-Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Xiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu-Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Typical Output States of Monitored Random Clifford Circuits: A Graph-Theoretic Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T04:19:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:24:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T05:00:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:21:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In many-body physics, an explicit wavefunction often provides the most thorough understanding, yet for monitored random Clifford circuit it has remained missing. Here we develop a framework that grants direct access to the typical output states. As every stabilizer state is local-Clifford-equivalent to a graph state, the graph adjacency matrix, a classical bit matrix, provides a complete description of the quantum state. In the large-$N$ limit, we show that these graphs converge to the Erdős--Rényi random graph $G(N,1/2)$, which allows us to resolve the open problem of Greenberger--Horne--Zeilinger (GHZ) entanglement generated by deep random Clifford circuits. We obtain analytically the mean GHZ content $\\langle g_3\\rangle=1.204$ for even $N$ and $1.325$ for odd $N$. For monitored Clifford circuits with a 1D layout, we uncover an emergent Erdős--Rényi subgraph $G(N_{\\mathrm{sub}},1/2)$ in the output states of the volume-law phase, where $N_{\\mathrm{sub}}/N\\approx \\sqrt{1-p/p_c}$ with $p$ the measurement rate and $p_c$ the critical point of the measurement-induced phase transition (MIPT). The output state is thus equivalent to the output of an unmonitored random Clifford circuit on $N_{\\mathrm{sub}}$ qubits, weakly perturbed by the remaining $N-N_{\\mathrm{sub}}$ qubits carrying little entanglement. This result directly accounts for the quantum error-correcting capability of the volume-law phase, and implies the same GHZ statistics for the whole volume-law phase. We further identify a clustering effect for qubits in the dense subgraph, which we reproduce with an infection-recovery toy model that exhibits a measurement-induced absorbing-state phase transition. Finally, a mean-field argument on the graph locates the MIPT critical point at $p_c = 0.1608$, in excellent agreement with the numerical value $p_c\\approx 0.16$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03102","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:05:07Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:05:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcello","familyName":"Barylli","name":"Barylli, Marcello","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Béna","name":"Béna, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Mordvintsev","name":"Mordvintsev, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eleni","familyName":"Nisioti","name":"Nisioti, Eleni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Risi","name":"Risi, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Organising Digital Circuits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-07T15:26:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T10:10:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:35:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fault tolerance in classical computing has traditionally relied on static strategies like hardware redundancy and error-correcting codes. Biological systems, in contrast, exhibit adaptive plasticity, maintaining function through dynamic re-organisation around damage. Inspired by this principle, we introduce Self-Organising Digital Circuits, framing functional logic generation and maintenance as a meta-learning problem on graphs. Our architecture employs a topology-masked Transformer that configures the Lookup Tables (LUT) of a circuit's Boolean gates. Extending the pattern-generation paradigm of Neural Cellular Automata (NCA), it navigates the degenerate Boolean search space to satisfy a computational task, rather than regenerating a fixed target state. We demonstrate that it can self-assemble functional circuits from scratch and rapidly re-route logic around permanent, previously unseen hardware faults. For soft errors, the policy achieves near-perfect recovery (\u0026gt;99.99\\% accuracy) from damage sizes far exceeding training conditions. We further observe generalisation across circuit scales: accuracy improves on graphs substantially wider than those seen during training. This work bridges the principles of biological self-organisation with the practical domain of digital hardware."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:53:40Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:53:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:12:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joanna","familyName":"Matulewicz","name":"Matulewicz, Joanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renata","familyName":"Ratajczak","name":"Ratajczak, Renata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ewa","familyName":"Grzanka","name":"Grzanka, Ewa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej Oskar","familyName":"Liedke","name":"Liedke, Maciej Oskar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damian","familyName":"Kalita","name":"Kalita, Damian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Hirschmann","name":"Hirschmann, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikolaj","familyName":"Grabowski","name":"Grabowski, Mikolaj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal A.","familyName":"Strozyk","name":"Strozyk, Michal A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cyprian","familyName":"Mieszczynski","name":"Mieszczynski, Cyprian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Przemyslaw","familyName":"Jozwik","name":"Jozwik, Przemyslaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulrich","familyName":"Kentsch","name":"Kentsch, Ulrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rene","familyName":"Heller","name":"Heller, Rene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederico","familyName":"Garrido","name":"Garrido, Frederico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From phase transformation to amorphization: damage accumulation in Yb-implanted $β-Ga_2O_3$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:12:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T12:35:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:46:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study provides a comprehensive analysis of the radiation response and structural evolution of differently oriented$β-Ga_2O_3$ single crystals subjected to Yb ion implantation over a wide fluence range from $5 \\times 10^{12}$ to $1 \\times 10^{16}$~cm$^{-2}$ ($0.04$--$74$~dpa). A multi-technique approach (RBS/c, PAS, HRTEM, and HRXRD) was employed to investigate the mechanisms of damage accumulation. The results reveal a multi-stage process of defect evolution. At a critical threshold of around $0.4$~dpa, the accumulation of lattice strain triggers a phase transformation from monoclinic $β$-Ga$_{2}$O$_{3}$ to a defective spinel structure of $γ$-Ga$_{2}$O$_{3}$. Notably, the formation of this new phase is accompanied by strain relaxation. With further irradiation, defects develop within the crystal structure of $γ$-Ga$_{2}$O$_{3}$. The associated atomic reorganization at this stage is reflected by a distinct dip in the damage accumulation curve and the appearance of stacking faults in the subsurface region of the implanted layer. In contrast to previous reports suggesting high radiation stability of this phase, the present study clearly demonstrates that continuous defect accumulation results in a significant increase in both displaced atoms and vacancy-type defects, with a strong depth dependence in their type and density. Ultimately, at an irradiation level of approximately $7$~dpa, the surface layer amorphizes. With further irradiation, the amorphous layer expands, gradually replacing the transient $γ$-Ga$_{2}$O$_{3}$ phase. These findings reveal that the radiation tolerance of gallium oxide is highly sensitive to ion-specific interactions and strain-induced instabilities, thereby challenging the previously assumed robustness of this material under high-fluence ion irradiation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Carried out within NCN project UMO-2022/45/B/ST5/02810, partially conducted at HZDR (IBC,ELBE)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:21:14Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:21:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Cuomo","name":"Cuomo, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Giombi","name":"Giombi, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Tizzano","name":"Tizzano, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Impurities Near the Light Cone"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:10:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:57:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T12:05:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:44:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The lightlike Wilson line cusp underlies the Sudakov double logarithm and is a central ingredient in gauge-theory factorization. We ask what replaces this behavior for general conformal line defects in Lorentzian signature. We argue that, when the defects admit local endpoint operators, the cusp anomalous dimension has a finite large-boost limit fixed by the scaling dimensions of the corresponding defect-creation operators. We further analyze the distinct analytic continuations of the cusp, elucidate their physical interpretations, and derive a positivity condition on the Lorentzian cusp anomalous dimension. We test these predictions in several perturbative examples, including pinning-field defects and spin impurities, and discuss their possible implications for gauge theories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"62 pages, 6 figures v2: included derivation of positivity condition, references added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basit","familyName":"Alawode","name":"Alawode, Basit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moshira Ali","familyName":"Abdalla","name":"Abdalla, Moshira Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dwarikanath","familyName":"Mahapatra","name":"Mahapatra, Dwarikanath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzammal","familyName":"Naseer","name":"Naseer, Muzammal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajid","familyName":"Javed","name":"Javed, Sajid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Multi-Resolution Cells to Gigapixel Whole Slide Images Foundation Model for Computational Pathology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:51:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:48:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T06:18:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:28:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) and their hierarchical variants have achieved strong performance in Computational Pathology (CPath). However, most are pre-trained on single-resolution Whole Slide Images (WSIs), limiting their generalization across arbitrary resolutions. Gigapixel WSIs inherently contain diagnostic patterns at multiple scales, including cellular morphologies, tissue architectures, and global context, mirroring how expert pathologists examine WSIs. We introduce Multi-Resolution Pyramid Transformer (MRPT), a model that hierarchically aggregates multi-resolution information from cellular to tissue and WSI levels. MRPT employs a biologically meaningful Consecutive Cross-Resolution Attention (CCRA) mechanism to capture scale-independent interactions and enforces multi-resolution semantic consistency by aligning embeddings across resolutions, yielding robust and generalizable WSI representations. Pre-trained in a multi-resolution self-supervised manner on 624M patches, 2.4M regions, and 36K WSIs, MRPT learns rich coarse-to-fine histopathology features. Extensive experiments on 34 diverse datasets show that MRPT surpasses recent foundation models and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) in cancer subtype classification, tissue phenotyping, and Visual Question Answering (VQA) for WSI understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:14:35Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:14:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfei","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Chenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyang","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Zeyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Feifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erliang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Erliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Truth Is Distributed: Misinformation Derails Collective Fact Recovery in LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:12:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:43:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T04:55:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:24:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T06:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:29:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based multi-agent systems promise effective collaborative reasoning, but communication may amplify local errors into collective risks, and while existing evaluations emphasize final outcomes, they leave the reliability and propagation dynamics of distributed information aggregation unclear, so we introduce ForesightSafety-TIDE, a controlled evaluation framework that strictly pairs all-honest collaboration with controlled deception by a key evidence holder and analyzes the aggregation process through multi-stage voting, testimony adoption, and evidence-root lineage propagation, and using 120 five-agent object-movement environments where partial observations jointly determine a unique endpoint, we evaluate 3 homogeneous LLM-based multi-agent systems, and across these paired conditions, aggregate truth recovery falls from 72.50% to 14.17%, with significant declines for every system, while process tracing and exit ablations show that a single false testimony is adopted more readily than truthful testimony, propagates to higher orders, and persists through honest agents after the deceiver exits, and observers without first-hand evidence suppress incorrect consensus but do not improve truth recovery, so together, these findings reveal both the fragility of distributed fact recovery and its underlying mechanism: false evidence gains collective influence through its adoption and continued propagation by other agents after entering communication."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:29Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03355","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03355","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03355","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li-Xiang","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Li-Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrepancy between the H-Function and Entropy: Insights into the rigorously established Boltzmann equation for hard-sphere gases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Physics (physics.class-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:04:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T14:01:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:51:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"By investigating the origin of the evolutionary discrepancy between the H-function and entropy, we elucidate that the H-function fails to serve as a valid arrow-of-time criterion in the rigorously derived Boltzmann equation for the hard-sphere gas model under the Boltzmann-Grad limit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"3 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03355","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:10:57Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sherry J. H.","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Sherry J. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kim Chester","familyName":"Eugenio","name":"Eugenio, Kim Chester","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Fon","name":"Fon, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surrogate Substitution Preserves PHI Detectability: A Multi-Detector Equivalence Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T06:04:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:28:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T08:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:34:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T03:45:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:23:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Structure-preserving de-identification replaces protected health information (PHI) with realistic same-type surrogates -- \"Anna S.\" becomes \"Maria S.\", not [NAME] -- so that clinical text stays fluent and downstream tools keep working. But this only helps if the substitution does not itself corrupt the signal those tools rely on. We ask a narrow, testable question: on the spans a de-identifier actually masks, can downstream PHI detectors still find the surrogate? We introduce a paired, multi-detector evaluation protocol that (i) scores utility only on masked spans, decoupling coverage from utility; (ii) uses equivalence testing (TOST) rather than null-hypothesis significance testing, which is uninformative at our sample size (57k paired spans); and (iii) builds a surrogate-failure typology separating fixable generator defects from intrinsic detector limits. Across 11 detectors, 7 benchmarks, and 7 languages (1,750 documents), recall on masked spans moves from 76.1% to 74.9% -- a change our equivalence test shows is statistically equivalent to zero within a +/-2-point margin (p ~ 3e-9), with detector ranking preserved. The residual loss does not reflect detectors getting worse at PHI: it concentrates in malformed and out-of-distribution surrogates (truncation Chicago -\u0026gt; Illino, salience loss Cedars-Sinai -\u0026gt; Vidant). A redaction floor and an open-source surrogate baseline indicate the effect is a property of well-formed substitution, not of one tool. We release the evaluation subsets, scoring code, and an interactive dashboard at https://custodianai.pages.dev so the protocol can audit any structure-preserving transform."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 3 figures, 10 tables. Code, data, and interactive dashboard: https://custodianai.pages.dev ; repository: https://github.com/Custodian-Labs/guardian-layer-phi-benchmark"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:45Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03073","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03073","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03073","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Kou","name":"Kou, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenchao","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Zhenchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finite-valuation approximable structures: a solution to the Jung--Tix problem of probabilistic powerdomains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.3.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"06B35, 06F30, 18D15, 68Q55, 60B05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T03:38:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:23:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T03:29:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:22:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the category \\(\\FVA\\) of finite-valuation approximable domains, a full subcategory of continuous domains contained in the category of pointed countably based FS-domains. We prove that \\(\\FVA\\) is Cartesian closed and closed under both the subprobability and probability valuation powerdomains. Hence the valuation monads \\(\\Vsub\\) and \\(\\Vone\\) restrict to \\(\\FVA\\), yielding a positive answer to the generalized form of Jung--Tix problem, one of the longest-standing open problem in domain theory since 1990s. The proof is divided into two steps. First, for every finite poset \\(P\\), we construct an increasing FS approximate identity on \\(\\Vsub(P)\\), and thereby show that \\(\\Vsub(P)\\) is a countably based FS-domain. Second, we call a domain finite-valuation approximable when its identity is the pointwise supremum of an increasing sequence of maps factoring through spaces \\(\\Vsub(P_n)\\), where each \\(P_n\\) is finite. A finite-separation saturation theorem and a unified kernel-lifting theorem then show that \\(\\FVA\\) is closed under Scott-continuous retracts, finite products, function spaces, \\(\\Vsub\\), and \\(\\Vone\\)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"55 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03073","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:04:27Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:04:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02711","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02711","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02711","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junliang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Junliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenkun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kenkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guocun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guocun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansong","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Yansong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunshi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chunshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yunhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiaao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunchao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chunchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hunyuan3D-Buffalo 1.0: A Unified Multimodal Model for Scalable 3D Generation, Understanding, and Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T17:57:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T15:39:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T01:04:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T08:47:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image generation have demonstrated the potential of unified multimodal models that integrate understanding, generation, and editing. However, unified 3D modeling remains constrained by scarce multimodal data, particularly the lack of large-scale and geometrically consistent editing data. To address this limitation, we propose Hunyuan3D-Buffalo 1.0, a unified framework supporting 3D understanding, text-to-3D generation, instruction-guided 3D editing, and text-grounded part generation within a single architecture. To enable scalable training, we construct an 87M-scale 3D multimodal corpus, comprising 25M understanding samples, 50M text-to-3D pairs, and 12M editing pairs generated using Nano3D-v2. Architecturally, the framework combines Hunyuan3D-VLM for semantic, structural, and spatial understanding with Hunyuan3D DiT for high-fidelity 3D synthesis. The VLM provides multimodal semantic conditions for generation, while editing and part generation additionally condition the diffusion process on the source object representation to preserve its overall structure and unedited regions. Extensive experiments show that Hunyuan3D-Buffalo 1.0 achieves state-of-the-art or leading performance on text-to-3D generation and 3D editing benchmarks, while exhibiting strong understanding and part-generation capabilities. Our analysis further shows that both generation and understanding improve editing, demonstrating the effectiveness of unified 3D multimodal training. Project Page: https://tencent-hunyuan.github.io/Hunyuan3D-Buffalo1.0/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://tencent-hunyuan.github.io/Hunyuan3D-Buffalo1.0"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02711","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:56:06Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:56:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:29:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijia","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Peijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ZiGuang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, ZiGuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Representational Similarity: Source-Conditioned Description-Length Gain for Generative Plagiarism Detection and Candidate Source Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:03:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:03:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-12T01:26:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:13:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) pose challenges to academic integrity and peer review. Yet generative plagiarism detection remains an underexplored and largely unresolved challenge. Prior work on LLM-generated-text detection targets AI involvement, which may be permissible, rather than source reuse, while similarity-based methods struggle after extensive rewriting and multi-source synthesis. Motivated by the description-length view of probabilistic prediction, in which relevant side information can reduce a target sequence's code length, we introduce Source-Conditioned Description-Length Gain (SCDG), a directional, training-free framework that contrasts a frozen language model's description length of a suspicious document $P$ with and without a candidate source $S$. This contrast yields token-level log-likelihood gains that measure the incremental predictive evidence supplied by $S$. We evaluate SCDG on the PAN at CLEF benchmarks for generative plagiarism. On a PAN 2025-derived pairwise benchmark, SCDG achieves 0.92 Precision, 0.97 Recall, and 0.94 F1, outperforming all baselines; on PAN 2026's multi-source retrieval task, it reaches 0.83 nDCG@10 and 0.96 Recall@100, surpassing all baselines. On a same-topic, same-event Multi-News test, the calibrated gain-distribution SCDG classifier predicts source reuse for only $0.125\\%$ of pairs, supporting robustness to topical overlap under this evaluation protocol. These results establish SCDG as a unified and token-decomposable signal for source-specific content reuse under extensive transformation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:38Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:17:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03642","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03642","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03642","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Mingyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lie","name":"Lie, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Papernot","name":"Papernot, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empirical Analysis of Evasion and Poisoning Against Malware Data Drift Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:27:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:54:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T22:05:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:10:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T03:35:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:18:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As concept drift due to malware evolution presents challenges for malware classification, machine learning-based data drift detection tools are developed to mitigate this problem. These data drift detector tools are designed for a different purpose and built with different techniques compared to malware classifiers. Although evasion and poisoning attacks against machine learning-based malware classifiers can cause misclassification of malware samples, it is not clear how these attacks work against data drift detectors and malware classifiers in combination. This work investigates the effect of evasion and poisoning attacks on the data drift detector along with the malware classifier. We demonstrate how unique characteristics of data drift detectors cause attacks against malware classifiers to work differently against them."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03642","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:17:38Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:17:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:17:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Viana","name":"Viana, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Common Structure Behind Birth Stopping Rules"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T23:41:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:14:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T20:53:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:07:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study stopping rules in which each family is assigned a positive integer and stops the first time its number of girls equals that integer plus a fixed nonnegative integer multiple of its number of boys. After all families have stopped, we ask for the expected proportion of boys and the expected boy-to-girl ratio in the combined population, and show that both expectations depend on the assigned integers only through their sum. We obtain exact series for both expectations, allowing the probabilities of a boy and a girl to be unequal. Our formulas recover the known formulas for stopping at the first girl and for stopping when girls first outnumber boys. These expectations can also be recovered from earlier general results on Galton--Watson degree profiles and shifted inverse moments, but the resulting series do not appear to have been recorded in these forms. To our knowledge, our two elementary proofs, one bijective and one probabilistic, are also new."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:01:49Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:17:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Complementary Measurements Count the Same Classical Bit Twice: Counterexamples to CQC, ECQC, and Complementarity-Based Certification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:36:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T13:38:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:55:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mutually unbiased measurements are commonly expected to expose independent facets of a quantum state: a correlation that is classical in one basis should disappear in a complementary basis. In higher dimensions, however, this intuition becomes particularly subtle because correlations recovered in different settings need not represent different information. To expose this loophole, we propose a two-branch classical null test: before the setting is chosen, a shared bit selects one of two orthogonal product preparations, producing a rank-two classical--classical state, and the candidate protocol then runs unchanged. Different settings can read the same bit through different outcome patterns. This two-branch classical architecture disproves the complementary-quantum correlation (CQC) conjecture in every dimension $d\\geq3$. A distinct rank-two classical--classical state disproves its complete-basis extension (ECQC) at $d=7$, with an overrun that grows without bound along prime dimensions. Its qutrit CQC instance also gives classical false positives for a proposed quantum-correlation measure and a proposed one-sided semi-device-independent steering criterion, and refutes a conditional-probability conjecture. The failures identify the missing requirement: information read in different settings must be nonredundant. In experiments and applications, the same low-overhead architecture can serve as a calibration test before a multibasis score is assigned quantum meaning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:21:55Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:21:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03804","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03804","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03804","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Young","name":"Young, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artyom","familyName":"Gabtraupov","name":"Gabtraupov, Artyom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenzy","familyName":"Soror","name":"Soror, Kenzy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinivasan","familyName":"Keshav","name":"Keshav, Srinivasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Usable Are Geospatial Foundation Models? A Systematic Evaluation of 89 Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:16:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T12:48:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Geospatial foundation models (GeoFMs) offer transformative potential for environmental monitoring, yet adoption among ecologists is uneven. Most evaluations are model-centric, focusing on architecture and benchmark accuracy, which overlooks whether the systems are usable by their intended audiences. To address this gap, we first conducted a pilot expert elicitation survey with ecology and conservation scientists that helped us identify misalignments between current GeoFM development priorities and their needs. Informed by these findings and based on HCI theory, we created a seven-dimension evaluation covering Access \u0026amp; Deployment, Interaction \u0026amp; Customization, Trust \u0026amp; Transparency, Community \u0026amp; Support, Scientific Permanence, Multilingual Support, and Offline Usability. Then, two raters applied this rubric to 89 GeoFMs. We found distinct accessibility gaps where nearly a third provide no support to practitioners beyond their source code. Dimensions along which ratings were highly consistent function as field-level diagnostics, revealing where there is room for improvement for current GeoFMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03804","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:21:22Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:21:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Indranil","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Indranil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayantan","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Sayantan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susmita","familyName":"Jana","name":"Jana, Susmita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Archana","familyName":"Pai","name":"Pai, Archana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Shankaranarayanan","name":"Shankaranarayanan, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Integrated cosmological memory: A dark-siren method to probe dark energy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:33:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:58:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T06:13:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:32:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gravitational-wave (GW) cosmology is currently bottlenecked by the scarcity of electromagnetic counterparts for bright sirens and the systematic uncertainties of galaxy catalogs for dark sirens. We propose a purely gravitational resolution using the Integrated Cosmological Memory (ICM)-the cumulative GW strain encoded in the spacetime geometry of an expanding Universe. While the GW transient emitted by the source provides the luminosity distance, the ICM accumulates a mathematically distinct integral of the cosmic expansion history. We demonstrate that extracting both observables from a single binary merger completely breaks the distance-redshift degeneracy within the gravitational sector. This establishes a novel, catalog-free dark siren framework for third-generation GW detector networks. Crucially, the resulting constraints on late-time dark energy are only weakly sensitive to the local expansion rate, providing a robust cosmological probe that can potentially mitigate the impact of the H0 tension."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Astrophysical Journal Letters. 16 pages, 10 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:50Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03699","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03699","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03699","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TARL: Transaction-Aware Reliable Ledgers for Executable Memory Management in Long-Term Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:02:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:56:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T12:23:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:51:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Persistent memory helps long-term agents retain knowledge, yet a single update error can repeatedly distort future retrieval and reasoning. Most existing systems reduce memory updating to a binary Write/Hold decision, which cannot distinguish whether new information should be added, ignored, used to revise an outdated belief, rejected as unreliable, or deferred for verification. These choices may share the same binary label while producing fundamentally different memory states. We introduce TARL, a memory state update framework that maps each statement to one of five executable actions. TARL identifies the affected memory, resolves its temporal scope, compares source reliability, and updates accepted, pending, and rejected ledgers. It is further trained by comparing the memory states produced by alternative update operations, encouraging the model to select the operation that leads to the correct result. We also introduce TARL-Mem, a benchmark with fine-grained action labels and next-state targets. Across in-domain, cross-source, temporal, counterfactual, and sequential evaluations, TARL improves action prediction and state recovery, reduces memory pollution, preserves conflicting evidence, and limits cumulative corruption."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03699","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:18:56Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:18:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03665","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03665","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03665","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Velasco","name":"Velasco, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriele","familyName":"Spada","name":"Spada, Gabriele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Midei","name":"Midei, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Perali","name":"Perali, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis A. Peña","familyName":"Ardila","name":"Ardila, Luis A. Peña","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Impurities as Probes of Finite-Temperature Fluctuations in Two-Dimensional Bose Gases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:42:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:55:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T10:43:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:46:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Two-dimensional quantum gases provide a distinctive setting in which enhanced thermal fluctuations, finite-size effects, and two-body bound-state formation are intrinsically intertwined. In this work, we study a single attractive impurity immersed in a finite, weakly interacting two-dimensional Bose gas, where finite size stabilizes a nonzero condensate fraction by introducing an infrared momentum scale, thereby enabling a Bogoliubov description of the bath. Using a hybrid approach that combines finite-temperature many-body scattering theory with input from path-integral Monte Carlo, we analyze the impurity quasiparticle energy across the condensate and normal regimes. The infrared scale generates a phonon-activation temperature below which the impurity energy remains nearly temperature independent. Once the resolved phonon modes become thermally populated, their contribution competes with condensate depletion, producing a nonmonotonic temperature dependence of the polaron energy. These results suggest that attractive Bose polarons may serve as sensitive probes of finite-size thermal fluctuations, phonon dressing, and bound-state physics in low-dimensional Bose gases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03665","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:18:09Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:18:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyue","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Mingyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bokai","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Bokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanjian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huanjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaguo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yaguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Enhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CastFSR: A Fast--Slow--Reflect Agentic Reasoning Framework for Context-Aware Time Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T02:21:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:19:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T02:51:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:23:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series forecasting is fundamental to decision-making in complex systems, where future dynamics are influenced not only by historical observations but also by evolving contextual features. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have extended forecasting beyond numerical extrapolation toward context-aware reasoning. However, existing approaches often lack explicit mechanisms to identify relevant contexts, reason about their impacts, and validate forecasts against temporal and domain constraints. In this work, we propose CastFSR, an agentic framework that formulates context-aware forecasting as a Fast--Slow--Reflect workflow. In fast thinking, CastFSR profiles observations and selects lightweight forecasters to construct a data-driven forecast prior. In slow deliberation, it retrieves contextual evidence, adaptively determines informative look-back windows, and reasons about how contexts reshape future dynamics. In reflection, it iteratively refines forecasts to ensure temporal, contextual, and domain consistency. CastFSR supports both training-free inference with off-the-shelf LLMs and efficient deployment through a two-stage SFT and reinforcement learning strategy that transfers its orchestration capability to compact LLMs. Extensive experiments on public datasets demonstrate that CastFSR consistently outperforms representative baselines. Our code is available at https://github.com/Xiaoyu-Tao/CastFSR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:03:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:03:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02908","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02908","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02908","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanna","familyName":"Borissova","name":"Borissova, Johanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Formation of extremal regular black holes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T21:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:11:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:07:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:57:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Static and slowly evolving regular black holes with a non-extremal inner horizon are generally expected to suffer from mass inflation. Here we consider the scenario of asymptotic gravitational collapse into extremal regular black holes. To that end, we first construct geometric models of static spherically symmetric single-horizon extremal and double-horizon inner-extremal regular black holes for generic black hole mass as the only dimensionful scale. These spacetimes may be interpreted as non-fine-tuned vacuum solutions of modified gravitational theories defined implicitly by the requirement that their spherical reduction yields an integrable two-dimensional Horndeski theory. As such, these so-called general quasi-topological gravities admit exact Vaidya solutions in which the mass becomes a time-dependent function. We use this effectively two-dimensional second-order dynamical framework to model the asymptotic formation of extremal regular black holes without a violation of energy conditions. The latter is illustrated explicitly by the equivalence between the strong energy condition for generalised Vaidya solutions of general relativity, and the kinematic timelike convergence condition reformulated dynamically as an onshell condition on the theory-dependent functions characterising the spherical reduction and correspondingly Vaidya solutions of a general quasi-topological gravity. The defocusing of geodesics necessary for regular black holes is thus triggered by the modified gravitational dynamics rather than by the addition of exotic matter degrees of freedom."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2: 34 pages, 15 figures; Sect. V.B + discussion updated"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02908","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:00:40Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:00:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingtian","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Qingtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangkun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shangkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul L.","familyName":"Rosin","name":"Rosin, Paul L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghui","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Guanghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling Scientific Experiment Scenes: Dataset and Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T21:20:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T08:49:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T20:32:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:10:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scene Graph Generation (SGG) is fundamental to structured visual understanding, yet existing benchmarks focus mainly on daily-life images and overlook scientific experiment scenes with specialized instruments, task-specific experimental semantics, and dense, fine-grained physical relations. Building upon PhysScene, our previously introduced SGG dataset for physics experiment scenes, we further identify two key challenges that such scientific environments pose to existing SGG models: a pronounced long-tail relational predicate distribution and a substantial visual-textual semantic gap. To address these challenges, we propose the Cross-Modal Dual-Path Generator (CM-DPG), a model for robust open-vocabulary SGG. The model enhances object-level semantic representations through joint visual-textual encoding and improves relational reasoning using complementary visual and geometric cues. We also incorporate relation-aware pre-training, caption-derived pseudo-supervision, and adaptive weighting to support balanced learning across head and tail predicates. Extensive experiments on PhysScene and VG150 show that CM-DPG achieves competitive performance across multiple evaluation settings, with ablation studies validating the contribution of each component. The dataset and code are publicly available at https://github.com/ZMH-SDUST/CM-DPG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The authors have identified issues that require substantial revision and have therefore decided to withdraw the current version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:00:16Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:00:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Roth","name":"Roth, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Millis","name":"Millis, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomohiro","familyName":"Soejima","name":"Soejima, Tomohiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large scale neural quantum states reveal the interplay between superconductivity and quantum criticality in the Hofstadter-Hubbard model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:01:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:02:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T18:12:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:04:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how a parent insulating state shapes the superconductivity that emerges upon doping is a long-standing problem dating back to Anderson's resonating-valence-bond proposal. The triangular-lattice Hofstadter-Hubbard model with $π/2$ flux per plaquette offers an ideal setting: at half filling it hosts two distinct parent states---an integer quantum Hall insulator and a chiral spin liquid---separated by a topological phase transition. Using neural quantum states on tori of up to $432$ sites, we present strong evidence that the transition is continuous, with a vanishing $2e$ charge gap and critical charge fluctuations. Upon doping, we find a topological superconductor on either side of the transition. While the pairing order parameter remains nearly unchanged across the transition, the superfluid stiffness is strongly enhanced near the critical point. The energy scale of the superconductor is therefore set not by which parent state is doped, but by proximity to the transition between them. Our results establish neural quantum states as a powerful tool for understanding the interplay between unconventional electronic correlations and superconductivity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:04Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02673","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02673","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02673","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hankun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hankun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Colin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Colin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"dots.tts.edit: Precisely Controlled Speech Editing with a Continuous Autoregressive Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-02T15:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:01:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T17:29:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T01:10:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech editing for content creation requires precise control over both what an edit should do and where it should apply. Free-form natural language provides a flexible interface for expressing edit requests, but its ambiguity may leave the intended operation, parameters, or target region underspecified. We study a precise and explicit interface for speech editing: a transcript-grounded structural edit instruction with XML-style tags explicitly specifies typed operations and localizes them to transcript spans or boundaries. This semantic timeline avoids explicit timestamp alignment and provides an externally inspectable contract for compositional edits. We instantiate the interface in dots$.$tts$.$edit, an editor adapted from the continuous autoregressive dots$.$tts foundation model. Four representative speech-creation controls cover lexical content, affective expression, pitch and speaking-rate delivery, and temporal phrasing through text, emotion, prosody, and pause editing. Task-specific data pipelines construct operation- and scope-controlled pairs while retaining source-derived context outside each target region. We further introduce doteBench, a bilingual evaluation suite that measures precise instruction following, local preservation, and audio quality across the four controls and their composition. Experiments show leading overall instruction following and local preservation across its five editing categories, while audio quality remains comparable to existing open-source systems. Across three Seed-TTS-Eval shards, the model shows negligible differences from the base model in zero-shot TTS recognition error rate and speaker similarity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02673","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:55:14Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:55:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:40:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dang Vo","familyName":"Phuc","name":"Phuc, Dang Vo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combinatorial Bounds on the Peterson Hit Problem via Certified Matrix Minors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Topology (math.AT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 55S10, Secondary 05E45, 05C65, 15A03, 15B33","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-07-21T03:42:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T12:12:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:50:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Peterson hit problem seeks a minimal set of generators for the polynomial algebra $\\mathcal P_k=\\mathbb F_2[x_1,\\ldots,x_k]$ as a module over the mod--2 Steenrod algebra. While completely resolved for $k \\leq 4$, the unrestricted problem remains widely open for $k \\geq 5$, where the combinatorial explosion of basis elements renders exact algorithmic computation intractable. To bypass full Gaussian elimination, we model the degree--$d$ hit space via a sparse matrix driven by the Cartan formula and Lucas's theorem, shifting the focus to the construction of certified matrix minors. We first prove that strict spike monomials exactly characterize the zero rows, establishing a hard structural limit on coordinate-level annihilators. To bound the matrix rank from above (cohit lower bound), we derive exact zero-column formulae, which are strictly refined by the exact homology of the $\\operatorname{Sq}^1$-layer and systematic linear dependencies induced by Adem relations. To bound the rank from below (cohit upper bound), we extract explicit independent column families: singleton columns yield permutation minors, acyclic pivot systems optimize triangular minors across all row orders, and $q$-support columns are formalized through hypergraph incidence. Crucially, we identify a congruence family that decomposes precisely into simplicial boundary matrices over $\\mathbb F_2$, yielding a sharp closed-form rank formula. The resulting two-sided bounds are universally computable for every $k \\geq 1$ and $d \\geq 0$. Significantly, these results establish the absolute limits of purely combinatorial approaches to the hit problem, cleanly separating universal discrete certificates from the degree-specific resolutions provided by representation theory and weight filtrations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages. We welcome constructive comments/feedback"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:54:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:54:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:39:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Giunti","name":"Giunti, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizia Giulia","familyName":"Garavaglia","name":"Garavaglia, Fabrizia Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Transformer Revolution, Part 1: Dynamic Processing through Output-Weight Interconnections"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:53:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:06:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T10:20:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:29:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper offers a new interpretation of the Transformer during inference. Against the \"stochastic parrot\" view that large language models merely reproduce statistical regularities learned in training, we argue that Transformers construct and apply prompt-dependent transformations whose parameters are generated during inference. We call this form of processing SIDPP: Sequence-level Interactive Dynamic Parallel Processing. The Transformer is interpreted as a system that transforms concepts by means of concepts. Token vectors are the concepts to be transformed; parameterized transformations defined by matrices and vectors are the transforming concepts. These may be static, when fixed through training, or dynamic, when generated from the input sequence. Mechanically, they correspond to groups of simple neural networks. The Transformer's architectural novelty lies in output-weight interconnections, through which the outputs of some networks determine the weights of others, alongside ordinary output-input interconnections. By means of these interconnections, the system constructs transformations from the prompt and uses them to modify token representations. The contribution of dynamic processing grows with prompt length and may equal or exceed that of static processing, a phenomenon we call strong prompt sensitivity. This account bears on interpretability, predictability, control, and the design of smaller, more sustainable systems. Finally, because the human neural system possesses mechanisms similar to those required for SIDPP, we argue that a form of SIDPP may, in principle, be realized in the cerebral cortex. We therefore conjecture that human language processing may itself be a form of SIDPP produced by a functional architecture relevantly similar to that of the Transformer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v1: 7 Sections, References, Appendix, Tables (2 tables), Figures Part A (6 figures), Figures Part B (42 figures), Figures Part C (5 figures); v2: corrected typos, new version of Figure 20"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:24:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:24:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03902","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03902","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03902","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaofeng","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Shaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runwei","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Runwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenshuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wenshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiemin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaishen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Kaishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songning","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Songning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daizong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daizong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Efficiency Becomes Fragility: Exploiting Dynamic Routing Vulnerabilities in Adaptive UAV Tracking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T11:19:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:31:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Resource constraints on UAV platforms have driven a paradigm shift in aerial tracking, from pursuing performance toward balancing accuracy with efficiency. Adaptive Transformer Trackers, which leverage an input-dependent dynamic routing architecture, have emerged as a representative solution to this challenge. However, we reveal that behind this computation-on-demand flexibility hides a critical structural flaw: the Lipschitz singularity of computational path decisions, which has an unbounded local Lipschitz constant at discrete layer-skipping decision boundaries. This mathematical discontinuity renders adaptive tracking networks inherently unstable: tiny input perturbations can be amplified at the gating modules, causing dramatic changes in the inference topology. We formally characterize this singularity in the context of adaptive tracking architectures and, for the first time, identify it as a directly exploitable new attack surface. This insight reveals a previously overlooked and highly vulnerable topological path space attack surface. Based on this, we propose the Adversarial Path-Inversion (API) framework. API generates imperceptible perturbations to precisely manipulate the gating decisions, forcing the inference onto altered computational paths. The severe inconsistency between the original and the inverted paths dismantles the representation capability of the model. Extensive experiments on state-of-the-art adaptive trackers demonstrate that API achieves superior perturbation stealthiness, more effective attack, and faster inference speeds. This work opens a new dimension for the security analysis of dynamic tracking networks and provides a theoretical warning for constructing robust adaptive tracking architectures in the future."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03902","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:23:37Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:23:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03894","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03894","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03894","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerated quantum Monte Carlo simulations of the attractive Hubbard model on the kagome lattice"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:26:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:38:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The recent discovery of several families of kagome materials and experimental realization of optical kagome lattices have stimulated growing numerical studies of interaction-driven correlated states on the kagome lattice. Among the available numerical approaches, determinant quantum Monte Carlo (DQMC) is a powerful method for investigating such strongly correlated states. However, the accessible system sizes of existing DQMC simulations remain limited, preventing reliable finite-size scaling analyses. Here we develop a general acceleration scheme based on fast Fourier transform (FFT) for propagator multiplications on composite lattices and combine it with the delay-update algorithm, enabling simulations on system sizes twice as large as those of previous DQMC studies, allowing reliable finite-size scaling analyses of the attractive kagome-lattice Hubbard model. Our large-scale simulations reveal the interaction-driven zero-temperature superfluid quantum criticality at the Dirac filling and provide reliable estimates of the associated critical exponents. Besides, we find no evidence that the previously proposed triangle-rule charge-density-wave order survives in the thermodynamic limit, suggesting that it is likely a finite-size effect. Moreover, for system sizes accessible in current two-dimensional optical lattice experiments, the combined FFT and delay-update scheme exhibits an effective computational cost scaling as $N^{2.49}$, substantially below the $\\mathcal{O}(N^3)$ computational cost of conventional DQMC simulations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03894","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:23:26Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:23:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yvan","familyName":"Richard","name":"Richard, Yvan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CPrefix: A Combinatorial Tensor Framework for Structured Discrete Color Mappings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4.1; I.4.10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T16:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:03:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T20:51:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:46:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete multi-channel mappings are typically represented through sampled values, providing accurate evaluations but limited insight into their underlying structure. We introduce CPrefix, a combinatorial observable representation for discrete mappings, realized within a unified tensor framework that enables representation, reconstruction, and structural analysis.\n The framework is based on a counting tensor induced by multinomial counting observables. Its support forms a discrete Pascal simplex, not as a constraint on the observable space, but as a latent combinatorial representation from which mappings are reconstructed. This formulation separates the combinatorial organization of a mapping from its measured values, exposing the observable structure underlying the mapping.\n The framework is validated on ICC display and printer profiles through latent reconstruction and perceptual gamut transport. Accurate reconstruction demonstrates that color mappings admit faithful observable representations, while reconstruction residuals provide insight into the compatibility of the underlying mapping with the proposed representation.\n Although demonstrated on color transformations, the framework is independent of the physical interpretation of the observables, making it applicable to structured multi-channel mappings arising from color imaging, spectral measurements and other discrete systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures. Accepted for presentation at the IEEE ICIP 2026 Workshop on Computational Color Imaging (CCIW 2026). Withdrawn from the proceedings because the author was unable to attend the conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:43Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathilde","familyName":"Houelle","name":"Houelle, Mathilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emeline","familyName":"Bolmont","name":"Bolmont, Emeline","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe","familyName":"Lovis","name":"Lovis, Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Turbet","name":"Turbet, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Chaverot","name":"Chaverot, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Mechineau","name":"Mechineau, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jérémy","familyName":"Leconte","name":"Leconte, Jérémy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A broad exploration of climate and observability of close-in rocky exoplanets: applications to Ross 128 b"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:59:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T13:06:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:49:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"VLT/RISTRETTO, ELT/ANDES and ELT/PCS will soon enable atmospheric characterization of non-transiting, small rocky exoplanets orbiting closer than the inner edge of the Habitable Zone around M dwarfs, combining high-contrast imaging with high-resolution spectroscopy in reflected light. A key parameter for reflected-light observability is wavelength-dependent reflectivity, shaped by climate, surface and atmospheric properties. This work refines spectral reflectivity predictions for this population. Using Ross 128 b as a prototype, we provide physically consistent geometric albedo estimates within the RISTRETTO and ANDES spectral ranges across diverse atmospheric scenarios. We run 3D global climate model simulations of Ross 128 b for varying atmospheric compositions, surface pressures, water inventories and spin-orbit resonances to explore its possible climate regimes. We then compute synthetic reflectance spectra with Pytmosph3R to assess spectral signatures per scenario and discuss detectability and instrument capacity for constraining climate. Results show hazeless rocky planets receiving irradiation similar to Ross 128 b exhibit low reflectivity, with geometric albedos of 0.07-0.2 in the RISTRETTO bandpass and 0-0.14 in the ANDES bandpass across all scenarios. This low reflectivity can stem from the lack of clouds or surface ice deposits on the dayside, or from strong atmospheric absorption due to high water vapor concentrations, depending on the parameter configurations. These features are characteristic of the climate moist bistability found in close-in, low-water-reservoir planets in previous studies. Our results suggest arbitrary albedo assumptions, such as the common 0.3 Earth-like value, can overestimate reflectivity for this population, highlighting the need for accurate climate models to improve reflectivity predictions and optimize preparation for next-generation spectrographs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:35Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03837","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03837","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03837","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jérémie","familyName":"Marquès","name":"Marquès, Jérémie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compact-Open Dualities for Stably Continuous Posets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary: 06B35. Secondary: 54E55, 06D50, 18F70, 06D22, 06E15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:47:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T18:29:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:43:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We organize and generalize several dualities involving continuous posets. The main theorem reads $\\mathbf{St}_α\\mathbf{Inf}_{α'}\\mathbf{Cont}_{β'}\\mathbf{Sup}_β\\simeq (\\mathbf{St}_β\\mathbf{Inf}_{β'}\\mathbf{Cont}_{α'}\\mathbf{Sup}_α)^{\\mathrm{op}}$, where $\\mathbf{St}$, $\\mathbf{Inf}$, $\\mathbf{Cont}$ and $\\mathbf{Sup}$ refer to stability, completeness, continuity and cocompleteness. The indices are \"ladders\", i.e., classes of sets $λ$ stable under dependent sums and quotients, with associated notions of $λ$-small infima and $λ$-filtered suprema. In the second half of the paper, we discuss algebraicity, proximity lattices and perfect maps."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages; v2: mainly added some references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03837","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03836","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03836","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03836","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resume Means Resume: A Machine-Checked Conformance Contract for Checkpoint, Interrupt, and Resume Semantics in Workflow Persistence Layers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.4; D.2.5; F.3.1; C.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:45:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:56:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-08T12:41:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:33:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A framework that persists execution state so a run can be interrupted, survive a crash, and continue must decide what a resume means for effects that already happened. Five widely deployed agent workflow frameworks answer differently, none exposes a machine-checkable contract, and measured behavior violates even the fragments they state. The RESUME CONTRACT states six properties over the persistence API (prefix continuation, effect exactly-once, fork determinism, checkpoint validity, consume-once, recovery determinism), plus fork-intent and liveness obligations. A TLA+ model checks a reference semantics exhaustively, unchanged at scaled bounds (7.4 million states), and the reference conjunction is additionally TLAPS-proved unbounded (196 obligations); a 39-cell fault matrix and two companion modules yield the separating models independence requires. A deterministic, LLM-free harness measures them at pinned releases. LangGraph 1.2.9 durably records a second resume value and never consults it, persists schema-invalid state silently, and re-executes durably recorded work after a real SIGKILL: exactly-once across interrupts, at-least-once across crashes, on one API. CrewAI 1.15.2 re-executes completed effect-bearing methods against its written claim; pydantic-graph 1.x cannot resume after a mid-node crash; no two probed frameworks share a conformance profile. Consume-once holds sequentially and fails under concurrent delivery: k processes resuming one parked interrupt fire the gated effect k times, saturation 1.0 in 36 of 40 cells, and the failure crosses hosts. REMIT, a reference sequencer whose Verus-verified recovery core is line-identical to the shipped executable, repairs the fork and validity cells. The cross-process cell is repaired at the read path: an opt-in gate claims consumption in the shared store, serving one racer and refusing the rest before any node executes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 10 tables, 1 figure. Supplementary material included as an ancillary file. v3: rejoins a paragraph split across a table float and harmonizes a label glyph in the supplement; no change to results, claims, or numbers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03836","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:07Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanning","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yanning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixiang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Qixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Sihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Keep the Needle, Prune the Haystack: Defect-Preserving Token Pruning for Efficient Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:53:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:56:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:49:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:15:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-09T09:55:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-shot visual anomaly detection has achieved remarkable progress, with recent vision-only approaches further improving performance while simplifying the inference pipeline. However, existing methods typically perform dense computation over all images and spatial tokens, despite the fact that normal samples dominate real-world scenarios and anomalies usually occupy only small regions. Token pruning offers a promising solution, but introduces an asymmetric pruning risk in anomaly detection: retaining normal tokens mainly incurs redundant computation, whereas removing anomalous tokens may eliminate the only evidence for detection and localization. This risk is particularly severe in early layers, where pruning provides the greatest computational benefit but anomaly semantics remain unreliable. We propose KeepAD, a defect-preserving token pruning framework that formulates token selection as high-recall, anomaly-aware routing. In shallow layers, KeepAD combines coverage-preserving selection over local $2\\times2$ patch neighborhoods with deterministic anomaly rescue to reduce the risk of discarding subtle defects. In deeper layers, frozen normal and abnormal prototypes guide pruning under an image-adaptive token budget, aggressively removing low-risk normal tokens while preserving local anomaly evidence. Dense-to-sparse self-distillation further supervises early token routing without introducing additional inference overhead. Experiments on six industrial and seven medical zero-shot anomaly detection benchmarks show that KeepAD reduces the token retention ratio to below $20\\%$, while limiting the average degradation in image-level and pixel-level AUROC to within $2.7$ percentage points. At the most aggressive operating point, KeepAD achieves a $7.9\\times$ speedup over the strongest CLIP-based baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:18:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:18:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xianliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language-Specialized Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation for Multilingual LLM-Based ASR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:02:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:52:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T11:37:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:45:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern LLM-based ASR systems have established multilingual capability as a standard feature, leveraging large-scale multilingual corpora and LLMs' cross-lingual knowledge to achieve competitive performance across multilingual benchmarks. However, jointly modeling languages with heterogeneous acoustic, phonological, and lexical characteristics inevitably introduces optimization conflicts, undermining language-wise specialization. To address this challenge, we propose Language-Specialized Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (LS-MOPD), which decouples language-specific knowledge acquisition from multilingual capability integration: language-specialized teachers are independently optimized via reinforcement learning (RL), with their expertise then integrated into a generalist multilingual student through language routing and token-level multi-teacher distillation, thereby reducing direct cross-lingual optimization conflicts. We further explore static and dynamic acoustic-prefix configurations to examine how teacher-student prefix consistency influences the efficacy of on-policy distillation. Experiments on benchmarks covering Mandarin, Mandarin subdialects, Cantonese, and English demonstrate that LS-MOPD substantially outperforms RL baselines and surpasses the empirical performance envelope defined by the best-performing RL teachers on nearly all benchmarks, revealing its potential to generalize beyond all teachers in multilingual ASR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:16:54Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:16:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaohe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"State Propagation Also Satisfies: A Complex-Valued State-Space Model for Deterministic State Tracking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T10:16:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:43:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T05:50:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:22:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based architectures have dominated sequence modeling, largely due to the expressive power of attention mechanisms. However, for a class of deterministic state tracking tasks---such as parity checking, modular counting, and parenthesis matching---attention may be overkill. In this paper, we show that \\textbf{state propagation alone is sufficient}.\n We propose the \\textbf{Complex State Propagator (CSP)}, a minimalistic recurrent architecture that \\textbf{only propagates hidden states} across layers without output projections at intermediate steps. The state is represented as a complex-valued vector, updated via input-dependent rotations in the complex domain. To enable deep propagation without gradient vanishing or degradation, we introduce a \\textbf{block-level skip connection} alongside element-wise complex normalization and SiLU activation at sequence boundaries. Applied with Focal Loss, CSP achieves \\textbf{100\\% accuracy} with perfect F1 scores across canonical tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:12:34Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:12:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hidekazu","familyName":"Yoshioka","name":"Yoshioka, Hidekazu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stochastic partial differential equation model for environmental DNA dynamics in river environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:15:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:40:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-09T00:20:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:49:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Environmental DNA (eDNA) has emerged as a novel tool for quantifying the seasonal abundance of aquatic species in water bodies; however, its mathematical modeling is still at a germinating stage because of its mechanistic uncertainties. We propose a first-step mathematical and computational framework for the eDNA dynamics of migratory fish based on a novel stochastic partial differential equation model with a delayed source input. The model governs spatiotemporal eDNA concentration in rivers where the source comes from a stochastic differential equation for the migration dynamics of the fish. The affine nature of the model facilitates its theoretical analysis, including the guarantee of well-posedness and the closed-form derivation of the Laplace functional despite the proportionality coefficient of the multiplicative noise term being non-Lipschitz. We also propose a discretization scheme for the model that theoretically generates nonnegative numerical solutions. We finally apply the proposed model to eDNA concentration data sampled from midstream reaches of a river system and perform sensitivity analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proof of Lemma A1 was revised on August 9, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:10Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03359","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03359","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03359","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxi","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Wenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Organic liquid scintillator neutrino detector experiment, theoretical modeling, and computational simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T09:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:39:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-09T16:49:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:11:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Liquid Scintillator Neutrino Detector (LSND) experiment aimed at investigating neutrino oscillations, particularly the transformation of muon-type antineutrinos (\\(\\overlineν_μ\\)) into electron-type antineutrinos (\\(\\overlineν_e\\)). This phenomenon challenges the Standard Model's assumption of massless neutrinos. The LSND employed a large organic liquid scintillator (LS) to detect low-energy neutrino interactions, enhanced by the addition of metal ions such as gadolinium (Gd) for improved signal sensitivity and noise suppression. Theoretical modeling and simulations are used in this paper to accurately interpret experimental results. The FLUKA Monte Carlo code was employed to simulate particle interactions and transport in the detector. Key processes modeled included neutrino interactions (\\(\\overlineν_e + p \\to e^+ + n\\)), neutron capture (\\(n + p \\to d + γ\\)), and the corresponding light output in the scintillator. The simulations accounted for quenching effects using Birks' law, enabling precise predictions of light yield and detector response to secondary particles. Neutrino fluxes from decay-at-rest (DAR) and decay-in-flight (DIF) processes were calculated, capturing the energy spectra of neutrinos generated by pion and muon decays. Pion production cross-sections and light output efficiency for various particles were also modeled to understand detector performance comprehensively. The theoretical modeling and simulation framework validated the experimental observations and provided insights into the detector's sensitivity and limitations. The LSND results hinted at deviations from the Standard Model, stimulating further investigations into neutrino oscillations and the potential existence of sterile neutrinos."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03359","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:11:03Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:11:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02971","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02971","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02971","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhou","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, Zhizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Qinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yecheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mapping the City Through the Lens of Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T00:09:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:15:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T22:07:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:12:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models often complete an underspecified reference to a city with unstated assumptions about urban size, form, infrastructure, environment, and function. We measure those assumptions without naming places. Ten open-weight checkpoints rate anonymized profiles derived from real morphological urban centres across 40 audited indicators and seven domains. The design combines constrained probability-based ratings, prespecified reliability screens, lineage-aware aggregation, multiple population weightings, an independent replication sample, and whole-profile validation. The clearest shared tendency favours urban profiles with larger developed area, faster recent growth, greater mapped infrastructure and non-residential capacity, and less sparse form. Most eligible directions recur in the replication data, and direct ratings of complete profiles show moderate agreement with the indicator-wise construction. Geographic differences shrink after accounting for city scale and development, while reliably measured paired tasks indicate that typicality and desirability are often closely aligned. The framework makes an otherwise vague notion of what models regard as an ordinary city empirically traceable. The resulting evidence delineates a shared yet model-dependent portrait of the city through the lens of language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02971","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:02:09Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:02:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03170","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03170","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03170","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnesh","familyName":"Sujanani","name":"Sujanani, Arnesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saeed","familyName":"Ghadimi","name":"Ghadimi, Saeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Wołkowicz","name":"Wołkowicz, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Nonergodic Primal-Dual Complexity of Efficient Inexact Parameter-Free Augmented Lagrangian Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"65K05, 49M37, 90C06, 65K10, 90C30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T06:01:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:28:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T05:24:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:29:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Augmented Lagrangian (AL) methods are a classical framework for constrained optimization, but for directly verifiable approximate KKT points, known first-order complexity bounds for standard inexact AL methods are suboptimal, while the best known proximal augmented Lagrangian (PAL) bounds retain an additional logarithmic factor. We consider linearly constrained convex composite problems with a smooth convex term and a possibly nonsmooth closed proper convex term with compact domain. We develop three inexact AL schemes that preserve the standard AL subproblem structure and attain the optimal primal-dual complexity $\\mathcal O(ε^{-1})$ in the convex setting, improving prior AL bounds of $\\mathcal O(ε^{-4/3})$, $\\mathcal O(ε^{-7/4})$, and $\\mathcal O(ε^{-2})$, and removing the logarithmic factor from PAL guarantees. Two variants are parameter-free, and all three admit nonergodic guarantees, including a stronger last-iterate guarantee for one variant.\n These results show that proximal regularization, ergodic averaging, and prior knowledge of problem-dependent constants are not intrinsic requirements for attaining optimal verifiable primal-dual complexity within the standard AL framework. A key ingredient is a parameter-free accelerated method that computes verifiable stationarity certificates for the standard, unregularized AL subproblems with optimal complexity. In the strongly convex setting, our methods attain near-optimal complexity $\\mathcal O(ε^{-1/2}\\log(ε^{-1}))$, with two parameter-free variants. Numerical experiments on six problem classes, including elastic-net least-squares regression, group-sparse Huberized support vector machines, and a quantum semidefinite program (SDP), demonstrate substantial computational advantages over a representative PAL method, with speedups frequently ranging from $5$ to $50$ times."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03170","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:06:43Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:06:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liangzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkun","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Mingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jichang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jichang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanbin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guanbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RealWeather: Realistic and Scene-Faithful Weather Translation with Driving World Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T23:33:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T09:30:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:39:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Realistic weather translation is valuable for developing and evaluating autonomous driving systems, yet collecting paired videos of the same scenes under different weather conditions at scale is impractical. Existing methods therefore rely on synthetic data, 3D weather editing, or geometry-conditioned generation, often compromising weather realism or scene fidelity. We propose RealWeather, a driving world model for both realistic and scene-faithful weather translation. Our key idea is to learn authentic weather dynamics directly from real-world videos. Specifically, RealWeather employs Progressive Realism Bootstrapping, an iterative data-refinement strategy. Assisted by an auxiliary Pseudo-Clear Generation pipeline, training initially starts with pseudo-style conditioning videos. As training proceeds, these inputs are progressively replaced with increasingly realistic videos generated by the model itself. This strategy bridges the pseudo-to-real domain gap, allowing the model to adapt seamlessly to real-world input distributions and naturally support bidirectional clear adverse translation. Furthermore, to strictly enforce structural integrity and suppress hallucinations, we introduce Scene-Fidelity RL Optimization, a reward-driven policy optimization strategy that explicitly penalizes alterations to safety-critical driving elements. Extensive experiments demonstrate that RealWeather significantly outperforms existing methods in visual realism and structural preservation, while enabling robust long-tail weather scenario generation and strong zero-shot out-of-distribution generalization. Our video demos can be found at https://hust-umi.github.io/RealWeather/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under submission"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:01:45Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:01:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahiko","familyName":"Yoshimura","name":"Yoshimura, Masahiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuyuki","familyName":"Matsuda","name":"Matsuda, Fuyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiki","familyName":"Ikeda","name":"Ikeda, Yoshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chihiro","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Chihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoko","familyName":"Yoneda","name":"Yoneda, Tomoko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minako","familyName":"Kikukawa","name":"Kikukawa, Minako","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rie","familyName":"Murakami","name":"Murakami, Rie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiharu","familyName":"Nogami","name":"Nogami, Chiharu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukihiko","familyName":"Sugita","name":"Sugita, Yukihiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiko","familyName":"Nakada-Nakura","name":"Nakada-Nakura, Yoshiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahiko","familyName":"Tsujimoto","name":"Tsujimoto, Masahiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daishi","familyName":"Fujita","name":"Fujita, Daishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A concentration-independent paradigm rendering weak interactions inherently quantifiable"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T20:36:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:09:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T08:47:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:27:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A vast class of weak, millimolar-affinity molecular interactions governs cellular function, yet their quantitative characterization has remained largely beyond conventional methods. For over a century, biochemistry has worked within a concentration-based framework where molarity scales with molecular number per volume (N/V), and experiments have usually, often implicitly, changed concentration by moving N while holding V fixed. The weak-interaction measurement bottleneck arises from this paradigm: reading weak binding through bulk concentration requires concentrations beyond practical limits, a framework constraint rather than one of instrumental sensitivity. Here we show that shifting experimental control from N to accessible volume V overcomes this bottleneck and opens previously intractable affinity ranges through nanoscale spatial confinement. Controlling V means controlling what biochemists have called \"local concentration\" and \"proximity effects,\" recasting these long-ambiguous notions as quantitative variables grounded in first principles. Implemented in DNA nanocavities, the approach showed that geometric arrangement alone can override solution-phase binding hierarchies. The same spatial control quantified a protein-peptide interaction of order 10 mM from femtomoles per well, totalling under a picomole per titration. Even so, a standard plate reader gave a signal-to-noise ratio near 10^3, leaving headroom for still weaker interactions. The affinity-and-geometry readout also enabled rational screening for protein-protein-interaction modulators, identifying compounds that enhance weak associations by reweighting local encounters rather than binding tightly on their own or forming a stable ternary complex. Together, this volume-based paradigm and its implementation provide a general strategy for probing and modulating previously inaccessible biochemical phenomena."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"96 pages, 6 figures, 20 supplementary figures. Companion paper: arXiv:2608.02864. v2 resolves the companion-paper cross-reference, adds SPR methods and Supplementary Fig. S19 supporting the previously reported result for compound 1, and updates funding and author-contribution statements. The main conclusions are unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:59:39Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:59:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuyuki","familyName":"Matsuda","name":"Matsuda, Fuyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahiko","familyName":"Yoshimura","name":"Yoshimura, Masahiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiro","familyName":"Ikeda","name":"Ikeda, Shiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daishi","familyName":"Fujita","name":"Fujita, Daishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Experimental access to molarity's blind spot in macroscopic assays"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T20:36:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:09:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-08T08:32:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:26:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chemical kinetics has long inferred local molecular behaviour through the flask-and-molarity pairing, where well-mixed concentrations serve as the experimental readout. Yet many biological reactions occur in structured environments. Researchers have long recognized that concentration may not carry the same operational meaning in such environments, but even local concepts such as effective molarity usually translate local effects back into a single value with units of concentration. What has been missing is the complementary path: a bench-compatible way to make local structure an experimental variable, rather than only a correction to molarity. Here we show a chemistry-geometry crossover that the flask-and-molarity interface could not make visible. In the micromolar-or-weaker affinity regime, inhibition can switch sharply out of the familiar concentration-and-affinity mode: chemical binding strength no longer determines the response, and the shape of the target's local space does. A bench-compatible interface made this switch measurable by separating bulk dose from local geometry. This blind spot arose from the hidden premise that macroscopic pooling makes a structured local state readable as a single local concentration. The chemistry-geometry crossover breaks that premise: in a structured target environment, a macroscopic assay can remain sensitive to the probability distribution of local states, so collapsing that distribution to one concentration-valued number removes the geometric control axis from the readout. By preserving that axis in the experiment, the interface bypasses molarity's hidden bottleneck and provides a routine experimental route to remeasure and reinterpret molecular interactions in structured space."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"58 pages, 6 figures. Companion paper: arXiv:2608.02865. v2 resolves the companion-paper cross-reference, which lacked an arXiv identifier in v1, updates funding information, and adds an LLM-use statement. The scientific content is unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:59:37Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:59:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Galicer","name":"Galicer, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariano","familyName":"Merzbacher","name":"Merzbacher, Mariano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damián","familyName":"Pinasco","name":"Pinasco, Damián","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Improved Volume Ratio Bound via Isotropic Positions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Metric Geometry (math.MG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"52A23, 52A38, 52A40 (Primary), 46B06 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T19:37:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:06:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T19:00:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:07:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that, for every pair of convex bodies $K,L\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $$ \\operatorname{vr}(K,L)\\leq C\\sqrt{n\\log(n+1)}. $$ The main point is to place $K$ and $L^\\circ$ in isotropic position. We then consider a random orthogonal image of $L$ and control the corresponding operator norm by combining the isotropic mean-gauge estimate of Bizeul and Klartag with Letwin's recent dimension-free bound for the third-moment parameter appearing in their estimate. Our result improves the bound $ \\operatorname{vr}(K,L)\\leq C\\sqrt n \\log(n+1)$ proved by Giannopoulos and Hartzoulaki, which had remained the best general estimate for nearly two and a half decades."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages. References added, together with alternative proofs along the same lines using recent results"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:58:43Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:58:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02782","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02782","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02782","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean D.","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Sean D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto dos","familyName":"Reis","name":"Reis, Roberto dos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuScope: An Open-Source Python Framework for Quantum-Circuit Simulation of Transmission Electron Microscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:25:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T03:44:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:27:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Image formation in transmission electron microscopy (TEM) is governed by the coherent evolution of the electron wavefunction through the specimen and the objective lens. This physics maps naturally onto the gate model of quantum computation. We present QuScope, an open-source Python framework that expresses the complete TEM image-formation pipeline as quantum circuits. The $N\\times N$ electron wavefunction is amplitude-encoded in $2\\log_2 N$ qubits, and every optical element, including phase-grating transmission, Fresnel propagation between specimen slices, and the aberrated objective lens, is implemented as a diagonal unitary conjugated by quantum Fourier transforms. On this foundation, QuScope v0.2.0 implements validated imaging pipelines, covering conventional TEM under the phase-object approximation, full multislice CTEM and STEM for thick specimens. All quantum results reported here come from exact, noise-free statevector simulation of the circuits on classical hardware, and every result is validated against a classical twin implementation. Quantum and classical multislice exit waves agree to unit fidelity, and all physical constants are verified against standard references. We provide transpiled quantum-resource estimates for each algorithm, an analysis of the diagonal-synthesis bottleneck that governs near-term hardware execution, and a fully tested, documented, and pip-installable package. QuScope v0.2.0 is available at https://github.com/QuScope/QuScope."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2 - fixes CTF plot. Open-source Python framework for quantum-circuit formulations of CTEM and STEM simulation. Code, documentation, and reproducible examples are available at https://github.com/QuScope/QuScope"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02782","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:44Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02762","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02762","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02762","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Markoff","name":"Markoff, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Praschl","name":"Praschl, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton Hjalte","familyName":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Anton Hjalte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian Emil","familyName":"Mogensen","name":"Mogensen, Christian Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias Bech","familyName":"Skadhauge","name":"Skadhauge, Mathias Bech","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Beery","name":"Beery, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Ørsted","name":"Ørsted, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David C.","familyName":"Schedl","name":"Schedl, David C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Oh Deer, How Should I Handle This? Seasonal Priors for Selective Wildlife Annotation and Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4.8; I.5.4; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:05:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:03:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T10:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:37:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-09T16:11:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:10:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-grained wildlife classification in aerial imagery is limited not only by model performance, but also by unreliable labels: animals occupy few pixels, key visual cues vary seasonally, and modality-specific evidence can be ambiguous. We study adult-male identification in red deer ($\\textit{Cervus elaphus}$), where the antler cycle defines predictable windows of reliable evidence for both annotation and prediction. Using 7,295 RGB-only, thermal-only, and matched RGB+thermal crop sets from low-altitude UAV surveys, labeled by three annotators, we show that seasonal structure links (I) annotation quality, (II) downstream classification, and (III) selective prediction. Matched RGB+thermal review resolves more samples than either single modality, recovering majority-male labels otherwise missed by RGB or thermal alone, in human-based as well as model-based classification. Months with high annotator abstention also show lower classifier confidence, and soft seasonal priors mainly benefit the season-limited thermal view. Uncertainty-band abstention further raises covered accuracy to 98.9%, though at reduced coverage and with deferral that falls disproportionately on males. Overall, a biologically grounded seasonal calendar predicts where annotation and prediction are unreliable, and can guide both annotation protocol design and modality weighting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the ECCV 2026 Workshop on Computer Vision for Ecology (CV4Ecology), archival proceedings track. 17 pages, 4 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02762","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:16Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02764","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.02764","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.02764","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaodi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stateful Governance for Concurrent Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-03T18:07:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:03:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T17:27:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-11T02:03:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are moving from advisory interfaces into systems that execute consequential operations: issuing refunds, reserving scarce inventory, provisioning cloud resources, and initiating financial transfers. These workflows require governance over effects, not only over model outputs. Existing safeguards often decide whether an action is allowed from the information available when the action is requested. For stateful policies, that request-time view may be incomplete: budgets, inventory, approval status, and risk signals can change before the effect occurs, making an earlier authorization or approval stale.\n This paper studies stateful governance for concurrent agentic systems. We identify stale authorization as the core failure mode and define policy-state serializability, a correctness condition requiring committed effects to be explainable as authorized against the policy state immediately before they occur. We present MasuGate, a runtime architecture that keeps policies as reviewable programs while coordinating the state and effects needed to preserve their decisions. In experiments with a PostgreSQL-backed prototype of MasuGate, the system prevents stale authorizations missed by baselines that pass policy state as ordinary request context, preserves delayed approvals while unrelated work proceeds, keeps policy evolution mostly in policy text rather than trusted provider code, and avoids policy violations in a scripted, LLM-free procurement workflow where agent-governance baselines produce stale authorizations over shared budgets and inventory. More broadly, MasuGate suggests a path for integrating stateful governance boundaries into agent frameworks and provider-backed domains where agents act on shared resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures. Revision notes: system terminology updated; technical content and results unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02764","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T01:57:19Z","registered":"2026-08-05T01:57:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:27:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.04009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.04009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.04009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghan","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Zhonghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Zhangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SocietyBench: Forecasting Counterfactual Social-World Evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T17:59:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:10:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T17:15:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:56:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs), and the agents built on top of them, are now benchmarked heavily on whether they can finish a task -- fix a bug, drive a browser, operate a GUI. A complementary social ability, namely how well a model understands and forecasts the way real social events unfold, has barely been measured. We introduce SocietyBench, an end-to-end benchmark that takes a one-line event topic, collects Web news and social-media posts across five platforms, distills them into a date-indexed timeline that keeps factual events and a public-opinion layer separate, and then turns every cutoff date on that timeline into an audited bank of forecasting questions. Questions are scored on two orthogonal 100-point axes: probability calibration and temporal accuracy. Before any model sees a timeline, a three-phase procedure replaces every named entity and shifts every date by a per-event constant, turning a real arc into a counterfactual social world -- structurally identical to what happened, but stripped of the surface labels a model could match against pre-training memory. On five heterogeneous events and 125 prediction points in Chinese and English editions, the strongest of six frontier LLMs reaches only 75.0 out of 100, against a trivial anchor of 50. The two axes come apart: a model can be calibration-strong but time-weak, or the reverse. Three agent frameworks built on a shared base model fail to improve on that base, and two model-free heuristics trail every LLM. Per-event gaps reach 21.4 points on a single axis, which is our main argument for evaluating on several events rather than one. All anonymized timelines, question banks, ground truth, and scoring code are released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://co-minder.github.io/Societybench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:26:04Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:26:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03725","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03725","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03725","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danilo","familyName":"Artigas","name":"Artigas, Danilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Pi","name":"Pi, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuko","familyName":"Urakawa","name":"Urakawa, Yuko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Dilatation Invariance Suppresses Loop Corrections to Curvature Perturbations on CMB Scales"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:22:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:21:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Departures from standard slow-roll (SR) inflation have attracted increasing interest in recent years. In particular, scenarios that strongly enhance the power spectrum of the curvature perturbation are often proposed as a mechanism for producing primordial black holes, which could account for part or the totality of dark matter. An ongoing debate in these models is whether non-linear interactions of cosmological perturbations on small scales, namely loop corrections, can become sufficiently large to backreact on CMB scales. In this work, we adopt a non-linear framework, dropping spatial-gradient terms, to study loop corrections at super-Hubble scales. Our focus is to clarify the role of spatial-diffeomorphism invariance, especially dilatation invariance, which is the symmetry under overall rescaling of spatial coordinates, in demonstrating the suppression of loop corrections to the CMB power spectrum. This analysis is valid for any inflaton potential, both with smooth and sharp transitions, and at any loop order, as long as the CMB scales cross the horizon during an SR phase. We compare this result with the different explanations proposed in the literature using various gauge choices, and show our analysis is consistent both in the comoving and in the spatially-flat gauges. In particular, we show that in the spatially-flat gauge, the cubic interaction contains a term that diverges in the sharp transition limit. However, this contribution is exactly cancelled by the quartic interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages main text, 4 pages appendix, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03725","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:31Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrián","familyName":"Gómez-Brandón","name":"Gómez-Brandón, Adrián","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Hogan","name":"Hogan, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gonzalo","familyName":"Navarro","name":"Navarro, Gonzalo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uplifting the Superpowers of Worst-Case-Optimal Join Algorithms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T15:48:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:02:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T05:52:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:25:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Worst-case-optimal (wco) join algorithms have demonstrated their power -- in both theory and practice -- to efficiently solve complex Basic Graph Patterns (BGPs). Modern graph query languages, such as SPARQL and GQL, have BGPs at their core, but also have a wide range of other features, including filters (aka.\\ selections). Such conditions are typically handled via pre- or post-filtering, before or after processing the BGPs. In this paper we show how to uplift wco join algorithms so as to incorporate such filtering natively, improving efficiency. We demonstrate the superiority of this approach by extending the \\textit{Ring} -- a compact index that provides wco resolution of BGPs within almost no extra space on top of the graph -- so as to handle property graphs using our new techniques while retaining compactness. We implement this extension and experimentally show that it outperforms various baseline systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:22:12Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:22:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasia","familyName":"Pipi","name":"Pipi, Anastasia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Duruisseaux","name":"Duruisseaux, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Been","name":"Been, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuecheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Xuecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor L.","familyName":"Patti","name":"Patti, Taylor L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anima","familyName":"Anandkumar","name":"Anandkumar, Anima","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prineha","familyName":"Narang","name":"Narang, Prineha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inverse Design of Quantum Control Sequences with Fourier Neural Operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T14:06:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:57:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T18:14:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:02:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum optimal control is a key tool for steering quantum dynamics, but its computational cost grows rapidly with the Hilbert space dimension. Here, we introduce a Fourier Neural Operator (FNO)-based framework for learning high dimensional molecular quantum dynamics and accelerating the inverse design of control protocols. Given an initial molecular population distribution, laser frequency, and polarization, the FNO predicts molecular-motional population dynamics up to $10^7$ times faster than GPU-accelerated numerical propagation with CUDA-Q Dynamics. Using this fast and differentiable surrogate, we develop the FNO stochastic pulse-measurement planner (FNO-SPMP), which constructs pulse sequences to purify an initially mixed Boltzmann distribution. We demonstrate the protocol in an 888-dimensional subspace of the hydronium molecule at 20 K, achieving a target-state population of 0.98 with a sequence success rate of up to 86.2%. In a shared discrete control space, FNO-SPMP achieves nearly twice the success rate of a reinforcement-learning baseline while using roughly half as many quantum control pulses and reducing pulse-sequence generation time from approximately 10 hours to 10-20 minutes. These results show that operator-learning surrogates can enable inverse design in quantum systems whose Hilbert spaces are too large for conventional direct optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 Pages, 6 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:19:00Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:19:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdallah","familyName":"Khemais","name":"Khemais, Abdallah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Layer Interaction under Weight-Space Ablation: A Closed-Form Attention Jacobian Bound and a Test on a Real Pretrained Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:14:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:53:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T15:45:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:52:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A companion paper studies when activation patching and weight-space ablation agree, inside an idealized model where a conditional computation is carried additively through a residual stream. For the one composition in that model where two carriers are architecturally dependent, an attention head and its own layer's normalization-MLP composition, it derives an exact first-order interaction formula, zero when only the MLP is ablated and second-order bounded when the head is also ablated. That result is confined to a single residual block and checked only on small transformers on a synthetic task.\n This paper extends the result past both limits. First, the interaction from ablating carriers spanning several layers decomposes exactly into same-block terms, one per touched layer, plus a cross-layer remainder on which the decomposition makes no claim of smallness. Second, we isolate that remainder exactly, for two layers, as a double integral of a mixed second derivative, and name the missing ingredient needed to bound it: a Jacobian bound for the attention sub-block. We derive this bound in closed form and verify it, without a single violation, against Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct's real weights, though we do not yet chain it across layers. We also give, in closed form, the curvature constant the companion paper's bound leaves unexhibited.\n Third, on that same model, we search for and find an emergent circuit for indirect object identification, never designed into it, using the original activation-patching method for this task, and test collapse, dissociation, and interaction on it. The result is mixed: a shared carrier emerges across all five tested instances, collapse and dissociation hold on most but not all, and a nonzero interaction is measurable on three of five, at layer pairs outside the same-block case the companion theorem covers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 2 figures. Part II of a two-part series; see the companion paper \"A Theory of Conditional Collapse under Low-Rank Weight-Space Ablations\" (Part I)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:17:20Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:17:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03620","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03620","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03620","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdallah","familyName":"Khemais","name":"Khemais, Abdallah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Theory of Conditional Collapse under Low-Rank Weight-Space Ablations: I. The Single-Block Theory and Synthetic Validation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T13:10:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:53:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T15:43:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:52:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Activation patching and weight-space ablation both claim a component is causally responsible for a behavior, yet they act on different objects: one forward pass versus the parameters behind every forward pass. We ask when they agree.\n We study an idealized model where a conditional computation is carried additively through a residual stream, $F(x)=F_0(x)+\\sum_iα_i(x)v_i$, read out by a linear functional, and prove three exact results. First, deleting a subset of carriers collapses a matched input pair onto the same unconditional output \\emph{if and only if} the removal is symmetric on the pair and leaves no outside contrast; the error is deterministic, and we give its exact form even when the two conditions hold only approximately. Second, patching a carrier moves the readout by its donor-receiver \\emph{contrast}, while ablating it moves the readout by its \\emph{absolute level}; neither bounds the other, and we construct pairs where every single-carrier patch flips the decision while no single-carrier ablation does. Third, for an attention head composed with its own layer's normalization and MLP, we derive an exact first-order interaction formula with a provably second-order remainder, vanishing identically when only the MLP is ablated but not, in general, when a head is.\n Small transformers trained on a synthetic conditional task illustrate all three predictions: across thirty-nine ablation configurations the measured interaction is strongly rank-correlated with the idealized model's predictive accuracy (Spearman $-0.83$), and a second task and architecture reproduces the same pattern, including a further polarity reversal.\n The single-block interaction result extends past one residual block, and the synthetic validation is tested against a real pretrained model, in a companion paper that takes this theory further along both axes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 2 figures. Part I of a two-part series; see the companion paper \"Cross-Layer Interaction under Weight-Space Ablation\" (Part II)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03620","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:17:08Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:17:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03513","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03513","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03513","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Ukleev","name":"Ukleev, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleg I.","familyName":"Utesov","name":"Utesov, Oleg I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Ubilla","name":"Ubilla, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radu-Marius","familyName":"Abrudan","name":"Abrudan, Radu-Marius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Wild","name":"Wild, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Holger","familyName":"Kropf","name":"Kropf, Holger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moritz","familyName":"Winter","name":"Winter, Moritz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Tahn","name":"Tahn, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernd","familyName":"Rellinghaus","name":"Rellinghaus, Bernd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim A.","familyName":"Butcher","name":"Butcher, Tim A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Finizio","name":"Finizio, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Wintz","name":"Wintz, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Weigand","name":"Weigand, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max T.","familyName":"Birch","name":"Birch, Max T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshinori","familyName":"Tokura","name":"Tokura, Yoshinori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasujiro","familyName":"Taguchi","name":"Taguchi, Yasujiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kosuke","familyName":"Karube","name":"Karube, Kosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florin","familyName":"Radu","name":"Radu, Florin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vector-field control and emergent basal-plane anisotropy of magnetic textures in noncentrosymmetric (Fe$_{0.63}$Ni$_{0.3}$Pd$_{0.07}$)$_3$P"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-04T11:57:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T00:48:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T11:07:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:39:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"(Fe$_{0.63}$Ni$_{0.3}$Pd$_{0.07}$)$_3$P is a room-temperature magnet with $S_4$ symmetry that hosts a rich variety of topological spin textures. Here, we report a combined resonant small-angle x-ray scattering and ptychography study of (Fe$_{0.63}$Ni$_{0.3}$Pd$_{0.07}$)$_3$P in vector magnetic fields over a broad temperature range. We demonstrate deterministic vector-field control of magnetic stripe domains, where in-plane fields continuously rotate their orientation via a transition from a chiral stripe to an achiral fan configuration. Furthermore, at 50 K and below, the stripe orientation becomes metastably pinned and retains its field-trained direction. While the magnitude of the wavevector is nearly isotropic within the basal plane at room temperature, a pronounced temperature evolution of anisotropic interactions emerges upon cooling. In particular, non-trivial anisotropy axes develop at 20-50 K reflecting the combined effects of magnetocrystalline anisotropy, anisotropic exchange, and Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya interaction (DMI), whose effective orientation is found to rotate with temperature. These results establish (Fe$_{0.63}$Ni$_{0.3}$Pd$_{0.07}$)$_3$P as a model system for vector-field control of chiral spin textures and reveal a previously unrecognized temperature-driven evolution of the effective DMI landscape in a noncentrosymmetric magnet."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03513","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-08-05T02:14:42Z","registered":"2026-08-05T02:14:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:12:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.03417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.03417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. P.","familyName":"Solodov","name":"Solodov, E. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. R.","familyName":"Akhmetshin","name":"Akhmetshin, R. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. N.","familyName":"Amirkhanov","name":"Amirkhanov, A. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. V.","familyName":"Anisenkov","name":"Anisenkov, A. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. M.","familyName":"Aulchenko","name":"Aulchenko, V. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"N. S.","familyName":"Bashtovoy","name":"Bashtovoy, N. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. V.","familyName":"Bedarev","name":"Bedarev, E. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. E.","familyName":"Berkaev","name":"Berkaev, D. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. E.","familyName":"Bondar","name":"Bondar, A. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. V.","familyName":"Bragin","name":"Bragin, A. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. E.","familyName":"Chistya